ilarly, multiplying by the tens digit (3) shifts two positions left? Wait, no. Wait, when you multiply by the tens digit, which is in the tens place, you shift one position left. So 978 x 3 = 2934, then shift one position left to make it 29340. Similarly, multiplying by the hundreds digit (4) is in the hundreds place, so shift two positions left: 978 x 4 = 3912, shift two positions left to make it 391200. Then, adding 4890 (units) + 29340 (tens shifted) + 391200 (hundreds shifted). So 4890 + 29340 + 391200 = 425,430. That's correct.\n\nBut when I did the standard multiplication with shifting, I added 48900 (shifted by one) + 293400 (shifted by two) + 3912000 (shifted by three). That's incorrect. Because when you multiply by the hundreds digit, you shift two positions, not three. Wait, no. Wait, the hundreds digit is in the hundreds place, so when you multiply by it, you shift two positions to the left. So 978 x 4 = 3912, then shift two positions left: 3912 becomes 391200. Similarly, 978 x 3 = 2934, shift one position left: 29340. 978 x 5 = 4890. So adding them as 4890 + 29340 + 391200 = 425,430. So that's correct.\n\nBut when I did the standard multiplication with shifting, I added 48900 + 293400 + 3912000. That's wrong because shifting three positions is not the case here. The hundreds digit is shifted two positions, not three. Wait, no. Wait, when you multiply by the hundreds digit, which is in the hundreds place, you shift two positions. So 978 x 4 = 3912, then shift two positions left: 3912 becomes 391200. So that's correct. So the three shifts are: units digit (5) shifted 1, tens digit (3) shifted 2, hundreds digit (4) shifted 3? Wait, no. Wait, when you multiply by the hundreds digit, which is in the hundreds place, you shift two positions. Wait, no. Wait, the place value is hundreds, so when you multiply by the hundreds digit, you shift two positions. Wait, no. Wait, the standard multiplication algorithm is:\n\nWhen you multiply by a digit in a certain place, you multiply the number by that digit and then shift the result to the left by the number of places equal to the digit's place value. So, for example, multiplying by the units digit (1's place) shifts left by 1, tens digit (10's place) shifts left by 2, hundreds digit (100's place) shifts left by 3, etc.\n\nTherefore, multiplying 978 by 4 (hundreds place) shifts left by 3 positions: 3912 becomes 391200.\n\nMultiplying 978 by 3 (tens place) shifts left by 2 positions: 2934 becomes 29340.\n\nMultiplying 978 by 5 (units place) shifts left by 1 position: 4890 becomes 4890.\n\nTherefore, adding 4890 + 29340 + 391200 = 425,430. So that's correct.\n\nBut when I did the standard multiplication with shifting, I added 48900 + 293400 + 3912000. That's incorrect because shifting three positions would be for the hundreds digit, but 978 x 4 is 3912, which is shifted three positions to make 3912000. But 3912000 is 3,912,000, which is different from 391,200. So that's a mistake. Therefore, the correct addition is 4890 + 29340 + 391200 = 425,430.\n\nTherefore, the correct product is 425,430.\n\nBut let me verify this with another method. Let's use the distributive property again.\n\n978 x 435 = 978 x (400 + 30 + 5) = 978 x 400 + 978 x 30 + 978 x 5.\n\nCompute each term:\n\n978 x 400 = 978 x 4 x 100 = 3912 x 100 = 391,200.\n\n978 x 30 = 978 x 3 x 10 = 2934 x 10 = 29,340.\n\n978 x 5 = 4,890.\n\nAdding them together: 391,200 + 29,340 = 420,540; 420,540 + 4,890 = 425,430. Correct.\n\nAlternatively, use the calculator-like step-by-step multiplication:\n\nFirst, multiply 978 by 435.\n\nLet me write it out:\n\n      978\n    x 435\n    -------\n\nStart with 978 x 5:\n\n5 x 8 = 40, write 0, carry 4.\n\n5 x 7 = 35 + 4 = 39, write 9, carry 3.\n\n5 x 9 = 45 + 3 = 48, write 8, carry 0.\n\nSo 4890.\n\nShift left by 1: 48900.\n\nNext, 978 x 3:\n\n3 x 8 = 24, write 4, carry 2.\n\n3 x 7 = 21 + 2 = 23, write 3, carry 2.\n\n3 x 9 = 27 + 2 = 29, write 9, carry 0.\n\nSo 2934.\n\nShift left by 2: 29340