70 times 9 is 3330, so 3330 times 100 is 333,000. Then, 370 times 17. Let's compute that. 370 times 10 is 3700, and 370 times 7 is 2590. Adding those together: 3700 + 2590 = 6290. Then, add 333,000 + 6,290. That would be 339,290. Wait, that's different from the previous result. Hmm, so now I have two different answers. That means I must have made a mistake somewhere.\n\nWait, hold on. Let me check the second method again. So, 370 * 900: 370 * 9 = 3330, so 3330 * 100 = 333,000. That's correct. Then 370 * 17: 370 * 10 = 3700, 370 * 7 = 2590. 3700 + 2590 = 6290. Then 333,000 + 6,290 = 339,290. But earlier, using the standard multiplication, I got 39,590. These numbers are way off. That can't be right. There must be a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the standard multiplication steps. Let me go back to that. So, 370 * 917. Let's write it out vertically:\n\n   370\nx 917\n-------\n   2590  (370 * 7)\n  3700   (370 * 1, shifted one position left)\n 33300   (370 * 9, shifted two positions left)\n-------\n 39590\n\nWait, but when I add 2590 + 3700 + 33300, that's 2590 + 3700 = 6290, then 6290 + 33300 = 39590. But in the second method, I got 339,290. That's a discrepancy. So, clearly, one of the methods is wrong. Let me check the second method again.\n\nWait, 370 * 900: 370 * 9 = 3330, so 3330 * 100 = 333,000. That's correct. Then 370 * 17: 370 * 17. Let's compute 370 * 17. 370 * 10 = 3700, 370 * 7 = 2590. 3700 + 2590 = 6290. Then 333,000 + 6,290 = 339,290. But that's not matching the standard multiplication. So, which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the second method. Let me check 370 * 917 again. Let's do it another way. Let's compute 370 * 917.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the fact that 917 is 900 + 17, but perhaps I need to consider the place values properly. Wait, 917 is 9 hundreds, 1 ten, and 7 ones. So, 900 + 10 + 7. So, 370 * 900 + 370 * 10 + 370 * 7. Which is exactly what I did before. So, 370*900=333,000; 370*10=3,700; 370*7=2,590. Then, 333,000 + 3,700 = 336,700; 336,700 + 2,590 = 339,290. So, that's correct. But why does the standard multiplication give a different answer?\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, in the standard multiplication, when you write 370 * 917, the first step is multiplying by the ones digit (7), then the tens digit (1), then the hundreds digit (9). But in the standard method, when you multiply by the tens digit, you shift left by one, and by the hundreds digit, shift left by two. So, the positions are correct. Let me check the standard multiplication again.\n\nSo, 370\nx 917\n--------\n  2590 (370 * 7)\n 3700 (370 * 1, shifted left by one)\n 33300 (370 * 9, shifted left by two)\n--------\n 39590\n\nBut when I add 2590 + 3700 + 33300, that's 2590 + 3700 = 6290, then 6290 + 33300 = 39590. But according to the other method, it's 339,290. So, there's a conflict here. Therefore, one of the methods is wrong. Let me check with another approach.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can compute 370 * 917 using another method. Let's try using the distributive property in a different way. Let's break down 917 into 900 + 17, but maybe I need to consider the multiplication step by step.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the fact that 370 * 917 = 370 * (900 + 17) = 370*900 + 370*17. Which is exactly what I did before. So, 370*900 is 333,000 and 370*17 is 6,290. So, 333,000 + 6,290 = 339,290. But according to the standard multiplication, it's 39,590. So, which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the standard multiplication. Let me check that again. Let's write it out properly.\n\nMultiplying 370 by 917:\n\nFirst, write 370\nMultiply by 7:\n370\nx  7\n------\n2590\n\nThen, multiply by 1 (tens place), shifted one position left:\n370\nx  1\n------\n3700\n\nThen, multiply by 9 (hundreds place), shifted two positions left:\n370\nx  9\n------\n3330\n\nNow, add them up:\n\n2590\n+3700\n------\n6290\n\n6290\n+33300\n------\n39590\n\nWait, that's correct. So, according to the standard 