uct: 2090\n\nBut wait, actually, when you do multiplication step by step, each partial product is shifted one position to the left as you go to the next digit. So when you multiply by the units digit, it's the first partial product, then multiplying by the tens digit gives the second partial product shifted one place to the left, and multiplying by the hundreds digit gives the third partial product shifted two places to the left.\n\nBut when adding them up, you have to align them properly. Let me write them down:\n\nFirst partial product: 2956\n\nSecond partial product: 17120 (since multiplying by 4 shifts it one place left)\n\nThird partial product: 209000 (since multiplying by 5 shifts it two places left)\n\nWait, but actually, when you multiply 418 by 5, you get 2090, which is three digits. Then when you shift it left by two places, it becomes 209000. But that might not be necessary. Wait, maybe I need to think differently.\n\nWait, perhaps the confusion is arising from how the partial products are combined. Let me recall that in the standard multiplication algorithm, each partial product is obtained by multiplying the multiplicand by each digit of the multiplier, starting from the right, and then adding them up, with the partial products shifted appropriately.\n\nSo, for 418 * 547:\n\nFirst, multiply 418 by 7 (units digit of 547), which is 2956.\n\nThen, multiply 418 by 4 (tens digit of 547), which is 1712, and shift it one place to the left (i.e., add a zero at the end), so it becomes 17120.\n\nThen, multiply 418 by 5 (hundreds digit of 547), which is 2090, and shift it two places to the left (add two zeros), so it becomes 209000.\n\nNow, add these three partial products together:\n\n2956\n\n+17120\n\n+209000\n\nLet me compute that step by step.\n\nFirst, add 2956 and 17120:\n\n2956 + 17120. Let's do 2956 + 17120.\n\n2956 + 17120 = 17120 + 2956. Let's add 17120 + 2000 = 19120, then add 956: 19120 + 956 = 20076.\n\nThen add 209000 to 20076:\n\n20076 + 209000. Le