output:  ['\n\nTo find the product of 952 and 365, we can use the distributive property and break down the multiplication into smaller parts:\n\n1. Break down 365 into 300, 60, and 5:\n   - \\( 952 \\times 300 \\)\n   - \\( 952 \\times 60 \\)\n   - \\( 952 \\times 5 \\)\n\n2. Calculate each part separately:\n   - \\( 952 \\times 300 = 952 \\times 3 \\times 100 = 2856 \\times 100 = 285600 \\)\n   - \\( 952 \\times 60 = 952 \\times 6 \\times 10 = 5712 \\times 10 = 57120 \\)\n   - \\( 952 \\times 5 = 4760 \\)\n\n3. Add the results together:\n   - \\( 285600 + 57120 = 342720 \\)\n   - \\( 342720 + 4760 = 347480 \\)\n\nThus, the product of 952 and 365 is \\(\\boxed{347480}\\).', "\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 952 and 365. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two three-digit numbers can be a bit tricky, but maybe there's a way to break it down step by step. Let me start by recalling the standard multiplication algorithm. \n\nFirst, I'll write 952 multiplied by 365. Let me set it up like this:\n\n\u202f\xa0\xa0952\n×\xa0\xa0365\n--------\n\nStarting with the rightmost digit of the second number, which is 5. I need to multiply 952 by 5 first. Let's do that:\n\n952 × 5. Breaking it down:\n900 × 5 = 4500\n50 × 5 = 250\n2 × 5 = 10\nAdding those together: 4500 + 250 = 4750; 4750 + 10 = 4760. So, 952 × 5 = 4760. I'll write that down as the first partial product, shifted one position to the left because it's the units place.\n\nNext, I move to the next digit in 365, which is 6. Now, multiply 952 by 6:\n\n952 × 6. Let's break it down again:\n900 × 6 = 5400\n50 × 6 = 300\n2 × 6 = 12\nAdding those: 5400 + 300 = 5700; 5700 + 12 = 5712. So, 952 × 6 = 5712. This will be the second partial product, shifted another position to the left (tens place).\n\nNow, the last digit is 3. Multiply 952 by 3:\n\n952 × 3. Breaking it down:\n900 × 3 = 2700\n50 × 3 = 150\n2 × 3 = 6\nAdding those: 2700 + 150 = 2850; 2850 + 6 = 2856. So, 952 × 3 = 2856. This is the third partial product,