      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 955 and 366. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two three-digit numbers can be a bit tricky, but maybe there's a way to break it down step by step. Let me start by recalling the standard multiplication algorithm. \n\nFirst, I'll write 955 multiplied by 366. Let me set it up like this:\n\n\u202f\u00a0\u00a0955\n\u00d7\u00a0\u00a0366\n--------\n\nStarting with the rightmost digit of the second number, which is 6. I need to multiply 955 by 6. Let me do that first. \n\n955 \u00d7 6. Let's break it down:\n- 900 \u00d7 6 = 5400\n- 50 \u00d7 6 = 300\n- 5 \u00d7 6 = 30\nAdding those together: 5400 + 300 = 5700; 5700 + 30 = 5730. So, 955 \u00d7 6 = 5730. I'll write that down as the first partial product, shifted one position to the left because it's the units place.\n\nNext, I move to the next digit in 366, which is 6. But wait, since it's the tens place, I need to multiply 955 by 60 (because 6 in the tens place is 6 \u00d7 10). \n\nSo, 955 \u00d7 60. Let's calculate that:\n- 900 \u00d7 60 = 54,000\n- 50 \u00d7 60 = 3,000\n- 5 \u00d7 60 = 300\nAdding those: 54,000 + 3,000 = 57,000; 57,000 + 300 = 57,300. So, 955 \u00d7 60 = 57,300. But since this is the tens place, I need to shift it two positions to the left, which would make it 57,300 \u00d7 10 = 573,000. Wait, no, actually, when you multiply by the tens digit, you multiply by 10 and shift left by one. So, 57,300 shifted left by one is 573,000. Let me confirm that. Yes, because 60 is 6 \u00d7 10, so multiplying by 6 and then by 10, which shifts the result left by one. So, 57,300 becomes 573,000.\n\nNow, moving to the next digit in 366, which is 3. This is the hundreds place, so I need to multiply 955 by 300 (since 3 in the hundreds place is 3 \u00d7 100). \n\nCalculating 955 \u00d7 300:\n- 900 \u00d7 300 = 270,000\n- 50 \u00d7 300 = 15,000\n- 5 \u00d7 300 = 1,500\nAdding those: 270,000 + 15,000 = 285,000; 285,000 + 1,500 = 286,500. So, 955 \u00d7 300 = 286,500. Since this is the hundreds place, we need to shift it three positions to the left, which would be 286,500 \u00d7 100 = 28,650,000. Wait, no, actually, multiplying by 300 is the same as multiplying by 3 and then by 100. So, 955 \u00d7 3 = 2,865, then \u00d7 100 shifts it two positions left, making it 286,500. Wait, maybe I confused the shift. Let me think again. \n\nWhen you multiply by the hundreds digit (3), you're effectively multiplying by 3 \u00d7 100. So, 955 \u00d7 3 = 2,865. Then, since it's the hundreds place, you shift the result two places to the left, which would be 2,865 \u00d7 100 = 286,500. Yes, that's correct. So, 286,500 is the partial product here.\n\nNow, adding up all the partial products:\n\nFirst partial product: 57,300 (from 6)\nSecond partial product: 573,000 (from 60)\nThird partial product: 286,500 (from 300)\n\nWait, hold on. Let me check the alignment. When you multiply by the units digit (6), the result is 57,300, which is a six-digit number. Then, multiplying by the tens digit (6) gives 57,300 shifted left by one, so 573,000. Then, multiplying by the hundreds digit (3) gives 286,500 shifted left by two, so 286,500. So, when adding them up, we need to align them properly:\n\n\u202f\u00a0\u00a057,300\n\u00a0+573,000\n\u00a0+286,500\n----------\nLet me add them step by step.\n\nFirst, add 57,300 and 573,000. \n\n57,300 + 573,000. Let's break it down:\n\n57,300 + 500,000 = 557,300\n\n557,300 + 73,000 = 630,300\n\nSo, 57,300 + 573,000 = 630,300\n\nNow, add 286,500 to 630,300.\n\n630,300 + 286,500. Let's break it down:\n\n630,300 + 200,000 = 830,300\n\n830,300 + 86,500 = 916,800\n\nSo, total is 916,800.\n\nWait, but let me check that again. Maybe I made a mistake in the addition.\n\nAlternatively, let's write them vertically:\n\n\u202f\u00a0\u00a057,300\n\u00a0+573,000\n\u00a0+286,500\n----------\nLet's add the thousands:\n\n57,300 is 57 thousand 300.\n\n573,000 is 573 thousand 000.\n\n286,500 is 286 thousand 500.\n\nAdding the thousands:\n\n57,000 + 573,000 = 630,000\n\n630,000 + 286,000 = 916,000\n\nThen add the remaining 300 + 500 = 800.\n\n916,000 + 800 = 916,800.\n\nYes, that's correct. So the total product is 916,800.\n\nBut wait, let me verify this with another method to be sure. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm step by step.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the distributive property. Let's see:\n\n955 \u00d7 366. Let's break down 366 into 300 + 60 + 6.\n\nSo, 955 \u00d7 300 = 286,500\n\n955 \u00d7 60 = 57,300\n\n955 \u00d7 6 = 57,30\n\nThen add them all together: 286,500 + 57,300 + 57,30.\n\nLet's compute 286,500 + 57,300 first. As before, that's 343,800.\n\nThen add 57,300 to 343,800: 343,800 + 57,300 = 401,100.\n\nThen add 57,30 to 401,100: 401,100 + 57,30 = 458,400.\n\nWait, that's different. Hmm, so now I get 458,400. But earlier, I got 916,800. There's a discrepancy here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, this is confusing. Let me check my calculations again.\n\nFirst method:\n\n955 \u00d7 366.\n\nBreak down 366 into 300 + 60 + 6.\n\n955 \u00d7 300 = 286,500\n\n955 \u00d7 60 = 57,300\n\n955 \u00d7 6 = 57,30\n\nAdding them:\n\n286,500 + 57,300 = 343,800\n\n343,800 + 57,300 = 401,100\n\n401,100 + 57,30 = 458,400\n\nBut in the second method, when I did the partial products shifted, I got 916,800. These two results are different. That means I must have made a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let's check the first method again. When I did the partial products:\n\nFirst partial product: 955 \u00d7 6 = 57,300 (units place)\n\nSecond partial product: 955 \u00d7 60 = 57,300 shifted left by one (tens place) \u2192 573,000\n\nThird partial product: 955 \u00d7 300 = 286,500 shifted left by two (hundreds place) \u2192 286,500 \u00d7 100 = 28,650,000? Wait, no. Wait, no, when you multiply by 300, which is 3 \u00d7 100, you first multiply by 3 and then by 100. So 955 \u00d7 3 = 2,865, then \u00d7 100 is 286,500. So that's correct.\n\nThen adding 57,300 + 573,000 + 286,500.\n\nWait, 57,300 + 573,000 is 630,300. Then 630,300 + 286,500 is 916,800. So that's correct.\n\nBut in the other method, breaking down into 300 + 60 + 6, the sum is 458,400. So which is correct?\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the second method. Let me check each multiplication again.\n\nFirst, 955 \u00d7 6:\n\n955 \u00d7 6. Let's compute:\n\n900 \u00d7 6 = 5,400\n\n50 \u00d7 6 = 300\n\n5 \u00d7 6 = 30\n\nTotal: 5,400 + 300 = 5,700; 5,700 + 30 = 5,730. Correct.\n\nThen 955 \u00d7 60:\n\n955 \u00d7 60. 955 \u00d7 6 \u00d7 10. 5,730 \u00d7 10 = 57,300. Correct.\n\nThen 955 \u00d7 300:\n\n955 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 100. 2,865 \u00d7 100 = 286,500. Correct.\n\nSo adding them: 57,300 + 57,300 + 286,500. Wait, no. Wait, in the second method, when I broke down 366 into 300 + 60 + 6, each of those is a separate partial product. So 955 \u00d7 300 is one partial product, 955 \u00d7 60 is another, and 955 \u00d7 6 is the third. So adding them together gives the total. But in the first method, when I did the partial products shifted, I had three partial products: 57,300; 573,000; 286,500. Then adding them gives 916,800. But in the second method, adding 286,500 + 57,300 + 57,300 gives 458,400. Wait, why is there a discrepancy?\n\nWait, no. Wait, in the second method, when I broke down 366 into 300 + 60 + 6, each of those is a separate multiplier. So 955 \u00d7 300 is one partial product, 955 \u00d7 60 is another, and 955 \u00d7 6 is the third. So when I add them, I should add 955 \u00d7 300 + 955 \u00d7 60 + 955 \u00d7 6. Which is 286,500 + 57,300 + 57,300. Let's compute that:\n\n286,500 + 57,300 = 343,800\n\n343,800 + 57,300 = 401,100\n\nBut 401,100 is different from 916,800. So clearly, there's a mistake here. Wait, but how?\n\nWait, perhaps I confused the place values. Let me think again. When you multiply 955 by 366, you have to consider each digit in the multiplier (366) and multiply by the multiplicand (955), then add all the partial products. However, when you break down 366 into 300 + 60 + 6, each of those is a separate partial product, but their positions in the addition should be shifted appropriately. Wait, no. Wait, actually, in the standard multiplication algorithm, each partial product is shifted left by the number of decimal places of the corresponding multiplier digit. Since all digits here are in the units, tens, hundreds places, shifting each partial product left by 1, 2, 3 positions respectively. Wait, but in the first method, when I did the partial products shifted, I shifted each subsequent partial product left by one more position. Let me clarify.\n\nFirst partial product: 955 \u00d7 6 = 57,300 (units place, so no shift)\n\nSecond partial product: 955 \u00d7 60 = 57,300 shifted left by 1 (tens place) \u2192 573,000\n\nThird partial product: 955 \u00d7 300 = 286,500 shifted left by 2 (hundreds place) \u2192 286,500 \u00d7 100 = 28,650,000? Wait, no. Wait, shifting left by two positions would be multiplying by 100, so 286,500 \u00d7 100 = 28,650,000. But that's not correct. Wait, no. Wait, when you multiply by 300, which is 3 \u00d7 100, you first multiply by 3 and then by 100. So 955 \u00d7 3 = 2,865, then \u00d7 100 = 286,500. So the partial product is 286,500, and since it's the hundreds place, you shift it two positions left, which is 286,500 \u00d7 100 = 28,650,000. But that's not correct. Wait, no. Wait, shifting left by two positions is equivalent to multiplying by 100. So 286,500 shifted left by two positions is 286,500 \u00d7 100 = 28,650,000. But that seems too large. Wait, but 955 \u00d7 300 is 286,500, which is correct. So when you shift it left by two positions, you get 286,500 \u00d7 100 = 28,650,000. But that's not the case here. Wait, maybe I'm confusing the shift. Let me think again.\n\nIn the standard multiplication algorithm, when you multiply by a digit in a certain place, you multiply the multiplicand by that digit and then shift the result to the left by the number of decimal places corresponding to that digit. Since all digits here are in the units, tens, hundreds places, shifting each partial product left by 1, 2, 3 positions respectively.\n\nSo:\n\nFirst partial product: 955 \u00d7 6 = 57,300 (no shift)\n\nSecond partial product: 955 \u00d7 60 = 57,300 shifted left by 1 (tens place) \u2192 573,000\n\nThird partial product: 955 \u00d7 300 = 286,500 shifted left by 2 (hundreds place) \u2192 286,500 \u00d7 100 = 28,650,000? Wait, no. Wait, shifting left by two positions is multiplying by 100, so 286,500 \u00d7 100 = 28,650,000. But that's not correct. Wait, no. Wait, 955 \u00d7 300 is 286,500. If you shift that left by two positions, you get 286,500 \u00d7 100 = 28,650,000. But that's not the case. Wait, no. Wait, shifting left by two positions is equivalent to multiplying by 100. So 286,500 \u00d7 100 = 28,650,000. But that seems way too big. Wait, but 955 \u00d7 300 is 286,500. So if you shift that left by two positions, you get 286,500 \u00d7 100 = 28,650,000. But that's not the same as 955 \u00d7 300 \u00d7 100. Wait, no. Wait, 955 \u00d7 300 is 286,500. Then, multiplying by 100 gives 28,650,000. But that's not the same as 955 \u00d7 300 \u00d7 100. Wait, no. Wait, 955 \u00d7 300 \u00d7 100 is 955 \u00d7 30,000 = 28,650,000. So that's correct. So in the multiplication algorithm, when you have a partial product from multiplying by a digit in a higher place, you shift it left by the number of places equal to the digit's position. So 6 is units, 60 is tens, 300 is hundreds. Therefore, the partial products are shifted left by 1, 2, 3 positions respectively.\n\nTherefore, the three partial products are:\n\n1. 57,300 (no shift)\n2. 57,300 shifted left by 1 \u2192 573,000\n3. 286,500 shifted left by 2 \u2192 286,500 \u00d7 100 = 28,650,000\n\nWait, but that can't be right. Wait, no. Wait, shifting left by two positions is multiplying by 100, so 286,500 \u00d7 100 = 28,650,000. But that's not the same as 955 \u00d7 300. Wait, 955 \u00d7 300 is 286,500. So shifting that lef