y each digit, you have to consider the place value. So 7 is in the units place, so it's 7*276. Then 2 is in the tens place, which is 2*276*10, and 7 is in the hundreds place, which is 7*276*100. So that's why the breakdown is correct.\n\nSo now, adding all three results together: 1932 + 5520 + 55200.\n\nLet me compute that step by step.\n\nFirst, add 1932 and 5520.\n\n1932 + 5520:\n\n1932 + 5000 = 6932\n\n6932 + 520 = 7452\n\nSo 1932 + 5520 = 7452.\n\nThen add 55200 to that.\n\n7452 + 55200:\n\n7452 + 50000 = 57452\n\n57452 + 5200 = 62652\n\nWait, 7452 + 55200:\n\nLet me check again. 55200 + 7000 = 62200\n\n62200 + 452 = 62652. Yes, that's correct.\n\nSo the total is 62652.\n\nWait, but let me verify this with another method to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the distributive property. Let's see.\n\n276 * 727 = 276 * (700 + 20 + 7)\n\nWhich is 276*700 + 276*20 + 276*7\n\nCompute each term:\n\n276*700: 276*7*100 = 1932*100 = 193200\n\n276*20: 5520\n\n276*7: 1932\n\nAdding them together: 193200 + 5520 + 1932\n\nLet's compute 193200 + 5520 first.\n\n193200 + 5000 = 198200\n\n198200 + 520 = 198720\n\nThen add 1932: 198720 + 1932\n\n198720 + 1000 = 199720\n\n199720 + 932 = 200652\n\nWait, that's different. Wait, so now I get 200652, but before I got 62652. That's a problem. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, in the first method, I broke down 727 into 700 + 20 + 7, which is correct. Then 276*700 is 193200, 276*20 is 5520, 276*7 is 1932. Then adding those gives 193200 + 5520 + 1932.\n\nBut in the second method, I broke down 727 into 700 + 20 + 7 as well, but maybe I miscalculated the multiplication.\n\nWait, let's check 276*700 again. 276*7 is 1932, so 1932*100 is 193200. That's correct.\n\n276*20: 276*2=552, 552*10=5520. Correct.\n\n276*7: 1932. Correct.\n\nSo adding 193200 + 5520 + 1932.\n\nLet me compute 193200 + 5520 first.\n\n193200 + 5520:\n\n193200 + 5000 = 198200\n\n198200 + 520 = 198720\n\nThen add 1932: 198720 + 1932\n\n198720 + 1000 = 199720\n\n199720 + 932 = 200,652\n\nWait, 199720 + 932:\n\n199720 + 900 = 199,620\n\n199,620 + 32 = 199,652\n\nWait, so 199720 + 1932 = 199,652\n\nBut that contradicts the previous total of 62,652. So clearly, there's a mistake here. Wait, but how?\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, in the first method, I had 1932 + 5520 + 55200 = 62,652. But in the second method, I have 193200 + 5520 + 1932 = 200,652. These are two different results. That can't be. So which one is correct?\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the first method. Let me check the first method again.\n\nFirst method:\n\n276 * 727 = 276*(700 + 20 + 7) = 276*700 + 276*20 + 276*7\n\n276*700 = 193,200\n\n276*20 = 5,520\n\n276*7 = 1,932\n\nAdding them together: 193,200 + 5,520 = 198,720; 198,720 + 1,932 = 200,652\n\nWait, so in the first method, I added 1932 + 5520 + 55200, but that's incorrect. Wait, no. Wait, in the first method, I had:\n\nFirst, multiply 276 by 7: 1932\n\nThen multiply 276 by 20: 5520\n\nThen multiply 276 by 7: 1932\n\nWait, but 727 is 7*100 + 2*10 + 7*1, so the three terms are 700, 20, and 7. Therefore, the three products are 276*700, 276*20, and 276*7. So adding those three products gives the total. So 193,200 + 5,520 + 1,932 = 200,652. So that's correct.\n\nBut in the first method, I had 1932 + 5520 + 55200. Wait, that's wrong. Because 276*700 is 193,200, not 1932. So I must have messed up the first method. So the correct total is 200,652. Therefore, my initial calculation was wrong because I incorrectly took 276*7 as 1932 instead of 193,200. Wait, no. Wait, 276*7 is 1932, but when you multiply by 700, it's 1932*100=193,200. So in the first method, I should have:\n\nFirst term: 276*700 = 193,200\n\nSecond term: 276*20 = 5,520\n\nThird term: 276*7 = 1,932\n\nAdding them: 193,200 + 5,520 = 198,720; 198,720 + 1,932 = 200,652\n\nTherefore, the correct total is 200,652. So my first method was wrong because I forgot to multiply by the appropriate power of 10. So the correct answer is 200,652.\n\nBut wait, let me check with another method. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm again, but more carefully.\n\nLet me write it out step by step.\n\nMultiplying 276 by 727:\n\nFirst, write 276\n\nMultiply by 727:\n\nStart with the units digit: 7\n\n276\n\n\u00d7\u00a0\u00a0727\n\n--------\n\nMultiply 276 by 7:\n\n7*6=42, write 2, carry 4\n\n7*7=49 +4=53, write 3, carry 5\n\n7*2=14 +5=19, write 9, carry 1\n\n7*0=0 +1=1, write 1\n\nSo that gives 1932\n\nNext, multiply 276 by 2 (tens place):\n\n276\n\n\u00d7\u00a0\u00a0727\n\n--------\n\nNext digit is 2, so multiply 276 by 2 and shift one position to the left (i.e., add a zero):\n\n276*2=552, so 5520\n\nThen multiply 276 by 7 (hundreds place):\n\n276\n\n\u00d7\u00a0\u00a0727\n\n--------\n\nNext digit is 7, multiply 276 by 7 and shift two positions to the left (i.e., add two zeros):\n\n276*7=1932, so 193200\n\nNow, add all three results:\n\n1932 (units place)\n\n5520 (tens place)\n\n193200 (hundreds place)\n\nSo adding them:\n\n1932 + 5520 = 7452\n\n7452 + 193200 = 200,652\n\nYes, that's the same result as before. So the correct answer is 200,652.\n\nTherefore, my initial mistake was in the first method where I incorrectly calculated 276*7 as 1932 instead of 193,200. That was a carryover error. So important to remember to shift the digits properly when multiplying by each place value.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another check. Let's use the fact that 276 * 727 = 276 * (700 + 20 + 7) = 276*700 + 276*20 + 276*7. As before, which gives 193,200 + 5,520 + 1,932 = 200,652.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use a calculator to verify. But since I don't have a calculator here, let me try another approach.\n\nAlternatively, note that 276 * 727 can be calculated as (200 + 70 + 6) * (700 + 20 + 7). But that might complicate things. Alternatively, use the distributive property in another way.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the difference of squares or other algebraic identities, but that might not be straightforward here.\n\nAlternatively, approximate the product. 276 is approximately 300, and 727 is approximately 700. So 300*700 = 210,000. The actual product is 200,652, which is less than 210,000, which makes sense because 276 is less than 300 and 727 is less than 700. So the approximate value is reasonable.\n\nAlternatively, compute 276*700 = 193,200\n\n276*20 = 5,520\n\n276*7 = 1,932\n\nAdding those gives 193,200 + 5,520 = 198,720; 198,720 + 1,932 = 200,652. So that's consistent.\n\nTherefore, I can be confident that the correct product is 200,652.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 276 and 727 is \\boxed{200652}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 276 and 727, we can break down the multiplication using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n276 \\times 727 = 276 \\times (700 + 20 + 7)\n\\]\n\nFirst, compute each term separately:\n\n1. \\(276 \\times 700\\):\n   \\[\n   276 \\times 700 = 276 \\times 7 \\times 100 = 1932 \\times 100 = 193200\n   \\]\n\n2. \\(276 \\times 20\\):\n   \\[\n   276 \\times 20 = 276 \\times 2 \\times 10 = 552 \\times 10 = 5520\n   \\]\n\n3. \\(276 \\times 7\\):\n   \\[\n   276 \\times 7 = 1932\n   \\]\n\nNext, add the results of these three products together:\n\n\\[\n193200 + 5520 + 1932\n\\]\n\nFirst, add 193200 and 5520:\n\n\\[\n193200 + 5520 = 198720\n\\]\n\nThen, add the result to 1932:\n\n\\[\n198720 + 1932 = 200652\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 276 and 727 is \\(\\boxed{200652}\\).",
