 is actually 15472? Let me check with a calculator. Wait, if I can't use a calculator, maybe I can check with another multiplication.\n\nAlternatively, let's use the fact that 967 * 700 = 67,690. Then subtract 967 * 9 = 8,703. So 67,690 - 8,703 = 58,987. Wait, that's not matching either. Wait, no, that's not the right approach. \n\nWait, perhaps I can use the following: 967 * 691 = 967 * (700 - 9) = 967*700 - 967*9 = 67,690 - 8,703 = 58,987. But that's not correct. Wait, 967*700 is 67,690. 967*9 is 8,703. So 67,690 - 8,703 = 58,987. But that's not the same as 15,472. So clearly, this approach is wrong. \n\nWait, but why? Because when you do 967*(700 - 9), you have to distribute the multiplication over subtraction. So 967*700 - 967*9. But 967*700 is 67,690. 967*9 is 8,703. So 67,690 - 8,703 = 58,987. But that's not the same as 15,472. So clearly, this approach is wrong. Therefore, my mistake here is that 691 is not 700 - 9. 700 - 9 is 691, but when you subtract 967*9 from 967*700, you get 58,987, which is not the correct product. Therefore, this method is invalid. \n\nTherefore, the first method must be correct. So why does the second method give a different answer? Because when I broke down 967 into 900 + 60 +7 and 691 into 600 +90 +1, and multiplied each part, then added all the terms, I must have made a mistake in the addition. Wait, but when I added all the terms, I got 668,197, which is way too big. But 967*691 is definitely not 668,197. Let me check with another approach. \n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the second method. Let me check each term again:\n\nFirst term: 900*600 = 540,000\n\nSecond term: 900*90 = 81,000\n\nThird term: 900*1 = 900\n\nFourth term: 60*600 = 36,000\n\nFifth term: 60*90 = 5,400\n\nSixth term: 60*1 = 60\n\nSeventh term: 7*600 = 4,200\n\nEighth term: 7*90 = 630\n\nNinth term: 7*1 = 7\n\nAdding them:\n\n540,000 + 81,000 = 621,000\n\n621,000 + 900 = 621,900\n\n621,900 + 36,000 = 657,900\n\n657,900 + 5,400 = 663,300\n\n663,300 + 60 = 66