-----\nStarting from the right:\n6 + 0 = 6\n9 + 2 = 11, carry over 1\n6 + 0 + 1 (carry) = 7\n0 + 7 = 7\nSo, 43896. Wait, let me check that again.\n\nWait, 6696 + 37200. Let's break it down:\n\n37200 + 6000 = 43200\n43200 + 696 = 43896. Yes, that's correct.\n\nSo, the total product is 43896. Let me verify this with another method to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the distributive property. Let's break down 186 and 236 into their prime factors or components. Alternatively, maybe break them into hundreds, tens, and units.\n\n186 can be broken into 100 + 80 + 6, and 236 can be broken into 200 + 30 + 6.\n\nSo, 186 * 236 = (100 + 80 + 6) * (200 + 30 + 6). Hmm, but that might get complicated. Alternatively, maybe use the formula (a + b)(c + d) = ac + ad + bc + bd. Let's try that.\n\nLet me assign a = 100, b = 80, c = 200, d = 6.\n\nThen, (a + b)(c + d) = a*c + a*d + b*c + b*d.\n\nCompute each term:\n\na*c = 100*200 = 20,000\n\na*d = 100*6 = 600\n\nb*c = 80*200 = 16,000\n\nb*d = 80*6 = 480\n\nNow, add all these together:\n\n20,000 + 600 = 20,600\n\n20,600 + 16,000 = 36,600\n\n36,600 + 480 = 37,080\n\nWait, but that's 37,080. But earlier, the standard multiplication gave me 43,896. That's a discrepancy. So, clearly, I made a mistake here. Let me check my breakdown.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the breakdown. Let me check again.\n\nWait, 186 is 100 + 80 + 6. 236 is 200 + 30 + 6. So, when I use the distributive property, I need to multiply each term in the first number with each term in the second number. So, it's (100 + 80 + 6)(200 + 30 + 6) = 100*200 + 100*30 + 100*6 + 80*200 + 80*30 + 80*6 + 6*200 + 6*30 + 6*6. That's 9 terms. So, when I did it earlier, I only considered four terms. So, I missed the 6*200 and 6*30 terms. That's why the total was off.\n\nSo, let's recalculate using the distributive property correctly.\n\nCompute each term:\n\n100*200 = 20,000\n\n100*30 = 3,000\n\n100*6 = 600\n\n80*200 = 16,000\n\n80*30 = 2,400\n\n80*6 = 480\n\n6*200 = 1,200\n\n6*30 = 180\n\n6*6 = 36\n\nNow, add all these together:\n\n20,000 + 3,000 = 23,000\n\n23,000 + 600 = 23,600\n\n23,600 + 16,000 = 39,600\n\n39,600 + 2,400 = 42,000\n\n42,000 + 480 = 42,480\n\n42,480 + 1,200 = 43,680\n\n43,680 + 180 = 43,860\n\n43,860 + 36 = 43,896\n\nAh, there we go! So, that matches the standard multiplication result of 43,896. So, that's reassuring. So, the product is indeed 43,896.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using another method, like the difference of squares or something, but that might be more complicated. Alternatively, use a calculator to verify. But since I don't have a calculator here, let me try another step-by-step multiplication.\n\nAlternatively, multiply 186 by 200 and 186 by 36, then add the results.\n\n186 * 200. Well, 186 * 2 = 372, so 186 * 200 = 37,200.\n\nThen, 186 * 36. Let's compute that. 186 * 30 = 5,580 and 186 * 6 = 1,116. So, 5,580 + 1,116 = 6,696.\n\nNow, add 37,200 + 6,696. Let's do that:\n\n37,200 + 6,000 = 43,200\n\n43,200 + 696 = 43,896. Yep, same result. So, that's another confirmation.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the lattice method? Hmm, that might be tedious, but let's try.\n\nBut maybe that's overcomplicating. Since multiple methods are giving me 43,896, I can be confident that's the correct answer.\n\nWait, just to make sure, let me do the standard multiplication again step by step.\n\nMultiplying 186 by 236:\n\nFirst, write 186 * 236.\n\nStart with the units digit of 236, which is 6. Multiply 186 by 6:\n\n6 * 186. Let's compute:\n\n6 * 100 = 600\n\n6 * 80 = 480\n\n6 * 6 = 36\n\nAdding those: 600 + 480 = 1,080; 1,080 + 36 = 1,116. So, 1,116. Write this as the first partial product, shifted one position to the left (since it's the units place).\n\nNext, multiply 186 by 3 (tens digit of 236):\n\n3 * 186. Let's compute:\n\n3 * 100 = 300\n\n3 * 80 = 240\n\n3 * 6 = 18\n\nAdding those: 300 + 240 = 540; 540 + 18 = 558. So, 558. Shifted one position left (tens place), so 5,580.\n\nNext, multiply 186 by 2 (hundreds digit of 236):\n\n2 * 186. Let's compute:\n\n2 * 100 = 200\n\n2 * 80 = 160\n\n2 * 6 = 12\n\nAdding those: 200 + 160 = 360; 360 + 12 = 372. So, 372. Shifted two positions left (hundreds place), so 37,200.\n\nNow, add the three partial products:\n\n1,116 (units place)\n+ 5,580 (tens place)\n+ 37,200 (hundreds place)\n\nLet's add 1,116 + 5,580 first. 1,116 + 5,580. Let's break it down:\n\n1,000 + 5,000 = 6,000\n\n100 + 500 = 600\n\n16 + 80 = 96\n\nSo, 6,000 + 600 = 6,600; 6,600 + 96 = 6,696. Then add 37,200 to 6,696:\n\n6,696 + 37,200. Let's compute:\n\n6,000 + 37,000 = 43,000\n\n696 + 200 = 896\n\n43,000 + 896 = 43,896. Yep, same result.\n\nSo, all methods lead to 43,896. Therefore, I can be confident that the product of 186 and 236 is 43,896.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 186 and 236 is \\boxed{43896}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 186 and 236, we can use the standard multiplication method:\n\n1. Multiply 186 by the units digit (6) of 236:\n   \\[\n   6 \\times 186 = 1116\n   \\]\n   This is the first partial product.\n\n2. Multiply 186 by the tens digit (3) of 236 and shift the result one position to the left:\n   \\[\n   3 \\times 186 = 558\n   \\]\n   Shifted left by one position: 5580.\n\n3. Multiply 186 by the hundreds digit (2) of 236 and shift the result two positions to the left:\n   \\[\n   2 \\times 186 = 372\n   \\]\n   Shifted left by two positions: 37200.\n\n4. Add the partial products together:\n   \\[\n   1116 + 5580 + 37200 = 43896\n   \\]\n\nWe can verify this using the distributive property:\n- Break down 186 into 100 + 80 + 6 and 236 into 200 + 30 + 6.\n- Calculate each term and sum them up:\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &100 \\times 200 = 20000, \\\\\n  &100 \\times 30 = 3000, \\\\\n  &100 \\times 6 = 600, \\\\\n  &80 \\times 200 = 16000, \\\\\n  &80 \\times 30 = 2400, \\\\\n  &80 \\times 6 = 480, \\\\\n  &6 \\times 200 = 1200, \\\\\n  &6 \\times 30 = 180, \\\\\n  &6 \\times 6 = 36.\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n- Sum all terms:\n  \\[\n  20000 + 3000 + 600 + 16000 + 2400 + 480 + 1200 + 180 + 36 = 43896\n  \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 186 and 236 is \\(\\boxed{43896}\\).",
