ns place: 8\n\nHundreds place: 8 + 1 (carry) = 9\n\nThousands place: 0 (since we started with 622, which is three digits, so there's no thousands place in the multiplicand)\n\nWait, but the multiplicand is 622, which is three digits, so when multiplying by 314 (a three-digit number), the product can be up to four digits. Wait, 622*314. Let's see, 600*300=180,000, so the product is at least 180,000. So, the product should be a four-digit number. But according to the step-by-step multiplication:\n\nWait, let's do the standard multiplication step by step properly.\n\nLet me write it out:\n\n\u202f\xa0\xa0622\n×\xa0\xa0314\n--------\n\xa0\xa0888\xa0\xa0(622 × 4)\n\xa0\xa06220\xa0\xa0(622 × 1, shifted one place left)\n\xa0\xa0186600\xa0\xa0(622 × 3, shifted two places left)\n--------\n\xa0\xa0196368\n\nWait, but when I added 8880 + 62200 + 1866000, I got 1,963,680. But when I did the step-by-step addition here, I get 196,368. Wait, that's inconsistent. There's a discrepancy here. Let me check the addition again.\n\nWait, 8880 + 62200 + 1866000.\n\nFirst, 8880 + 62200 = 71,080.\n\nThen, 71,080 + 1,866,000 = 1,937,080.\n\nWait, that's 1,937,080. But in the step-by-step multiplication, I have 196,368. That's way off. So clearly, there's a mistake in the shifting.\n\nWait, perhaps I messed up the shifting in the first method. Let me check again.\n\nWhen multiplying by the units digit (4), we shift the result one place left: 888 becomes 8880.\n\nWhen multiplying by the tens digit (1), we shift two places left: 622 becomes 62200.\n\nWhen multiplying by the hundreds digit (3), we shift three places left: 1866 becomes 1866000.\n\nBut 1866000 is a six-digit number. Adding 8880 (four digits) + 62200 (five digits) + 1866000 (six digits) is not straightforward. Wait, actually, when adding, we need to align the digits properly.\n\nLet me write all three numbers with the correct shifts:\n\n- 8880 (from 4)\n- 62200 (from 1 shifted left by two)\n- 1866000 (from 3 shifted left by three)\