      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 744 and 121. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers can be done by breaking them down into smaller parts or using some multiplication techniques. Let me think about the best way to approach this.\n\nFirst, maybe I can just do the standard multiplication method. Let's try that. 744 times 121. Let me write it out vertically to keep track:\n\n\u202f\u00a0\u00a0744\n\u00d7\u00a0\u00a0121\n--------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit of the second number, which is 1. Multiply 744 by 1, that's easy: 744.\n\nThen move to the next digit, which is 2. But wait, actually, in multiplication, you go from right to left, so the next digit is 2 in the tens place. So I need to multiply 744 by 2 and then shift one position to the left (which is equivalent to multiplying by 10). So 744 \u00d7 2 = 1488, and then shift left: 14880.\n\nNext, the leftmost digit is 1 in the hundreds place. So multiply 744 by 1 and shift two positions to the left (multiplying by 100). So 744 \u00d7 1 = 744, shifted two places becomes 74400.\n\nNow add all those together: 74400 + 14880 + 744.\n\nLet me add them step by step. First, 74400 + 14880. Let's see, 74400 + 10000 is 84400, then subtract the 1200 that was subtracted from 14880? Wait, maybe another way. 74400 + 14880. Break it down:\n\n74400 + 10000 = 84400\n\n84400 + 4000 = 88400\n\n88400 + 880 = 89280\n\nSo 74400 + 14880 = 89280\n\nThen add 744 to that: 89280 + 744. Let's break it down:\n\n89280 + 700 = 89980\n\n89980 + 44 = 90024\n\nSo total is 90,024.\n\nWait, let me check that again. Maybe I made a mistake in the addition.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's verify.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the distributive property. 121 is 120 + 1. So 744 \u00d7 121 = 744 \u00d7 (120 + 1) = 744 \u00d7 120 + 744 \u00d7 1.\n\nCalculate 744 \u00d7 120. Let's break that down: 744 \u00d7 100 = 74,400; 744 \u00d7 20 = 14,880. So 74,400 + 14,880 = 89,280. Then add 744: 89,280 + 744 = 90,024. Same result. Okay, that seems consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use another decomposition. For example, 121 is 125 - 4. So 744 \u00d7 125 - 744 \u00d7 4. Let's try that.\n\nFirst, 744 \u00d7 125. Hmm, 125 is 100 + 25. So 744 \u00d7 100 = 74,400; 744 \u00d7 25 = ?\n\n744 \u00d7 25: 744 \u00d7 10 = 7,440; 744 \u00d7 20 = 14,880; 744 \u00d7 5 = 3,720. So 7,440 + 14,880 = 22,320; 22,320 + 3,720 = 26,040. So 744 \u00d7 25 = 26,040. Then 744 \u00d7 100 = 74,400. So 74,400 + 26,040 = 100,440. Then subtract 744 \u00d7 4. 744 \u00d7 4: 700 \u00d7 4 = 2,800; 44 \u00d7 4 = 176; total is 2,800 + 176 = 2,976. So 100,440 - 2,976 = 97,464. Wait, that's different. Hmm, so this method gives 97,464, which is different from the previous answer of 90,024. That's a problem. So I must have made a mistake here.\n\nWait, let me check again. 744 \u00d7 125 - 744 \u00d7 4. Let's compute 744 \u00d7 125 first.\n\nAlternatively, 125 is 5 \u00d7 25. So 744 \u00d7 25 = 26,040 as before. Then 26,040 \u00d7 5 = 130,200. Then subtract 744 \u00d7 4 = 2,976. So 130,200 - 2,976 = 127,224. Wait, that's even different. Hmm, now I'm confused.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the decomposition. Let me start over.\n\nIf 121 = 125 - 4, then 744 \u00d7 121 = 744 \u00d7 (125 - 4) = 744 \u00d7 125 - 744 \u00d7 4.\n\nCompute 744 \u00d7 125:\n\nFirst, 744 \u00d7 100 = 74,400\n\n744 \u00d7 25 = ?\n\n744 \u00d7 25: 744 \u00d7 20 = 14,880; 744 \u00d7 5 = 3,720. So 14,880 + 3,720 = 18,600. Wait, earlier I thought 744 \u00d7 25 was 26,040. That was wrong. Wait, 744 \u00d7 25 is 744 \u00d7 (20 + 5) = 744\u00d720 + 744\u00d75 = 14,880 + 3,720 = 18,600. So 744 \u00d7 25 = 18,600. Then 744 \u00d7 100 = 74,400. So 74,400 + 18,600 = 93,000. Then 744 \u00d7 125 = 93,000. Then subtract 744 \u00d7 4 = 2,976. So 93,000 - 2,976 = 90,024. Ah, there we go. So that matches the previous result. So my initial mistake was miscalculating 744 \u00d7 25. So that method also gives 90,024. So that's good.\n\nTherefore, regardless of the method, the product is 90,024. Let me confirm with another method just to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the lattice method or another multiplication technique. But that might be more complicated. Alternatively, use a calculator. But since I don't have a calculator here, maybe I can check with another step-by-step multiplication.\n\nLet me try multiplying 744 by 121 using the standard algorithm again, step by step.\n\nFirst, write 744 \u00d7 121:\n\n\u202f\u00a0\u00a0744\n\u00d7\u00a0\u00a0121\n--------\n\nStart with the rightmost digit of 121, which is 1. Multiply 744 by 1: 744.\n\nNext digit is 2 (tens place). Multiply 744 by 2: 1,488. Shift one position to the left (multiply by 10): 14,880.\n\nNext digit is 1 (hundreds place). Multiply 744 by 1: 744. Shift two positions to the left (multiply by 100): 74,400.\n\nNow add them up:\n\n74,400\n\n+\u00a0\u00a014,880\n\n= 89,280\n\n89,280\n\n+\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0744\n\n= 90,024\n\nYes, same result. So that's consistent. Therefore, the product of 744 and 121 is 90,024.\n\nAlternatively, maybe check using another decomposition. For example, 121 is 120 + 1, so 744 \u00d7 121 = 744 \u00d7 120 + 744 \u00d7 1. As before, 744 \u00d7 120 = 89,280 and 744 \u00d7 1 = 744, so total is 89,280 + 744 = 90,024. Same answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the fact that 121 is 11 squared. So 744 \u00d7 11\u00b2. Let's compute 744 \u00d7 11 first, then square the result.\n\n744 \u00d7 11: 744 \u00d7 10 = 7,440; 744 \u00d7 1 = 744; sum is 7,440 + 744 = 8,184. Then square that: 8,184\u00b2. Hmm, that might be more complicated, but let's try.\n\n8,184 \u00d7 8,184. Let's compute this using (a + b)\u00b2 = a\u00b2 + 2ab + b\u00b2, where a = 8,000 and b = 184.\n\nSo, (8,000 + 184)\u00b2 = 8,000\u00b2 + 2 \u00d7 8,000 \u00d7 184 + 184\u00b2.\n\nCompute each term:\n\n8,000\u00b2 = 64,000,000\n\n2 \u00d7 8,000 \u00d7 184 = 16,000 \u00d7 184. Let's compute 16,000 \u00d7 100 = 1,600,000; 16,000 \u00d7 80 = 1,280,000; 16,000 \u00d7 4 = 64,000. So total is 1,600,000 + 1,280,000 = 2,880,000 + 64,000 = 2,944,000.\n\n184\u00b2: 180\u00b2 = 32,400; 4\u00b2 = 16; 2 \u00d7 180 \u00d7 4 = 1,440. So 32,400 + 1,440 + 16 = 33,856.\n\nNow add all three terms:\n\n64,000,000 + 2,944,000 = 66,944,000\n\n66,944,000 + 33,856 = 67,077,856\n\nSo 8,184\u00b2 = 67,077,856. Wait, but that's way larger than 90,024. That can't be right. Wait, that's a problem. So clearly, I made a mistake here. Wait, but 744 \u00d7 121 is 90,024, but when I compute 8,184 squared, I get 67 million? That's way off. So clearly, this method is wrong. Wait, why?\n\nWait, no. Wait, 744 \u00d7 121 is 744 \u00d7 (11 \u00d7 11) = (744 \u00d7 11) \u00d7 11. So first compute 744 \u00d7 11 = 8,184, then multiply by 11 again. So 8,184 \u00d7 11. Let's compute that.\n\n8,184 \u00d7 10 = 81,840\n\n8,184 \u00d7 1 = 8,184\n\nSum: 81,840 + 8,184 = 89,024. Wait, that's different from before. Wait, earlier when I did 744 \u00d7 121, I got 90,024. But here, using this method, I get 89,024. Which is conflicting. So there's a mistake here.\n\nWait, let's check 744 \u00d7 11 again. 744 \u00d7 10 is 7,440. 744 \u00d7 1 is 744. So 7,440 + 744 = 8,184. Then 8,184 \u00d7 11: 8,184 \u00d7 10 = 81,840; 8,184 \u00d7 1 = 8,184. So 81,840 + 8,184 = 89,024. But earlier, when I did 744 \u00d7 121, I had 90,024. So there's a discrepancy here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's compute 744 \u00d7 121 directly. Let's do it step by step.\n\nFirst, 744 \u00d7 100 = 74,400\n\n744 \u00d7 20 = 14,880\n\n744 \u00d7 1 = 744\n\nSo 74,400 + 14,880 = 89,280\n\n89,280 + 744 = 90,024\n\nBut when I did 744 \u00d7 11 first, then \u00d711 again, I got 89,024. So which is correct?\n\nWait, 744 \u00d7 121 is 744 \u00d7 (100 + 20 + 1) = 74,400 + 14,880 + 744 = 90,024. So that's correct. Then why when I did 744 \u00d7 11 = 8,184, then \u00d711 again, I get 89,024? Because 8,184 \u00d7 11 is 89,024, but 744 \u00d7 121 is 90,024. So there's a mistake here. Wait, no. Wait, 744 \u0