      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 310 and 687. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers can be done by breaking them down into parts or using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let me think which method would be easier here. Maybe breaking them down would be better to avoid mistakes. Let me try that.\n\nFirst, let's break down 310 into 300 + 10. Then, 687 can be broken down into 600 + 80 + 7. So, if I multiply each part separately and then add them up, that should work. Let me write that out:\n\n(300 + 10) * (600 + 80 + 7)\n\nUsing the distributive property, this becomes:\n\n300*600 + 300*80 + 300*7 + 10*600 + 10*80 + 10*7\n\nLet me calculate each term step by step.\n\nFirst term: 300*600. Well, 3*6 is 18, and then there are three zeros from 300 and two zeros from 600, so total of five zeros. So that's 180000.\n\nSecond term: 300*80. 3*8 is 24, and then two zeros from 300 and one zero from 80, so three zeros. That's 24000.\n\nThird term: 300*7. That's straightforward, 300*7 is 2100.\n\nFourth term: 10*600. 1*6 is 6, and two zeros from 10 and one zero from 600, so three zeros. That's 6000.\n\nFifth term: 10*80. 1*8 is 8, two zeros from 10 and one zero from 80, so three zeros. That's 800.\n\nSixth term: 10*7. That's 70.\n\nNow, let's add all these up:\n\n180000 + 24000 = 204000\n\n204000 + 2100 = 206100\n\n206100 + 6000 = 212100\n\n212100 + 800 = 212900\n\n212900 + 70 = 213070\n\nWait, so the total is 213,070? Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake in addition.\n\nStarting over:\n\nFirst term: 180,000\n\nSecond term: +24,000 \u2192 204,000\n\nThird term: +2,100 \u2192 206,100\n\nFourth term: +6,000 \u2192 212,100\n\nFifth term: +800 \u2192 212,900\n\nSixth term: +70 \u2192 213,070\n\nYes, that seems right. But let me verify using another method to be absolutely sure. Maybe the standard multiplication algorithm.\n\nLet's write it out vertically:\n\n    310\n  x687\n  -------\n\nFirst, multiply 310 by 7:\n\n310*7 = 2170\n\nThen multiply 310 by 80 (which is 8*10):\n\n310*8 = 2480, then add a zero: 24800\n\nThen multiply 310 by 600 (which is 6*100):\n\n310*6 = 1860, add two zeros: 186000\n\nNow add them all together:\n\n2170\n\n+24800\n\n+186000\n\nLet's add 2170 + 24800 first. 2170 + 24800 = 27, 970? Wait, 2170 + 24800. Let's do 24800 + 2000 = 26800, then +170 = 26970.\n\nThen add 186000 to 26970. 186000 + 20000 = 206000, then +6970 = 212,970.\n\nWait, that's different from the previous result. Wait, so now I have 212,970. But earlier I had 213,070. There's a discrepancy here. Which one is correct?\n\nHmm, so I must have made a mistake in one of the methods. Let me check both again.\n\nFirst method:\n\nBreaking down 310 into 300 + 10 and 687 into 600 + 80 + 7.\n\nCalculations:\n\n300*600 = 180,000\n\n300*80 = 24,000\n\n300*7 = 2,100\n\n10*600 = 6,000\n\n10*80 = 800\n\n10*7 = 70\n\nAdding them up:\n\n180,000 + 24,000 = 204,000\n\n204,000 + 2,100 = 206,100\n\n206,100 + 6,000 = 212,100\n\n212,100 + 800 = 212,900\n\n212,900 + 70 = 213,070\n\nSecond method:\n\nStandard multiplication:\n\n310*7 = 2170\n\n310*80 = 24,800\n\n310*600 = 186,000\n\nAdding them:\n\n2170 + 24,800 = 26,970\n\n26,970 + 186,000 = 212,970\n\nSo now, two different results. Which one is correct? Let's check with another method. Maybe using the standard algorithm step by step.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made a mistake in the second method. Let's check 310*7. 310*7: 300*7=2100, 10*7=70, so 2100+70=2170. That's correct.\n\n310*80: 310*8=2480, then add a zero: 24800. Correct.\n\n310*600: 310*6=1860, add two zeros: 186000. Correct.\n\nAdding 2170 + 24800: 2170 + 24800. Let's compute 24800 + 2000 = 26800, then +170 = 26970. Then 26970 + 186000: 26970 + 186000. Let's break it down: 186000 + 20000 = 206000, then +6970 = 212,970. So that's 212,970.\n\nBut the first method gave 213,070. There's a difference of 100. Hmm. Let's check if I added correctly in the first method.\n\nFirst method:\n\n180,000 (300*600)\n\n+24,000 (300*80) = 204,000\n\n+2,100 (300*7) = 206,100\n\n+6,000 (10*600) = 212,100\n\n+800 (10*80) = 212,900\n\n+70 (10*7) = 213,070\n\nWait, so in the first method, when adding 212,100 + 6,000, that's 218,100? Wait, no. Wait, 212,100 + 6,000 is 218,100? Wait, no. Wait, 212,100 + 6,000 is 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000 is 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000 is 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000 is 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 218,100? Wait, 212,100 + 6,000: 212,100 + 6,000 = 