ct.\n\nSo the distributive method gives 157,990, which is different from the standard multiplication result of 11,956. So clearly, I messed up the standard multiplication steps.\n\nWait, why is there such a big difference? Let me check the standard multiplication again.\n\nWait, perhaps I wrote the numbers in the multiplication setup incorrectly. Let me check the original multiplication:\n\n\u202f\u00a0\u00a0854\n\u00d7\u00a0\u00a0185\n--------\n\nSo, 854 is the multiplicand, and 185 is the multiplier. So, when multiplying, we start from the rightmost digit of the multiplier (5), then 8, then 1.\n\nSo, the partial products should be:\n\nFirst partial product: 854 \u00d7 5 = 4,270\n\nSecond partial product: 854 \u00d7 8 = 6,832\n\nThird partial product: 854 \u00d7 1 = 854\n\nBut when adding them, we need to align them by place value. Let me write them vertically:\n\n\u202f\u00a0\u00a04,270\n\u202f\u00a0\u00a06,832\n+\u00a0\u00a0854\n--------\n\nBut when adding, the rightmost digits are 0, 2, 4. So:\n\nUnits place: 0 + 2 + 4 = 6\n\nTens place: 7 + 3 + 5 = 15 (write 5, carry 1)\n\nHundreds place: 2 + 8 + 8 + 1 (carry) = 21 (write 1, carry 2)\n\nThousands place: 4 + 6 + 0 + 2 (carry) = 12 (write 2, carry 1)\n\nTen-thousands place: 0 + 0 + 0 + 1 (carry) = 1\n\nSo total is 121,556. Wait, but that's different from the previous 11,956. Wait, so maybe I added the partial products incorrectly before. Let me check again.\n\nWait, 4,270 + 6,832 + 854.\n\nLet me add 4,270 + 6,832 first:\n\n4,270 + 6,832:\n\n4,000 + 6,000 = 10,000\n\n200 + 800 = 1,000\n\n70 + 30 = 100\n\n0 + 2 = 2\n\nTotal: 10,000 + 1,000 = 11,000; 11,000 + 100 = 11,100; 11,100 + 2 = 11,102\n\nThen add 854:\n\n11,102 + 854:\n\n11,102 + 800 = 11,902\n\n11,902 + 50 = 11,952\n\n11,952 + 4 = 11,956\n\nBut according to the distributive method, the answer is 157,990. So there's a conflict here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the multiplication steps. Let me check 854 \u00d7 5 again.\n\n854 \u0