ct two places to the left (multiplying by 100) and add it to the sum of the first two partial products.\n\nSo, let's correct that.\n\nFirst partial product: 4,270 (units place)\n\nSecond partial product: 6,832 (tens place, shifted left by 1: 68,320)\n\nThird partial product: 854 (hundreds place, shifted left by 2: 85,400)\n\nNow, add them all together:\n\n4,270\n\n+68,320\n\n+85,400\n\nLet's compute step by step.\n\nFirst, add 4,270 + 68,320:\n\n4,270 + 68,320 = 72,590\n\nThen, add 85,400 to 72,590:\n\n72,590 + 85,400 = 157,990\n\nWhich matches the result from the distributive method. So, the correct product is 157,990.\n\nTherefore, my initial mistake was not shifting the second partial product one place to the left when adding. That's why the sum was off by a factor of 10. So, the correct answer is 157,990.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 854 and 185 is \\boxed{157990}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 854 and 185, we can use the distributive property:\n\n1. Break down 185 into 200 - 15.\n2. Calculate each part separately:\n   - \\( 854 \\times 200 = 170800 \\)\n   - \\( 854 \\times 15 = 12810 \\)\n3. Subtract the results:\n   - \\( 170800 - 12810 = 157990 \\)\n\nThus, the product of 854 and 185 is \\(\\boxed{157990}\\).",
