ave an error in the partial products. But how?\n\nWait, perhaps the error is in the alignment of the partial products. Let me check the vertical multiplication again.\n\nMultiplying 818 by 818:\n\n          818\n        x 818\n        --------\n\nFirst, multiply 818 by 8 (units place):\n\n          818\n        x   8\n        --------\n          6542\n\nNext, multiply 818 by 1 (tens place), shifted one position to the left:\n\n          818\n        x  1\n        --------\n          65420\n\nThen, multiply 818 by 8 (hundreds place), shifted two positions to the left:\n\n          818\n        x  800\n        --------\n          654200\n\nThen, multiply 818 by 1 (thousands place), shifted three positions to the left:\n\n          818\n        x 1000\n        --------\n          818000\n\nNow, adding these partial products:\n\n6542\n\n+65420\n\n+654200\n\n+818000\n\nBut when adding, we need to align them properly. Let's write them with the same number of digits:\n\nFirst partial product: 06,542\n\nSecond partial product: 065,420\n\nThird partial product: 654,200\n\nFourth partial product: 818,000\n\nNow, adding them:\n\n06,542\n\n+065,420\n\n+654,200\n\n+818,000\n\nFirst, add 06,542 + 065,420 = 71,962\n\nThen, add 71,962 + 654,200 = 726,162\n\nThen, add 726,162 + 818,000 = 1,544,162\n\nBut this is still wrong. Therefore, there must be a mistake in the partial products. But how?\n\nWait, perhaps the error is in the third partial product. 818 x 800 = 654,400. Shifted two positions to the left: 654,400 becomes 654,4000? Wait, no. Wait, shifting two positions to the left is multiplying by 100, so 654,400 x 100 = 65,440,000. But that's not correct. Wait, no. Wait, the partial product is 654,400, which is 654,400. When you shift it two positions to the left, you have to write it as 654,400 shifted two places to the left, which is 654,400 -> 654,4000? Wait, no. Wait, shifting two positions to the left is equivalent to multiplying by 100, so 654,400 x 100 = 65,440,000. But th