ds digit 9) is shifted left by 3: 6642 becomes 6642000.\n\nThen, adding them:\n\n36900\n+\u00a0\u00a00000\n+6642000\n= 6678900\n\nBut 6678900 is 6,678,900. However, when I did the distributive method, I got 667,890. So, why is there a difference?\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the distributive method. Let me check that again.\n\n738 \u00d7 905 = 738 \u00d7 (900 + 5) = 738\u00d7900 + 738\u00d75.\n\n738 \u00d7 900: 738 \u00d7 9 = 6642, so 6642 \u00d7 100 = 664,200.\n\n738 \u00d7 5 = 3,690.\n\nAdding 664,200 + 3,690: 664,200 + 3,000 = 667,200; 667,200 + 690 = 667,890.\n\nSo, that's 667,890. But according to the standard multiplication, it's 6,678,900. That's a factor of 10 difference. So, which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the standard multiplication. Let me check the partial products again.\n\nFirst partial product: 738 \u00d7 5 = 3,690. Shift left by 1: 36,900.\n\nSecond partial product: 738 \u00d7 0 = 0. Shift left by 2: 00,000.\n\nThird partial product: 738 \u00d7 9 = 6,642. Shift left by 3: 664,200.\n\nAdding them: 36,900 + 0 + 664,200 = 661,100. Wait, that's different. Wait, no, 36,900 + 664,200 = 661,100. But that's not matching either. Wait, this is confusing.\n\nWait, maybe I need to write them all aligned properly:\n\nFirst partial product: 3,690 shifted left by 1: 36,900\n\nSecond partial product: 0 shifted left by 2: 00,000\n\nThird partial product: 6,642 shifted left by 3: 664,200\n\nNow, adding them:\n\n36,900\n\n+\u00a0\u00a000,000\n\n+664,200\n\n= 661,100\n\nWait, that's 661,100. But that's not matching the distributive method either. Wait, this is getting more confusing. There must be a mistake in how I'm shifting the partial products.\n\nWait, perhaps the problem is that when you shift left by 3 places, you need to shift each subsequent partial product further left. Wait, no. Let me recall the standard multiplication steps.\n\nWhen you multiply by each digit, you bring down the partial product and shift left by the number o