output:  ['\n\nTo find the product of 786 and 114, we can use the distributive property and break down the numbers into smaller components.\n\nFirst, split 786 into 700, 80, and 6, and 114 into 100, 10, and 4. Then multiply each part separately and sum the results:\n\n1. Multiply 700 by each part of 114:\n   - \\(700 \\times 100 = 70,000\\)\n   - \\(700 \\times 10 = 7,000\\)\n   - \\(700 \\times 4 = 2,800\\)\n\n2. Multiply 80 by each part of 114:\n   - \\(80 \\times 100 = 8,000\\)\n   - \\(80 \\times 10 = 800\\)\n   - \\(80 \\times 4 = 320\\)\n\n3. Multiply 6 by each part of 114:\n   - \\(6 \\times 100 = 600\\)\n   - \\(6 \\times 10 = 60\\)\n   - \\(6 \\times 4 = 24\\)\n\nNow, add all these results together:\n\\[\n70,000 + 7,000 + 2,800 + 8,000 + 800 + 320 + 600 + 60 + 24\n\\]\n\nAdding step-by-step:\n\\[\n70,000 + 7,000 = 77,000\n\\]\n\\[\n77,000 + 2,800 = 79,800\n\\]\n\\[\n79,800 + 8,000 = 87,800\n\\]\n\\[\n87,800 + 800 = 88,600\n\\]\n\\[\n88,600 + 320 = 88,920\n\\]\n\\[\n88,920 + 600 = 89,520\n\\]\n\\[\n89,520 + 60 = 89,580\n\\]\n\\[\n89,580 + 24 = 89,604\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 786 and 114 is \\(\\boxed{89604}\\).', "\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 786 and 114. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two-digit numbers can sometimes be broken down into smaller parts to make it easier. Maybe I can use the method where you split the numbers into parts that are easier to multiply. Let me think.\n\nFirst, let me write down the numbers: 786 and 114. Both are three-digit numbers. Maybe I can break them down into smaller components. For example, 786 can be split into 700 + 80 + 6, and 114 can be split into 100 + 10 + 4. Then, I can use the distributive property to multiply each part. Let me try that.\n\nSo, if I split 786 into 700, 80, and 6, and 114 into 100, 10, and 4, then the multiplication would be:\n\n(700 + 80 + 6) × (100 + 10 + 4)\n\nBut actually, since multiplication distributes over addition, I can rearrange the terms. Maybe it's easier to multiply each part separately and then add them up. Let me try that.\n\nFirst, multiply 700 by each of the parts of 114:\n\n700 × 100 = 70,000\n\n700 × 10 = 7,000\n\n700 × 4 = 2,800\n\nThen, multiply 80 by each part of 114:\n\n80 × 100 = 8,000\n\n80 × 10 = 800\n\n80 × 4 = 320\n\nNext, multiply 6 by each part of 114:\n\n6 × 100 = 600\n\n6 × 10 = 60\n\n6 × 4 = 24\n\nNow, add all those results together:\n\n70,000 + 7,000 + 2,800 + 8,000 + 800 + 320 + 600 + 60 + 24\n\nLet me add them step by step.\n\nStart with 70,000 + 7,000 = 77,000\n\n77,000 + 2,800 = 79,800\n\n79,800 + 8,000 = 87,800\n\n87,800 + 800 = 88,600\n\n88,600 + 320 = 88,920\n\n88,920 + 600 = 89,520\n\n89,520 + 60 = 89,580\n\n89,580 + 24 = 89,604\n\nWait, so the total is 89,604? Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake in addition.\n\nStarting over:\n\n70,000 + 7,000 = 77,000\n\n77,000 + 2,800 = 79,800\n\n79,800 + 8,000 = 87,800\n\n87,800 + 800 = 88,600\n\n88,600 + 320 = 88,920\n\n88,920 + 600 = 89,520\n\n89,520 + 60 = 89,580\n\n89,580 + 24 = 89,604\n\nYes, that seems right. But maybe there's a quicker way to do this without breaking it down into so many parts. Let me try another method to verify.\n\nAlternatively, I can use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's do that.\n\nMultiply 786 by 114.\n\nFirst, write it down:\n\n\u202f\xa0\xa0786\n×\xa0\xa0114\n--------\n\nStart with the units digit of 114, which is 4. Multiply 786 by 4:\n\n786 × 4 = 3,144\n\nNext, the tens digit of 114 is 1. Multiply 786 by 1 and add the result to the previous partial product, shifted one place to the left:\n\n3,144\n+\xa0\xa0786\n------\n3,929\n\nThen, the hundreds digit of 114 is 1. Multiply 786 by 1 and add the result to the previous partial product, shifted two places to the left:\n\n3,929\n+\xa0\xa07,860\n--------\n11,789\n\nWait, hold on. Let me check that again. When multiplying by the hundreds digit, which is 1, we need to shift the previous partial product (3,929) two places to the left, so it becomes 39,290. Then add that to 3,929:\n\n3,929\n+\xa0\xa039,290\n--------\n43,219\n\nWait, that doesn't match the previous result. Hmm, maybe I messed up the shifting.\n\nWait, no. Let me clarify. When multiplying by the hundreds digit (1), the partial product is 786 × 1 = 786. But since we're multiplying by the hundreds place, we need to shift the partial product two places to the left (because 100 is 10^2). So, 786 shifted two places left is 78600. Then add that to the previous partial product 3,929:\n\n3,929\n+\xa0\xa078,600\n--------\n82,529\n\nWait, that's different. Hmm, maybe I need to track each step carefully.\n\nLet me write it out step by step:\n\nFirst partial product (units place 4):\n\n786 × 4 = 3,144\n\nSecond partial product (tens place 1), shifted one place left:\n\n786 × 1 = 786\n\nAdding to the first partial product: 3,144 + 786 = 3,929\n\nThird partial product (hundreds place 1), shifted two places left:\n\n786 × 1 = 786\n\nAdding to the second partial product: 3,929 + 7860 = 8,809\n\nWait, but that's not matching the previous total. Wait, maybe I need to shift the third partial product two places left, so 786 × 1 = 786, shifted two places left is 78,600. Then adding to the second partial product 3,929:\n\n3,929 + 78,600 = 82,529\n\nBut then the total product is 82,529? But that contradicts the previous result of 89,604. So clearly, I made a mistake in one of the methods. Let me check both again.\n\nFirst method: breaking into parts.\n\n786 = 700 + 80 + 6\n\n114 = 100 + 10 + 4\n\nMultiplying each part:\n\n700 × 100 = 70,000\n\n700 × 10 = 7,000\n\n700 × 4 = 2,800\n\n80 × 100 = 8,000\n\n80 × 10 = 800\n\n80 × 4 = 320\n\n6 × 100 = 600\n\n6 × 10 = 60\n\n6 × 4 = 24\n\nAdding all these:\n\n70,000 + 7,000 = 77,000\n\n77,000 + 2,800 = 79,800\n\n79,800 + 8,000 = 87,800\n\n87,800 + 800 = 88,600\n\n88,600 + 320 = 88,920\n\n88,920 + 600 = 89,520\n\n89,520 + 60 = 89,580\n\n89,580 + 24 = 89,604\n\nSo that's 89,604.\n\nSecond method: standard multiplication.\n\nFirst partial product (units digit 4):\n\n786 × 4 = 3,144\n\nSecond partial product (tens digit 1), shifted one place left:\n\n786 × 1 = 786\n\nTotal so far: 3,144 + 786 = 3,929\n\nThird partial product (hundreds digit 1), shifted two places left:\n\n786 × 1 = 786\n\nTotal so far: 3,929 + 7860 = 8,809\n\nWait, but that's only two partial products. Wait, no. Wait, when multiplying by a three-digit number, you have three partial products. Let me correct that.\n\nWait, 114 is 1 hundred, 1 ten, and 4 units. So when multiplying by 114, you have three partial products:\n\n1. Multiply by the units digit (4): 786 × 4 = 3,144\n\n2. Multiply by the tens digit (1), shifted one place left: 786 × 1 = 786\n\n3. Multiply by the hundreds digit (1), shifted two places left: 786 × 1 = 786, shifted two places left becomes 786 × 100 = 78,600\n\nThen, add all three partial products:\n\n3,144 + 786 + 78,600\n\nLet's compute that:\n\n3,144 + 786 = 3,929\n\n3,929 + 78,600 = 82,529\n\nBut this contradicts the first method. So which one is correct?\n\nWait, there must be a mistake here. Let me check the second method again.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the shifting. Let me recall: when you multiply by the hundreds digit, you shift the partial product two places to the left because it's 100, which is 10^2. So, for example, if you have 786 × 1 (tens place), it's 786, and for the hundreds place, it's 786 × 100, which is 78600. So when you add them, you have to align the digits properly.\n\nSo, the first partial product is 3,144 (units place).\n\nSecond partial product is 786 (tens place), which is 786.\n\nThird partial product is 78,600 (hundreds place), which is 78600.\n\nAdding them together:\n\n3,144\n\n+\xa0\xa0786\n\n+\xa0\xa078,600\n\nFirst, 3,144 + 786 = 3,929\n\nThen, 3,929 + 78,600 = 82,529\n\nBut according to the first method, the answer is 89,604. These two results are different. Therefore, one of the methods is wrong. Let me check the first method again.\n\nFirst method: breaking into parts.\n\n786 = 700 + 80 + 6\n\n114 = 100 + 10 + 4\n\nMultiplying each part:\n\n700 × 100 = 70,000\n\n700 × 10 = 7,000\n\n700 × 4 = 2,800\n\n80 × 100 = 8,000\n\n80 × 10 = 800\n\n80 × 4 = 320\n\n6 × 100 = 600\n\n6 × 10 = 60\n\n6 × 4 = 24\n\nAdding all these:\n\n70,000 + 7,000 = 77,000\n\n77,000 + 2,800 = 79,800\n\n79,800 + 8,000 = 87,800\n\n87,800 + 800 = 88,600\n\n88,600 + 320 = 88,920\n\n88,920 + 600 = 89,520\n\n89,520 + 60 = 89,580\n\n89,580 + 24 = 89,604\n\nSo that's 89,604.\n\nBut the standard multiplication gave 82,529. There's a discrepancy here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the standard multiplication. Let me check again.\n\nLet me write out the multiplication step by step:\n\n\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0786\n\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0×\xa0\xa0114\n\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0----------\n\nFirst, multiply 786 by 4 (units place):\n\n786 × 4:\n\n700 × 4 = 2,800\n\n80 × 4 = 320\n\n6 × 4 = 24\n\nAdding those: 2,800 + 320 = 3,120; 3,120 + 24 = 3,144. Correct.\n\nNext, multiply 786 by 1 (tens place), shifted one position to the left:\n\n786 × 1 = 786\n\nBut since it's the tens place, we shift it one position left: 786 × 10 = 7,860\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, when you multiply by the tens digit (1), you have to shift the partial product one place to the left. So 786 × 1 = 786, then shift left by one: 7860. Then add that to the previous partial product.\n\nWait, but in the first step, we had 3,144. Then adding 786 shifted left by one: 7860. So 3,144 + 7,860 = 11,004\n\nThen, multiply 786 by 1 (hundreds place), shifted two places to the left:\n\n786 × 1 = 786\n\nShifted two places left: 786 × 100 = 78,600\n\nNow, add all three partial products:\n\n3,144 (units)\n\n+\xa0\xa07,860 (tens shifted)\n\n+\xa0\xa078,600 (hundreds shifted)\n\nFirst, 3,144 + 7,860 = 11,004\n\nThen, 11,004 + 78,600 = 89,604\n\nAh! There we go. So I made a mistake in the shifting earlier. When multiplying by the tens digit, we shift the partial product one place left, so 786 × 1 = 786 becomes 7,860. Then adding to the first partial product (3,144) gives 11,004. Then adding the hundreds shifted partial product (78,600) gives 89,604. So that matches the first method. So the correct answer is 89,604.\n\nTherefore, the product of 786 and 114 is 89,604.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 786 and 114 is \\boxed{89604}.\n"] 32%|███▏      | 162/500 [2:44:01<2:44:44, 29.24s/it]
