      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 273 and 270. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers can be done by breaking them down into smaller parts. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. Let me recall: the distributive property is a(b + c) = ab + ac. So, if I can express 273 and 270 in terms of numbers that add up to something, maybe I can make the multiplication easier.\n\nLooking at 273 and 270, I notice that 270 is 3 less than 273. So, 273 = 270 + 3. That might be helpful. Let me write that down:\n\n273 \u00d7 270 = (270 + 3) \u00d7 270\n\nNow, applying the distributive property:\n\n= 270 \u00d7 270 + 3 \u00d7 270\n\nOkay, so now I need to calculate 270 \u00d7 270 and 3 \u00d7 270 separately, then add them together.\n\nFirst, let's compute 270 \u00d7 270. Hmm, 270 times 270. I know that multiplying by 270 is the same as multiplying by 27 and then by 10. So, 27 \u00d7 10 = 270. Therefore, 270 \u00d7 270 = 27 \u00d7 27 \u00d7 10. Let me compute 27 \u00d7 27 first.\n\n27 \u00d7 27. Let me do that step by step. 20 \u00d7 27 = 540, and 7 \u00d7 27 = 189. Adding those together: 540 + 189 = 729. So, 27 \u00d7 27 = 729. Therefore, 270 \u00d7 270 = 729 \u00d7 10 = 7290.\n\nAlright, so the first part is 7290. Now, the second part is 3 \u00d7 270. That's straightforward. 3 \u00d7 270 = 810.\n\nNow, adding those two results together: 7290 + 810. Let me add them. 7290 + 800 = 8090, and then +10 more is 8100. So, 7290 + 810 = 8100.\n\nWait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. 7290 + 810. Breaking it down: 7000 + 800 = 7800, and 290 + 10 = 300. Then 7800 + 300 = 8100. Yep, that checks out.\n\nSo, putting it all together, 273 \u00d7 270 = 8100. Let me verify this another way to be sure. Maybe using another method for multiplication.\n\nAlternatively, I can think of 273 as 270 + 3 again, but maybe use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's try that.\n\nMultiplying 273 by 270. Let's ignore the zeros first. 273 \u00d7 27. Then, since 270 is 27 \u00d7 10, the result will be (273 \u00d7 27) \u00d7 10. So, first compute 273 \u00d7 27.\n\nLet's compute 273 \u00d7 27. Breaking it down:\n\n273 \u00d7 20 = 5460\n\n273 \u00d7 7 = 1911\n\nAdding those together: 5460 + 1911. Let's compute that. 5460 + 1000 = 6460, 6460 + 900 = 7360, 7360 + 11 = 7371. So, 273 \u00d7 27 = 7371. Then, multiplying by 10 gives 7371 \u00d7 10 = 73710. Wait, but that's different from the previous result of 8100. Hmm, that can't be right. There must be a mistake here.\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, 273 \u00d7 270 is the same as 273 \u00d7 (27 \u00d7 10) = (273 \u00d7 27) \u00d7 10. So, if 273 \u00d7 27 is 7371, then 7371 \u00d7 10 is 73710. But earlier, using the distributive property, I got 8100. These two results are conflicting. That means I made a mistake somewhere. Let me check both calculations again.\n\nFirst method: 273 \u00d7 270 = (270 + 3) \u00d7 270 = 270\u00b2 + 3\u00d7270 = 7290 + 810 = 8100.\n\nSecond method: 273 \u00d7 270 = 273 \u00d7 27 \u00d7 10 = 7371 \u00d7 10 = 73710.\n\nThese two results are different. That's a problem. So, one of the methods is wrong. Let me check the second method again.\n\nWait, 273 \u00d7 27. Let's compute 273 \u00d7 27 step by step.\n\nFirst, 273 \u00d7 20 = 5460.\n\nThen, 273 \u00d7 7. Let's compute 273 \u00d7 7:\n\n7 \u00d7 200 = 1400\n\n7 \u00d7 70 = 490\n\n7 \u00d7 3 = 21\n\nAdding those: 1400 + 490 = 1890, 1890 + 21 = 1911. So, 273 \u00d7 7 = 1911. Then, 273 \u00d7 27 = 5460 + 1911. Let's add those again.\n\n5460 + 1911. Let's break it down:\n\n5460 + 1000 = 6460\n\n6460 + 900 = 7360\n\n7360 + 11 = 7371. So, that's correct. Then 7371 \u00d7 10 = 73710. But according to the first method, it's 8100. So, which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the first method. Let me check that again.\n\nFirst method: 273 \u00d7 270 = (270 + 3) \u00d7 270 = 270\u00b2 + 3\u00d7270.\n\n270 squared: 270 \u00d7 270. Let's compute that again.\n\n270 \u00d7 270. Let's do it the standard way.\n\n270\n\n\u00d7270\n\n--------\n\nFirst, multiply 270 by 0: 0\n\nThen, multiply 270 by 7: 1890\n\nThen, multiply 270 by 2: 5400\n\nAdding them up: 0 + 1890 + 5400 = 7290. So, 270 \u00d7 270 = 7290. Correct.\n\nThen, 3 \u00d7 270 = 810. So, 7290 + 810 = 8100. So, that's correct.\n\nBut why is the second method giving 73710? That's way off. So, there must be a mistake in the second method. Wait, 273 \u00d7 270. Let me compute it directly.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can compute 273 \u00d7 270 by expanding:\n\n273 \u00d7 270 = 273 \u00d7 (200 + 70) = 273 \u00d7 200 + 273 \u00d7 70.\n\nCompute 273 \u00d7 200: 273 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 100 = 546 \u00d7 100 = 54600.\n\nCompute 273 \u00d7 70: 273 \u00d7 7 \u00d7 10. 273 \u00d7 7 = 1911, so 1911 \u00d7 10 = 19110.\n\nAdding 54600 + 19110: 54600 + 19110. Let's compute that.\n\n54600 + 10000 = 64600\n\n64600 + 9000 = 73600\n\n73600 + 110 = 73710. So, same result as before. So, 273 \u00d7 270 = 73710. But according to the first method, it's 8100. That's a problem. So, which one is correct?\n\nWait, this is confusing. How can two different methods give different results? There must be a miscalculation somewhere. Let me check with another method.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the standard multiplication algorithm.\n\nLet's write 273 \u00d7 270.\n\nFirst, write 270 \u00d7 273. Let's ignore the zeros for a moment.\n\nMultiplying 27 by 273:\n\n27\n\n\u00d7273\n\n--------\n\nFirst, multiply 27 by 3: 81\n\nThen, multiply 27 by 7: 189\n\nThen, multiply 27 by 2: 54\n\nAdding them up: 81 + 189 = 270, 270 + 54 = 324. So, 27 \u00d7 273 = 7371. Then, since we ignored a zero, the actual multiplication is 7371 \u00d7 10 = 73710. So, same result as before.\n\nBut according to the first method, it's 8100. So, which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the first method. Let me check again.\n\nFirst method: 273 \u00d7 270 = (270 + 3) \u00d7 270 = 270\u00b2 + 3\u00d7270.\n\n270 squared is 7290. 3\u00d7270 is 810. 7290 + 810 =