      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 503 and 762. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two three-digit numbers can be a bit tricky, but maybe there's a way to break it down step by step. Let me start by recalling the standard multiplication algorithm. \n\nFirst, I'll write 503 multiplied by 762. Let me set it up like this:\n\n\u202f\u00a0\u00a0503\n\u00d7\u00a0\u00a0762\n--------\n\nStarting with the rightmost digit of the second number, which is 2. I need to multiply 503 by 2. That should be straightforward. 503 \u00d7 2. Let me calculate that. 500 \u00d7 2 is 1000, and 3 \u00d7 2 is 6, so adding those together gives 1006. So the first partial product is 1006.\n\nNext, I move to the middle digit of 762, which is 6. Now, I need to multiply 503 by 6, and then add that to the previous partial product. Let's do 503 \u00d7 6. Breaking it down: 500 \u00d7 6 = 3000, and 3 \u00d7 6 = 18. Adding those gives 3000 + 18 = 3018. Now, adding this to the first partial product: 1006 + 3018. Let me add those. 1000 + 3000 is 4000, and 6 + 18 is 24, so total is 4024. So the second partial product is 4024.\n\nNow, the leftmost digit of 762 is 7. Multiply 503 by 7 and add that to the previous partial product. Calculating 503 \u00d7 7. Let's see, 500 \u00d7 7 = 3500, and 3 \u00d7 7 = 21. Adding those together gives 3500 + 21 = 3521. Now, adding this to 4024: 4024 + 3521. Let me break it down. 4000 + 3500 = 7500, and 24 + 21 = 45. So total is 7500 + 45 = 7545. \n\nWait, let me check that addition again. 4024 + 3521. Maybe another way: 4024 + 3000 = 7024, then add 521 more. 7024 + 500 = 7524, then +21 = 7545. Yeah, that's correct. So the final partial product is 7545.\n\nNow, adding all the partial products together: 1006 (from the first step) + 4024 (second) + 7545 (third). Let's add them step by step. 1006 + 4024. 1000 + 4000 = 5000, 6 + 24 = 30, so total is 5030. Then add 7545 to 5030. 5000 + 7000 = 12000, 30 + 545 = 575. So total is 12000 + 575 = 12575. \n\nWait, let me verify that addition again. 5030 + 7545. Breaking it down: 5000 + 7000 = 12000, 30 + 545 = 575. So 12000 + 575 = 12575. That seems right. \n\nBut let me cross-check using another method to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Maybe using the distributive property. Let's see. 503 \u00d7 762 can be broken down into (500 + 3) \u00d7 (700 + 60 + 2). Let's compute each part:\n\nFirst, 500 \u00d7 700 = 350,000\n\nThen, 500 \u00d7 60 = 30,000\n\nThen, 500 \u00d7 2 = 1,000\n\nNext, 3 \u00d7 700 = 2,100\n\n3 \u00d7 60 = 180\n\n3 \u00d7 2 = 6\n\nNow, add all these together:\n\n350,000 + 30,000 = 380,000\n\n380,000 + 1,000 = 381,000\n\n381,000 + 2,100 = 383,100\n\n383,100 + 180 = 383,280\n\n383,280 + 6 = 383,286\n\nWait, that's different from the previous result of 12,575. Hmm, that can't be right. There must be a mistake here. Let me check my breakdown again.\n\nWait, hold on. The original problem is 503 \u00d7 762. If I break it down as (500 + 3) \u00d7 (700 + 60 + 2), that's correct. But when I distribute, I need to make sure that each term is multiplied by each part. Let me redo the distribution step by step.\n\nFirst, 500 \u00d7 700 = 350,000\n\n500 \u00d7 60 = 30,000\n\n500 \u00d7 2 = 1,000\n\nThen, 3 \u00d7 700 = 2,100\n\n3 \u00d7 60 = 180\n\n3 \u00d7 2 = 6\n\nAdding all these:\n\n350,000 + 30,000 = 380,000\n\n380,000 + 1,000 = 381,000\n\n381,000 + 2,100 = 383,100\n\n383,100 + 180 = 383,280\n\n383,280 + 6 = 383,286\n\nBut when I added the partial products earlier, I got 12,575. That's way off. There's a discrepancy here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the addition when I added the partial products. Let me check that again. The partial products were 1006, 4024, and 7545. Let's add them step by step.\n\nFirst, 1006 + 4024. Let's compute 1000 + 4000 = 5000, 6 + 24 = 30. So 5000 + 30 = 5030. Then, 5030 + 7545. Let's do 5030 + 7000 = 12030, then 12030 + 545 = 12575. So that's 12,575. But according to the distribution method, it's 383,286. That's a huge difference. Clearly, one of the methods is wrong. \n\nWait, but 503 \u00d7 762. Let me compute it directly using another method. Let's use the standard multiplication algorithm again, but more carefully.\n\nMultiplying 503 by 762:\n\nFirst, write 503 \u00d7 2 = 1006\n\nThen, 503 \u00d7 60 = 30,180 (since 503 \u00d7 6 = 3018, then add a zero)\n\nThen, 503 \u00d7 700 = 352,100 (since 503 \u00d7 7 = 3521, then add two zeros)\n\nNow, add these three partial products:\n\n1006 + 30,180 + 352,100\n\nLet's add 1006 + 30,180 first. 1000 + 30,000 = 31,000; 6 + 180 = 186. So 31,000 + 186 = 31,186.\n\nThen, add 31,186 + 352,100. 30,000 + 350,000 = 380,000; 1,186 + 2,100 = 3,286. So total is 380,000 + 3,286 = 383,286. \n\nAh! So here, when I broke it down into 2, 60, 700, then multiplied and added, I got 383,286. But when I did the partial products by writing each digit separately, I got 12,575. That's a problem. So clearly, the second method was wrong. \n\nWait, why is that? Let me check the first method again. When I did the partial products by multiplying each digit separately, I got 1006, 4024, 7545. Adding those gives 12,575. But when I broke it down into 2, 60, 700, I got 383,286. These two results are vastly different. That means I made a mistake in one of the methods. \n\nWait, 503 \u00d7 762. Let me compute it directly using another approach. Let's use the standard multiplication:\n\n\u202f\u00a0\u00a0503\n\u00d7\u00a0\u00a0762\n--------\n\u00a0\u00a01006\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0(503 \u00d7 2)\n\u00a0\u00a030180\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0(503 \u00d7 60)\n\u00a0\u00a0352100\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0(503 \u00d7 700)\n--------\n\u00a0\u00a0383286\n\nYes, that's correct. So the correct product is 383,286. Therefore, my first method of breaking it into partial products was wrong. Let me figure out why.\n\nWait, when I did the partial products by multiplying each digit separately, I think I messed up the place values. Let me explain. When you multiply by the units digit (2), you get 1006, which is correct. Then, multiplying by the tens digit (6), you have to remember that it's actually 60, so you need to shift the partial product one place to the left, making it 40240. Then, multiplying by the hundreds digit (7), which is 700, you have to shift it two places to the left, making it 754500. Then, adding them all together:\n\n1006 + 40240 + 754500\n\nLet's compute that step by step.\n\nFirst, 1006 + 40240. 1000 + 40000 = 41000; 6 + 240 = 246. So 41000 + 246 = 41246.\n\nThen, 41246 + 754500. 40000 + 750000 = 790000; 1246 + 500 = 1746. So 790000 + 1746 = 791,746. Wait, that's not matching either. Hmm, this is confusing. \n\nWait, maybe I need to align the partial products properly. Let me write them down with the correct shifts:\n\nFirst partial product (units place): 1006\n\nSecond partial product (tens place shifted left by one): 40240\n\nThird partial product (hundreds place shifted left by two): 754500\n\nNow, adding them:\n\n1006\n\n+40240\n\n= 41246\n\nThen, 41246 + 754500 = 795,746\n\nWait, that's 795,746. But according to the other method, it's 383,286. That's still different. What's going on here?\n\nWait, I think I messed up the shifts. Let me recall: when you multiply by a digit in a different place, you have to shift the partial product accordingly. So when you multiply by 2 (units place), you don't shift. When you multiply by 6 (tens place), you shift left by one. When you multiply by 7 (hundreds place), you shift left by two. So the partial products should be:\n\n1006 (units)\n\n40240 (tens place shifted left by one)\n\n754500 (hundreds place shifted left by two)\n\nNow, adding them:\n\nFirst, 1006 + 40240. Let's compute 1006 + 40240. 1000 + 40000 = 41000; 6 + 240 = 246. So 41000 + 246 = 41246.\n\nThen, 41246 + 754500. Let's compute 41246 + 754500. 40000 + 750000 = 790000; 1246 + 500 = 1746. So 790000 + 1746 = 791,746. \n\nBut according to the other method, it's 383,286. There's a conflict here. Which one is correct? Let me compute 503 \u00d7 762 directly using another method. Let's use the standard multiplication:\n\nMultiply 503 by 762:\n\nFirst, multiply 503 by 2: 1006\n\nThen, multiply 503 by 60: 503 \u00d7 6 = 3018, so 3018 \u00d7 10 = 30,180\n\nThen, multiply 503 by 700: 503 \u00d7 7 = 3521, so 3521 \u00d7 100 = 352,100\n\nNow, add them up: 1006 + 30,180 + 352,100\n\n1006 + 30,180 = 31,186\n\n31,186 + 352,100 = 383,286\n\nYes, that's correct. So the correct product is 383,286. Therefore, my first method of breaking into partial products was wrong. Let me figure out why.\n\nWait, when I did the partial products by multiplying each digit separately, I think I forgot to shift the partial products properly. Let me try again:\n\nFirst, multiply 503 by 2: 1006 (no shift)\n\nSecond, multiply 503 by 6: 3018. Since this is the tens place, shift left by one: 30180\n\nThird, multiply 503 by 7: 3521. Since this is the hundreds place, shift left by two: 352100\n\nNow, adding them:\n\n1006\n\n+30180\n\n= 31186\n\n31186 + 352100 = 383,286\n\nAh! There we go. So when I did the first time, I added 1006 + 40240 + 754500, but I think I made a mistake in the second partial product. Wait, 503 \u00d7 6 is 3018, which should be shifted left by one, making it 30180. Then, 503 \u00d7 7 is 3521, shifted left by two, making it 352100. So the partial products are 1006, 30180, 352100. Adding them gives 1006 + 30180 = 31186, then 31186 + 352100 = 383,286. \n\nEarlier, when I did the first partial products, I wrote 503 \u00d7 6 as 3018 and added it as 30180, but when I did the first partial product, I wrote 503 \u00d7 6 as 3018 and added it as 30180. Wait, but in the first method, I wrote:\n\nFirst partial product: 503 \u00d7 2 = 1006\n\nSecond partial product: 503 \u00d7 6 = 3018, then added to 1006, resulting in 4024\n\nThird partial product: 503 \u00d7 7 = 3521, added to 4024, resulting in 7545\n\nBut 3018 shifted left by one is 30180, not 3018. So when I added 1006 + 30180, that's 31186, not 4024. Then adding 31186 + 352100 gives 383,286. So my first time, I incorrectly shifted the second partial product by one place but added it as if it wasn't shifted. That's where the mistake was. \n\nTherefore, the correct product is 383,286. So the initial method where I broke it down into 2, 60, 700 and multiplied and added gave the correct result, and the first method where I multiplied each digit separately but forgot to shift the partial products properly was incorrect. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 383,286. Let me just verify once more using another approach. Let's use the formula (a + b)(c + d) = ac + ad + bc + bd, where a=500, b=3, c=700, d=60. \n\nSo:\n\n500 \u00d7 700 = 350,000\n\n500 \u00d7 60 = 30,000\n\n3 \u00d7 700 = 2,100\n\n3 \u00d7 60 = 180\n\nAdding them up: 350,000 + 30,000 = 380,000; 2,100 + 180 = 2,280; 380,000 + 2,280 = 382,280. Wait, that's different from 383,286. Hmm, that's not matching. What's wrong here?\n\nWait, no. Wait, the formula is (a + b)(c + d) = ac + ad + bc + bd. But in this case, a=500, b=3, c=700, d=60. So:\n\nac = 500 \u00d7 700 = 350,000\n\nad = 500 \u00d7 60 = 30,000\n\nbc = 3 \u00d7 700 = 2,100\n\nbd = 3 \u00d7 60 = 180\n\nAdding them: 350,000 + 30,000 = 380,000; 2,100 + 180 = 2,280; total is 380,000 + 2,280 = 382,280. But according to the other methods, it's 383,286. There's a discrepancy here. So why is this happening?\n\nWait, maybe the formula is not applicable here. Wait, the formula (a + b)(c + d) is correct, but in this case, a=500, b=3, c=700, d=60. So the formula gives 382,280, but the actual product is 383,286. So there's a difference of 1,006. Wait, 383,286 - 382,280 = 1,006. Which is exactly 503 \u00d7 2. So that suggests that the formula is missing something. Wait, no. Wait, the formula is (500 + 3)(700 + 60) = 500\u00d7700 + 500\u00d760 + 3\u00d7700 + 3\u00d760. Which is exactly what I did. So why is there a discrepancy?\n\nWait, no. Wait, 500 \u00d7 700 = 350,000\n\n500 \u00d7