er methods, the correct answer is 146,115. So, clearly, the standard multiplication was done incorrectly. Therefore, my initial approach was wrong.\n\nWait, so why is there a discrepancy? Let me check the partial products again.\n\nFirst partial product: 255 x 3 = 765. Correct.\n\nSecond partial product: 255 x 7. Let's compute 255 x 7:\n\n200 x 7 = 1400\n\n50 x 7 = 350\n\n5 x 7 = 35\n\n1400 + 350 = 1750 + 35 = 1785. Then add the carryover 7: 1785 + 7 = 1792. So, 1792. Correct.\n\nThird partial product: 255 x 5. 200 x 5 = 1000, 50 x 5 = 250, 5 x 5 = 25. 1000 + 250 = 1250 + 25 = 1275. Then add the carryover 17: 1275 + 17 = 1292. Correct.\n\nSo, the partial products are 765, 1792, 1292. Adding them up:\n\n765 + 1792 = 2557\n\n2557 + 1292 = 3849\n\nBut according to the other methods, the correct answer is 146,115. So, there's a problem here. Therefore, my initial approach must have a mistake. Wait, but how?\n\nWait, perhaps I misaligned the digits. Let me check the original multiplication setup.\n\nWait, when multiplying 255 by 573, the rightmost digit is 3, then 7, then 5. So, the partial products should be:\n\nFirst partial product: 255 x 3 = 765 (units place)\n\nSecond partial product: 255 x 7 = 1785 (tens place, shifted one position to the left)\n\nThird partial product: 255 x 5 = 1275 (hundreds place, shifted two positions to the left)\n\nSo, when adding them:\n\n765 (units)\n\n1785 (tens)\n\n12750 (hundreds)\n\nWait, but in the standard multiplication, we write the partial products one under the other, shifted appropriately. So, the first partial product is 765, the second is 1792 shifted one position left (i.e., 17920), and the third is 1292 shifted two positions left (i.e., 129200). Then adding them:\n\n765\n\n17920\n\n129200\n\nAdding these:\n\n765 + 17920 = 18685\n\n18685 + 129200 = 147,885\n\nWait, that's 147,885. But according to the other methods, it's 146,115. Hmm, still not matching. Wait, maybe I need to check the addition again.\n\nWait, 765 + 17920:\n\