ication must have an error.\n\nTherefore, the mistake must be in the standard multiplication steps. Let me check again.\n\nWait, when I did the standard multiplication, I got 153,120, but using the algebraic identity, it's 904,800. So clearly, the standard multiplication was done incorrectly.\n\nWait, let me try the standard multiplication again, but more carefully.\n\nMultiplying 928 by 975:\n\nFirst, write 928 x 5:\n\n5\nx928\n------\n4,640\n\nThen, multiply 928 by 7, shifted left by one:\n\n7\nx928\n------\n6,496\nShift left: 64,960\n\nThen, multiply 928 by 9, shifted left by two:\n\n9\nx928\n------\n8,352\nShift left: 83,520\n\nNow, adding these three partial products:\n\n4,640 (units)\n+64,960 (tens)\n+83,520 (hundreds)\n= 4,640 + 64,960 = 69,600; 69,600 + 83,520 = 153,120\n\nBut according to the algebraic method, it's 904,800. So there's a discrepancy. Therefore, the standard multiplication must have been performed incorrectly.\n\nWait, but how? Let me check the alignment again. When you shift left by two positions, the partial product 83,520 is added in the hundreds place. So when adding, the first partial product is 4,640 (units), second is 64,960 (tens), third is 83,520 (hundreds). So adding them:\n\n4,640 + 64,960 = 69,600\n\n69,600 + 83,520 = 153,120\n\nBut according to the algebraic method, it's 904,800. So there's a problem here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the algebraic method. Let me check that again.\n\nUsing the algebraic identity:\n\n928 * 975 = (900 + 28)(1000 - 25) = 900*1000 - 900*25 + 28*1000 - 28*25\n\nCompute each term:\n\n900*1000 = 900,000\n\n900*25 = 22,500\n\n28*1000 = 28,000\n\n28*25 = 700\n\nSo:\n\n900,000 - 22,500 + 28,000 - 700\n\nFirst, 900,000 - 22,500 = 877,500\n\n877,500 + 28,000 = 905,500\n\n905,500 - 700 = 904,800\n\nYes, that's correct. So the algebraic method gives 904,800, which matches the distributive method. Therefore, the standard multiplication must have been performed incorrectly.\n\nBut why