124. So 170,800 + 5,124 = 175,924. But earlier, when I added the partial products, I got 173,924. There's a discrepancy here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, this is confusing. Let me check both methods again.\n\nFirst method: multiplying each partial product and adding:\n\n854 (from 2) + 4270 (from 1) + 168800 (from 4) = 854 + 4270 = 5124; 5124 + 168800 = 173,924.\n\nSecond method: breaking into 427 x 400 + 427 x 12 = 170,800 + 5,124 = 175,924.\n\nThese two results are different. That means I made a mistake somewhere. Let me check the second method again.\n\nWait, 427 x 400: 427 x 4 is 1,708, so 1,708 x 100 is 170,800. That's correct.\n\n427 x 12: 427 x 10 is 4,270; 427 x 2 is 854. 4,270 + 854 = 5,124. That's correct. So 170,800 + 5,124 = 175,924. But according to the first method, it's 173,924. So which one is right?\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the partial products. Let me check the first method again.\n\nFirst partial product: 427 x 2 = 854. Correct.\n\nSecond partial product: 427 x 1 (tens place), which is 427, but since it's the tens place, we add a zero: 4270. Correct.\n\nThird partial product: 427 x 4 (hundreds place), which is 1688, but since it's the hundreds place, we add two zeros: 168800. Correct.\n\nAdding them: 854 + 4270 + 168800.\n\n854 + 4270: Let's compute 854 + 4270. 800 + 4200 = 5000; 54 + 70 = 124. So 5000 + 124 = 5124. Correct.\n\n5124 + 168800: 5124 + 168800. Let's add 168800 + 5000 = 173800; then 173800 + 124 = 173924. So that's 173,924.\n\nBut the other method gives 175,924. So there's a conflict here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the second method. Let me check 427 x 412 again.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another approach. Let's compute 427 x 412 using the standard multiplication step by step.\n\nFirst, write 427 x 412:\n\n      427\n    x 412\n    -------\n\nMultiply 427 by 2: 854\n\nMultiply 427 by 1 (tens place): 4270\n\nMultiply 427 by 4 (hundreds place): 168800\n\nNow, adding them:\n\n854\n+4270\n------\n5124\n\n5124\n+168800\n-------\n173,924\n\nYes, that's the same as before. So why does the other method give a different result?\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the second method. Let me check 427 x 12 again.\n\n427 x 12: 427 x 10 = 4,270; 427 x 2 = 854. 4,270 + 854 = 5,124. That's correct.\n\n427 x 400: 427 x 4 = 1,708; 1,708 x 100 = 170,800. Correct.\n\n170,800 + 5,124 = 175,924. But according to the first method, it's 173,924. So there's a discrepancy of 2,000. Hmm. That's a significant difference. So which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the first method. Let me check the addition again.\n\nFirst partial products: 854, 4270, 168800.\n\nAdding 854 + 4270:\n\n854 + 4270. Let's do this step by step.\n\n854 + 4000 = 4854\n\n4854 + 270 = 5124. Correct.\n\nThen 5124 + 168800:\n\n5124 + 168800. Let's add 168800 + 5000 = 173800; then 173800 + 124 = 173924. Correct.\n\nBut according to the other method, it's 175,924. So which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the second method. Let me check 427 x 412 again.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another multiplication method. Let's try multiplying 427 by 412 using the lattice method.\n\nBut that might be time-consuming. Alternatively, use a calculator-like approach.\n\nAlternatively, let's compute 427 x 412 using the standard multiplication:\n\n      427\n    x 412\n    -------\n\nMultiply 427 by 2: 854\n\nMultiply 427 by 1 (tens place): 4270\n\nMultiply 427 by 4 (hundreds place): 168800\n\nNow, adding them:\n\n854\n+4270\n------\n5124\n\n5124\n+168800\n-------\n173,924\n\nYes, same result. So why does the other method give a different answer? Wait, maybe I made a mistake in the second method. Let me check 427 x 412 again.\n\nWait, 427 x 412. Let's compute it as (400 + 27) x (400 + 12). Wait, that's not helpful. Alternatively, use the distributive property:\n\n427 x 412 = 427 x (400 + 12) = 427 x 400 + 427 x 12 = 170,800 + 5,124 = 175,924. But according to the first method, it's 173,924. 