output:  ['\n\nTo find the product of 699 and 875, we can use the distributive property and step-by-step multiplication:\n\n1. **Break down 875 into 800 and 75:**\n   \\[\n   699 \\times 875 = 699 \\times (800 + 75)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate each part separately:**\n   - \\(699 \\times 800\\):\n     \\[\n     699 \\times 800 = 699 \\times 8 \\times 100 = 5592 \\times 100 = 559200\n     \\]\n   - \\(699 \\times 75\\):\n     \\[\n     699 \\times 75 = 699 \\times (70 + 5) = 699 \\times 70 + 699 \\times 5\n     \\]\n     - \\(699 \\times 70\\):\n       \\[\n       699 \\times 70 = 699 \\times 7 \\times 10 = 4893 \\times 10 = 48930\n       \\]\n     - \\(699 \\times 5\\):\n       \\[\n       699 \\times 5 = 3495\n       \\]\n     - Add the results:\n       \\[\n       48930 + 3495 = 52425\n       \\]\n\n3. **Sum the results of the two parts:**\n   \\[\n   559200 + 52425 = 611625\n   \\]\n\n4. **Verification using another method:**\n   - Using the difference of squares:\n     \\[\n     699 \\times 875 = 699 \\times (1000 - 125) = 699 \\times 1000 - 699 \\times 125\n     \\]\n     - \\(699 \\times 1000 = 699000\\)\n     - \\(699 \\times 125 = 87375\\)\n     - Subtract:\n       \\[\n       699000 - 87375 = 611625\n       \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 699 and 875 is \\(\\boxed{611625}\\).', "\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 699 and 875. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers can be done in different ways, maybe breaking them down into smaller parts or using some multiplication tricks. Let me think. \n\nFirst, maybe I can just do the standard multiplication method. Let's try that. 699 times 875. Alright, starting from the rightmost digit. So, 699 times 5. Let me calculate that first. 699 * 5. Well, 700 * 5 is 3500, but since it's 699, that's 700 - 1, so 3500 - 5 = 3495. Okay, so the first part is 3495.\n\nNow, moving to the next digit, which is 7. So, 699 * 7. Again, 700 * 7 is 4900, minus 1 * 7 is 7, so 4900 - 7 = 4893. Wait, no, t