her method to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the distributive property. Let's break down 981 into 900 + 80 + 1. Then multiply each part by 444 and add them up.\n\nFirst, 444 x 900. Let's compute that. 444 x 9 = 3996, so 444 x 900 = 399600.\n\nNext, 444 x 80. 444 x 8 = 3552, so 444 x 80 = 35520.\n\nThen, 444 x 1 = 444.\n\nNow, add all three results: 399600 + 35520 + 444.\n\nLet's add 399600 + 35520 first. 399600 + 35520. Let's break it down:\n\n399600 + 30000 = 429600\n\n429600 + 5520 = 435120\n\nThen add 444: 435120 + 444 = 435564.\n\nWait, so according to this method, the product is 435564. But earlier, using the standard multiplication, I got 35944. These two results are different. That means I must have made a mistake somewhere. Let me check both methods again.\n\nFirst, the standard multiplication:\n\n444\nx981\n------\nFirst, multiply 444 by 1: 444\n\nThen multiply 444 by 8: 3552 (shifted left by one)\n\nThen multiply 444 by 9: 3996 (shifted left by two)\n\nWait, hold on! Wait, in the standard multiplication, when you multiply by the hundreds digit, you have to shift the partial product two places to the left because 9 is in the hundreds place. So, the first partial product is 444 (units place), the second is 3552 (tens place), and the third is 39960 (hundreds place). Then adding them up.\n\nWait, so maybe I messed up the shifting in the first method. Let me redo the standard multiplication properly.\n\nSo, step by step:\n\nMultiply 444 by 1: 444\n\nMultiply 444 by 8 (tens place): 3552, shifted one place left: 35520\n\nMultiply 444 by 9 (hundreds place): 3996, shifted two places left: 399600\n\nNow, add them all together:\n\n444\n\n+35520\n\n+399600\n\nLet's add 444 + 35520 first. As before, that's 35964. Then add 399600 to that.\n\n35964 + 399600. Let's compute:\n\n35964 + 399600. Let's break it down:\n\n35964 + 300000 = 335964\n\n335964 + 99600 = 435564\n\nAh! So the correct total is 435564. So my first method was wrong because I didn't shift the second partial product correctly. The second partial product should have been shifted two places left, not one. That's where I made the mistake. So the correct product is 435564.\n\nAlternatively, using the distributive method, I also got 435564, which matches the corrected standard multiplication. So that's reassuring.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using another method, like the difference of squares or something, but that might be more complicated. Alternatively, use a calculator if I have access. But since I don't, let me try another way.\n\nAlternatively, note that 444 x 981. Let's see, 444 is 400 + 40 + 4. So, 400 x 981 = 392400, 40 x 981 = 39240, 4 x 981 = 3924. Then add them up: 392400 + 39240 = 431640; 431640 + 3924 = 435564. Yep, same result. So that's correct.\n\nTherefore, the product of 444 and 981 is 435,564.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 444 and 981 is \\boxed{435564}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 444 and 981, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Multiply 444 by the rightmost digit (1):\n   \\[\n   444 \\times 1 = 444\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 444 by the next digit (8), shifting the result one place to the left:\n   \\[\n   444 \\times 8 = 3552 \\quad \\text{(shifted left by one place: 35520)}\n   \\]\n\n3. Multiply 444 by the next digit (9), shifting the result two places to the left:\n   \\[\n   444 \\times 9 = 3996 \\quad \\text{(shifted left by two places: 399600)}\n   \\]\n\n4. Add the three partial products together:\n   \\[\n   444 + 35520 + 399600\n   \\]\n\n5. Perform the addition step-by-step:\n   - First, add 444 and 35520:\n     \\[\n     444 + 35520 = 35964\n     \\]\n   - Then, add the result to 399600:\n     \\[\n     35964 + 399600 = 435564\n     \\]\n\nThus, the product of 444 and 981 is \\(\\boxed{435564}\\).",
