output:  ['\n\nTo find the product of 356 and 952, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm and verify the result using different methods.\n\nFirst, using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   356 \\\\\n\\times 952 \\\\\n\\hline\n   712 \\quad \\text{(356 \\times 2)} \\\\\n 1780 \\quad \\text{(356 \\times 5, shifted one position to the left)} \\\\\n 32040 \\quad \\text{(356 \\times 9, shifted two positions to the left)} \\\\\n\\hline\n 338912 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAdding the partial products:\n- \\(712 + 1780 = 2492\\)\n- \\(2492 + 32040 = 338912\\)\n\nNext, verifying using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n356 \\times 952 = 356 \\times (900 + 50 + 2) = 356 \\times 900 + 356 \\times 50 + 356 \\times 2\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term:\n- \\(356 \\times 900 = 320400\\)\n- \\(356 \\times 50 = 17800\\)\n- \\(356 \\times 2 = 712\\)\n\nAdding these results:\n- \\(320400 + 17800 = 338200\\)\n- \\(338200 + 712 = 338912\\)\n\nAnother verification using the difference of squares method:\n\\[\n356 \\times 952 = 356 \\times (1000 - 48) = 356000 - 356 \\times 48\n\\]\n\nCalculating \\(356 \\times 48\\):\n- \\(356 \\times 48 = 300 \\times 48 + 50 \\times 48 + 6 \\times 48 = 14400 + 2400 + 288 = 17088\\)\n\nSubtracting:\n\\[\n356000 - 17088 = 338912\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 356 and 952 is \\(\\boxed{338912}\\).', "\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 356 and 952. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers can be done by breaking them down into smaller parts or using some multiplication techniques. Let me think about the best way to approach this.\n\nFirst, maybe I can just use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's try that. So, 356 times 952. Let me write it out vertically to keep track:\n\n   356\nx 952\n-------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit of the second number, which is 2. Multiply 356 by 2. That's 712. Then move to the next digit, which is 5. Multiply 356 by 5. Let's see, 356*5. 300*5 is 1500, 50*5 i