      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 597 and 107. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers can be done by breaking them down into smaller parts or using some multiplication techniques. Let me think about the best way to approach this.\n\nFirst, maybe I can just use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's try that. 597 times 107. Alright, so 107 is a three-digit number, and 597 is a three-digit number as well. Let me write it out vertically to avoid confusion:\n\n\u202f\u00a0\u00a0597\n\u00d7\u00a0\u00a0107\n--------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit of the second number, which is 7. Multiply 597 by 7 first. Let me calculate that:\n\n597 \u00d7 7. Let's break it down:\n7 \u00d7 7 = 49, write down 9 and carry over 4.\n7 \u00d7 9 = 63, plus the carried 4 is 67, write down 7 and carry over 6.\n7 \u00d7 5 = 35, plus the carried 6 is 41. So that gives 4179.\n\nWait, let me check that again. Maybe I should do it step by step:\n\n7 \u00d7 7 = 49. So write 9, carry 4.\n7 \u00d7 9 = 63 + 4 = 67. Write 7, carry 6.\n7 \u00d7 5 = 35 + 6 = 41. Write 41. So yes, 4179. Okay, that seems right.\n\nNow, moving to the next digit in 107, which is 0. Multiply 597 by 0. That should be 0. So we write 0 next to the 9.\n\nThen the leftmost digit is 1. Multiply 597 by 1, which is 597. So we write that at the leftmost side.\n\nPutting it all together: 4179 (from 7), 0 (from 0), 597 (from 1). So 41790597? Wait, that seems too long. Wait, no. Wait, when you multiply by 107, which is 100 + 7, so 597 \u00d7 100 + 597 \u00d7 7. Let me check that.\n\n597 \u00d7 100 is 59700, and 597 \u00d7 7 is 4179. Then adding them together: 59700 + 4179. Let's compute that.\n\n59700 + 4000 = 63700\n63700 + 179 = 63879\n\nWait, so that's 63879. But when I did the vertical multiplication earlier, I got 41790597? That can't be right. Wait, no, I think I messed up the vertical multiplication.\n\nWait, no. Wait, when you do vertical multiplication, each step is shifted 