81 is approximately 800 + 80 + 1\n\n623 is approximately 600 + 20 + 3\n\nSo, 800 × 600 = 480,000\n\n80 × 20 = 1,600\n\n1 × 3 = 3\n\nThen, the remaining parts:\n\n800 × 20 = 16,000\n\n80 × 3 = 240\n\n1 × 600 = 600\n\nAdding all these:\n\n480,000 + 16,000 + 240 + 600 + 1,600 + 3\n\nWait, that's similar to the previous breakdown. Let's compute:\n\n480,000 + 16,000 = 496,000\n\n496,000 + 240 = 496,240\n\n496,240 + 600 = 496,840\n\n496,840 + 1,600 = 498,440\n\n498,440 + 3 = 498,443\n\nWait, that's different. Wait, maybe I messed up the breakdown here. Let me check again.\n\nWait, front-end estimation with 881 and 623:\n\nFirst, 800 × 600 = 480,000\n\nThen, 80 × 20 = 1,600\n\nThen, 1 × 3 = 3\n\nBut also, 800 × 20 = 16,000\n\n80 × 3 = 240\n\n1 × 600 = 600\n\nSo total is 480,000 + 16,000 + 240 + 600 + 1,600 + 3\n\nWait, that's 480,000 + 16,000 = 496,000\n\n496,000 + 240 = 496,240\n\n496,240 + 600 = 496,840\n\n496,840 + 1,600 = 498,440\n\n498,440 + 3 = 498,443\n\nBut that's 498,443, which is different from the previous total of 548,863. Wait, that can't be. Wait, maybe I confused the breakdown here. Wait, actually, when you do front-end estimation, you take the main digits and then adjust. But perhaps this method isn't as straightforward here because the numbers are close to round numbers. Alternatively, maybe this approach isn't the best here. Let me avoid this method now since it's leading me astray.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the fact that 881 × 623 = (880 + 1) × (620 + 3). Let's expand this:\n\n880 × 620 + 880 × 3 + 1 × 620 + 1 × 3\n\nCompute each term:\n\n880 × 620: Let's compute 88 × 62 first, then add two zeros.\n\n88 × 62: 80 × 62 = 4960, 8 × 62 = 496, so total 4960 + 496 = 5456. Then add two zeros: 545,600.\n\n880 × 3 = 2,640\n\n1 × 620 = 620\n\n1 × 3 = 3\n\nNow, add all these together:\n\n545,600 + 2,640 = 548,240\n\n548,240 + 620 = 548,860\n\n548,860 + 3 = 548,863\n\nSame result! So that's another confirmation. So regardless of the method, the product is 548,863.