the rightmost digit and move left, but when you multiply, you have to shift the result one place to the left for each subsequent digit. Wait, maybe I should do it step by step.\n\nWait, actually, when multiplying by a three-digit number, you can break it down into multiplying by each digit and then adding the results, shifted appropriately. Let me recall the standard multiplication steps.\n\nSo, 361 × 743. Let's break it down:\n\nFirst, multiply 361 by 3 (the ones place of 743). That's 1083.\n\nThen, multiply 361 by 4 (the tens place of 743), but since it's the tens place, we need to shift the result one place to the left. So 361 × 4 = 1444. Then shift left: 14440.\n\nNext, multiply 361 by 7 (the hundreds place of 743), shift two places to the left. So 361 × 7 = 2527. Shift left twice: 252700.\n\nNow, add all three results together: 1083 + 14440 + 252700.\n\nLet me compute that step by step.\n\nFirst, add 1083 and 14440. Let's see:\n\n1083 + 14440. Let's break it down:\n\n1000 + 14400 = 15400\n\n83 + 40 = 123\n\nSo total is 15400 + 123 = 15523.\n\nThen add 252700 to 15523:\n\n252700 + 15523. Let's break it down:\n\n252700 + 10000 = 262700\n\n262700 + 5000 = 267700\n\n267700 + 523 = 268223\n\nWait, let me check that again. 252700 + 15523:\n\n252700 + 15000 = 267700\n\n267700 + 523 = 268223. Yes, that's correct.\n\nSo the total product is 268,223.\n\nWait, let me verify this with another method to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Maybe using the distributive property.\n\nAlternatively, I can compute 361 × 743 by expanding it:\n\nFirst, 361 × 700 = 252,700\n\nThen, 361 × 40 = 14,440\n\nThen, 361 × 3 = 1,083\n\nAdding them together: 252,700 + 14,440 = 267,140; then 267,140 + 1,083 = 268,223. Yep, same result. So that seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use another approach. Let's see, 361 is 360 + 1, and 743 is 700 + 40 + 3. So:\n\n(360 + 1) × (700 + 40 + 3) = 360×700 + 360×40 + 360×3 + 1×700 + 1×40 + 1×3\n\nCompute each term:\n\n360×700 = 252,000\n\n360×40 = 14,400\n\n360×3 = 1,080\n\n1×700 = 700\n\n1×40 = 40\n\n1×3 = 3\n\nNow add all these together:\n\n252,000 + 14,400 = 266,400\n\n266,400 + 1,080 = 267,480\n\n267,480 + 700 = 268,180\n\n268,180 + 40 = 268,220\n\n268,220 + 3 = 268,223\n\nSame result again. So that's reassuring. So the product is 268,223.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use a calculator to check. But since I don't have a calculator here, but given that multiple methods give the same answer, I think it's safe to say the product is 268,223.\n\nWait, just to be thorough, let me check the multiplication step-by-step again.\n\nFirst, 361 × 3:\n\n300 × 3 = 900\n\n60 × 3 = 180\n\n1 × 3 = 3\n\n900 + 180 = 1,080\n\n1,080 + 3 = 1,083. Correct.\n\nThen 361 × 4:\n\n300 × 4 = 1,200\n\n60 × 4 = 240\n\n1 × 4 = 4\n\n1,200 + 240 = 1,440\n\n1,440 + 4 = 1,444. Correct.\n\nThen 361 × 7:\n\n300 × 7 = 2,100\n\n60 × 7 = 420\n\n1 × 7 = 7\n\n2,100 + 420 = 2,520\n\n2,520 + 7 = 2,527. Correct.\n\nAdding them up:\n\n1,083 + 1,444 = 2,527. Wait, no. Wait, 1,083 + 1,444. Let's compute that:\n\n1,000 + 1,000 = 2,000\n\n83 + 44 = 127\n\nSo total is 2,000 + 127 = 2,127. Wait, but earlier I had 1,083 + 1,4440. Wait, no, in the first method, when I shifted the 1,444 to the left by one place, making it 14,440. Then adding 1,083 + 14,440 = 15,523. Then adding 252,700 + 15,523 = 268,223. So that's correct.\n\nBut when I did the breakdown into 361×700, 361×40, 361×3, adding them up, I got 252,700 + 14,440 + 1,083 = 268,223. So that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, if I use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n\u202f\xa0\xa0361\n×\xa0\xa0743\n--------\n\xa0\xa01083\xa0\xa0(361 × 3)\n\xa0\xa014440\xa0\xa0(361 × 4, shifted left by one)\n\xa0\xa0252700\xa0\xa0(361 × 7, shifted left by two)\n--------\n\xa0\xa0268223\n\nYes, that's the same as before. So all methods converge to 268,223. Therefore, I can be confident that the product of 361 and 743 is 268,223.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 361 and 743 is \\boxed{268223}.\n"] 50%|█████     | 250/500 [4:03