      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 396 and 122. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers can be done by breaking them down into smaller parts or using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let me try both methods to make sure I get the right answer.\n\nFirst, maybe breaking it down. Let's split 396 into 300 + 96. Then, 122 can be split into 100 + 22. So, if I multiply each part separately and then add them up, that should work. Let me write that out:\n\n396 \u00d7 122 = (300 + 96) \u00d7 (100 + 22)\n\nUsing the distributive property, which is a + b)(c + d) = ac + ad + bc + bd.\n\nSo, first calculate 300 \u00d7 100. That's straightforward. 300 times 100 is 30,000.\n\nNext, 300 \u00d7 22. Let me compute that. 300 \u00d7 20 is 6,000 and 300 \u00d7 2 is 600. So, 6,000 + 600 = 6,600.\n\nThen, 96 \u00d7 100. That's easy, 96 \u00d7 100 = 9,600.\n\nLastly, 96 \u00d7 22. Hmm, this might be a bit trickier. Let me break 22 into 20 + 2. So, 96 \u00d7 20 = 1,920 and 96 \u00d7 2 = 192. Adding those together: 1,920 + 192 = 2,112.\n\nNow, add all those results together: 30,000 + 6,600 + 9,600 + 2,112.\n\nLet me add them step by step. First, 30,000 + 6,600 = 36,600.\n\nThen, 36,600 + 9,600 = 46,200.\n\nNext, 46,200 + 2,112. Let's see, 46,200 + 2,000 = 48,200, and then +112 gives 48,312.\n\nWait, so according to this method, the product is 48,312. Let me check if that's correct by using another method to verify.\n\nAlternatively, I can use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's write it out:\n\n      396\n    \u00d7 122\n    -------\n      792  (396 \u00d7 2)\n     8928   (396 \u00d7 20, shifted one position to the left)\n    892800  (396 \u00d7 100, shifted two positions to the left)\n    -------\n     48312\n\nWait, let me verify each step. Starting from the rightmost digit:\n\nFirst, multiply 396 by 2. That's 792. Then, multiply 396 by 20. Since 20 is 2 \u00d7 10, we shift the result one position left: 792 \u00d7 10 = 7,920. But wait, actually, 396 \u00d7 20 is 7,920, so shifted one position left would be 79,20. Wait, maybe I confused the shift. Let me clarify.\n\nWhen multiplying by the tens place (2 in 122), we shift the partial product one position to the left. So, 396 \u00d7 2 = 792. Then, 396 \u00d7 20 = 7,920. But since we're multiplying by the tens place (20), we shift the partial product one position left, so it becomes 7,920 shifted left by one: 7,920 becomes 7920. Similarly, multiplying by the hundreds place (1 in 122), we shift two positions left: 396 \u00d7 1 = 396, shifted two positions becomes 39600.\n\nNow, adding all the partial products:\n\n792 (from 2)\n7920 (from 20)\n39600 (from 100)\n-------\nAdding them up:\n\nFirst, 792 + 7920. Let's compute that. 792 + 7920. 7920 + 700 = 8620, then +92 = 8712.\n\nThen, add 39600 to 8712. 39600 + 8000 = 47600, then +712 = 48312.\n\nSo, same result as before. 48,312. That seems consistent. Let me check with another method just to be thorough.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the lattice method? Hmm, that might be more complicated, but let's try.\n\nBut maybe another way: 396 \u00d7 122. Let's compute 396 \u00d7 100 = 39,600; 396 \u00d7 20 = 7,920; 396 \u00d7 2 = 792. Then add them up: 39,600 + 7,920 = 47,520; 47,520 + 792 = 48,312. Yep, same answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using prime factors? Let's see. But that might be more involved. Let's try.\n\nFactorize 396 and 122.\n\n396: Let's divide by 2 first. 396 \u00f7 2 = 198. 198 \u00f7 2 = 99. 99 \u00f7 3 = 33. 33 \u00f7 3 = 11. So, 396 = 2\u00b2 \u00d7 3\u00b2 \u00d7 11.\n\n122: Divide by 2 first. 122 \u00f7 2 = 61. 61 is a prime number. So, 122 = 2 \u00d7 61.\n\nSo, multiplying them together: 2\u00b2 \u00d7 3\u00b2 \u00d7 11 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 61. Combine like terms: 2\u00b3 \u00d7 3\u00b2 \u00d7 11 \u00d7 61.\n\nCompute 2\u00b3 = 8; 3\u00b2 = 9; 8 \u00d7 9 = 72; 72 \u00d7 11 = 792; 792 \u00d7 61. Let's compute that.\n\n792 \u00d7 60 = 47,520; 792 \u00d7 1 = 792. So, 47,520 + 792 = 48,312. Again, same result.\n\nOkay, so all methods are leading me to 48,312. That gives me confidence that the answer is correct. Let me just verify with a calculator if possible. Wait, since I don't have a calculator here, but I can cross-check with another multiplication step.\n\nAlternatively, let's compute 396 \u00d7 122 using the standard algorithm step by step.\n\nFirst, write 396 \u00d7 122:\n\n      396\n    \u00d7 122\n    -------\n      792  (396 \u00d7 2)\n     8928   (396 \u00d7 20, shifted one)\n    892800  (396 \u00d7 100, shifted two)\n    -------\n     48312\n\nWait, let me check each partial product:\n\n- 396 \u00d7 2 = 792. Correct.\n- 396 \u00d7 20: 396 \u00d7 2 = 792, then \u00d710 is 7,920. So, 7,920. Shifted one position left, so it's 7920. Correct.\n- 396 \u00d7 100: 396 \u00d7 1 = 396, shifted two positions left is 39600. Correct.\n\nAdding them:\n\n792 + 7920 = 8712\n\n8712 + 39600 = 48312. Yep, same as before.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use another decomposition. Let's see, 122 is 120 + 2. So, 396 \u00d7 120 + 396 \u00d7 2.\n\n396 \u00d7 120: 396 \u00d7 100 = 39,600; 396 \u00d7 20 = 7,920. So, 39,600 + 7,920 = 47,520.\n\n396 \u00d7 2 = 792.\n\n47,520 + 792 = 48,312. Same answer.\n\nAlright, so all methods converge to 48,312. Therefore, I can be confident that the product of 396 and 122 is 48,312.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 396 and 122 is \\boxed{48312}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 396 and 122, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n396 \\times 122 = (300 + 96) \\times (100 + 22)\n\\]\n\nUsing the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n(300 \\times 100) + (300 \\times 22) + (96 \\times 100) + (96 \\times 22)\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n\n1. \\(300 \\times 100 = 30,000\\)\n2. \\(300 \\times 22 = 6,600\\)\n3. \\(96 \\times 100 = 9,600\\)\n4. \\(96 \\times 22 = 2,112\\)\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n30,000 + 6,600 + 9,600 + 2,112 = 48,312\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n  396 \\\\\n\\times 122 \\\\\n\\hline\n   792 \\quad \\text{(396 \\times 2)} \\\\\n  7920 \\quad \\text{(396 \\times 20, shifted one position left)} \\\\\n 39600 \\quad \\text{(396 \\times 100, shifted two positions left)} \\\\\n\\hline\n 48312 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAdding the partial products:\n\n\\[\n792 + 7920 + 39600 = 48312\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 396 and 122 is \\(\\boxed{48312}\\).",
