 11 (carry over 1)\n5 + 5 + 1 = 11 (carry over 1)\nThen the thousands place: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1\n\nSo, 5467 + 54670 = 59,137.\n\nNow, add 1562 to 59,137. Let's do that:\n\n\u202f\u00a0\u00a059137\n+\u00a0\u00a0\u00a01562\n----------\nStarting from the right:\n7 + 2 = 9\n3 + 6 = 9\n1 + 5 = 6\n9 + 1 = 10 (carry over 1)\n5 + 0 + 1 = 6\n\nSo, 59,137 + 1,562 = 60,699.\n\nWait, let me check that addition again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. \n\nFirst addition: 5467 + 54670. Let's verify:\n\n5467 + 54670. Let's break it down:\n\n5467 + 54670 = 54670 + 5467. Let's add 54670 + 5000 = 59670, then +467 = 59670 + 467 = 60,137. Wait, that's different from what I had before. Hmm, maybe I messed up the alignment.\n\nWait, when adding 5467 and 54670, the second number is 54670, which is 5 digits, so when adding to 5467 (4 digits), we need to align the rightmost digits:\n\n\u202f\u00a0\u00a05467\n+\u00a054670\n--------\nStarting from the right:\n7 + 0 = 7\n6 + 7 = 13 (carry 1)\n4 + 6 + 1 = 11 (carry 1)\n5 + 5 + 1 = 11 (carry 1)\n0 + 0 + 1 = 1\n\nSo, the sum is 60,137. Wait, so my initial calculation was wrong. I thought it was 59,137, but it's actually 60,137. That's a mistake. Let me correct that.\n\nSo, 5467 + 54670 = 60,137. Then, adding 1562 to 60,137:\n\n60,137 + 1,562. Let's do this step by step.\n\n60,137 + 1,000 = 61,137\n\n61,137 + 500 = 61,637\n\n61,637 + 60 = 61,697\n\n61,697 + 2 = 61,699\n\nSo, the total is 61,699. Let me check that again.\n\nAlternatively, 60,137 + 1,562:\n\n60,137 + 1,562. Let's add 60,137 + 1,000 = 61,137\n\n61,137 + 500 = 61,637\n\n61,637 + 60 = 61,697\n\n61,697 + 2 = 61,699. Yes, that's correct.\n\nSo, the final product is 61,699. Let me verify this using another method to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the distributive property. Let's see:\n\n781 \u00d7 277. Let's break down 277 into 200 + 70 + 7.\n\nSo, 781 \u00d7 200 = 156,200\n\n781 \u00d7 70 = 54,670\n\n781 \u00d7 7 = 5,467\n\nNow, add them all together:\n\n156,200 + 54,670 = 210,870\n\n210,870 + 5,467 = 216,337\n\nWait, that's different from 61,699. Hmm, that can't be right. There must be a mistake here. Wait, no, hold on. Wait, 781 \u00d7 200 is 156,200? Let me check that again.\n\n781 \u00d7 200. 781 \u00d7 2 = 1,562. Then \u00d7 100 is 156,200. That's correct.\n\n781 \u00d7 70. 781 \u00d7 7 = 5,467. Then \u00d7 10 is 54,670. Correct.\n\n781 \u00d7 7. 5,467. Correct.\n\nAdding 156,200 + 54,670: 156,200 + 50,000 = 206,200; 206,200 + 4,670 = 210,870. Then 210,870 + 5,467: 210,870 + 5,000 = 215,870; 215,870 + 467 = 216,337. So, according to this method, the product is 216,337. But that contradicts the previous result of 61,699. Clearly, something is wrong here. Wait, but 781 \u00d7 277. Let me check with another method.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake in the first method. Let me check the first multiplication step-by-step again.\n\nFirst partial product: 781 \u00d7 7. Let's compute 781 \u00d7 7:\n\n700 \u00d7 7 = 4,900\n\n80 \u00d7 7 = 560\n\n1 \u00d7 7 = 7\n\nAdding them: 4,900 + 560 = 5,460; 5,460 + 7 = 5,467. That's correct.\n\nSecond partial product: 781 \u00d7 70. 781 \u00d7 7 = 5,467; \u00d710 is 54,670. Correct.\n\nThird partial product: 781 \u00d7 2. 700 \u00d7 2 = 1,400; 80 \u00d7 2 = 160; 1 \u00d7 2 = 2. Adding: 1,400 + 160 = 1,560; 1,560 + 2 = 1,562. Correct.\n\nAdding the three partial products:\n\nFirst: 5,467\n\nSecond: 54,670\n\nThird: 1,562\n\nAdding 5,467 + 54,670:\n\n5,467 + 54,670. Let's do 54,670 + 5,000 = 59,670; 59,670 + 467 = 60,137. Then add 1,562: 60,137 + 1,562 = 61,699. So that's correct.\n\nBut when I broke down 277 into 200 + 70 + 7, I got 216,337. That's way off. Wait, why is that? Because 781 \u00d7 277 is not the same as 781 \u00d7 200 + 781 \u00d7 70 + 781 \u00d7 7. Wait, no, actually, that is the distributive property. Wait, 277 is 200 + 70 + 7, so 781 \u00d7 277 = 781 \u00d7 (200 + 70 + 7) = 781\u00d7200 + 781\u00d770 + 781\u00d77. But according to this, it's 156,200 + 54,670 + 5,467 = 216,337. But