output:  ['\n\nTo find the product of 781 and 277, we can use the distributive property and break down the multiplication into smaller parts:\n\n1. Break down 277 into 200, 70, and 7.\n2. Multiply 781 by each part separately:\n   - \\( 781 \\times 200 = 156200 \\)\n   - \\( 781 \\times 70 = 54670 \\)\n   - \\( 781 \\times 7 = 5467 \\)\n3. Add the results together:\n   - First, add \\( 156200 \\) and \\( 54670 \\):\n     \\[\n     156200 + 54670 = 210870\n     \\]\n   - Then, add the result to \\( 5467 \\):\n     \\[\n     210870 + 5467 = 216337\n     \\]\n\nThus, the product of 781 and 277 is \\(\\boxed{216337}\\).', "\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 781 and 277. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two three-digit numbers can be a bit tricky, but maybe there's a way to break it down step by step. Let me start by recalling the standard multiplication algorithm. \n\nFirst, I'll write 781 multiplied by 277. Let me set it up like this:\n\n\u202f\xa0\xa0781\n×\xa0\xa0277\n--------\n\nStarting with the rightmost digit of the second number, which is 7. I need to multiply 781 by 7 first. Let me do that:\n\n781 × 7. Let's break it down:\n- 700 × 7 = 4900\n- 80 × 7 = 560\n- 1 × 7 = 7\nAdding those together: 4900 + 560 = 5460, then 5460 + 7 = 5467. So, 781 × 7 = 5467. I'll write that down as the first partial product.\n\nNext, I move to the middle digit of 277, which is 7. But wait, in the multiplication setup, the digits are written from right to left, so the next digit is actually the tens place. So, the second partial product will be shifted one place to the left. Let me adjust that.\n\nNow, multiply 781 by 70. Since 70 is 7 times 10, I can do 781 × 7 first and then multiply by 10. We already know 781 × 7 is 5467, so 5467 × 10 = 54670. That's the second partial product.\n\nThen, the leftmost digit is 2. So, multiply 781 by 2. Let's calculate that:\n- 700 × 2 = 1400\n- 80 × 2 = 160\n- 1 × 2 = 2\nAdding those: 1400 + 160 = 1560, then 1560 + 2 = 1562. So, 781 × 2 = 1562. This is the third partial product.\n\nNow, I need to add all three partial products together: 5467 (from the first multiplication), 54670 (shifted left by one), and 1562 (shifted left by two). Let me write them aligned properly:\n\n\u202f\xa0\xa05467\n\xa0\xa054670\n+\xa01562\n-------\nLet's add them step by step.\n\nFirst, add 5467 and 54670. Let's line them up:\n\n\u202f\xa0\xa05467\n+\xa054670\n--------\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n7 + 0 = 7\n6 + 7 = 13 (carry over 1)\n4 + 6 + 1 (carry) = 11 (carry over 1)\n5 + 5 + 1 = 11 (carry over 1)\nThen the thousands place: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1\n\nSo, 5467 + 54670 = 59,137.\n\nNow, add 1562 to 59,137. Let's do that:\n\n\u202f\xa0\xa059137\n+\xa0\xa0\xa01562\n----------\nStarting from the right:\n7 + 2 = 9\n3 + 6 = 9\n1 + 5 = 6\n9 + 1 = 10 (carry over 1)\n5 + 0 + 1 = 6\n\nSo, 59,137 + 1,562 = 60,699.\n\nWait, let me check that addition again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. \n\nFirst addition: 5467 + 54670. Let's verify:\n\n5467 + 54670. Let's break it down:\n\n5467 + 54670 = 54670 + 5467. Let's add 54670 + 5000 = 59670, then +467 = 59670 + 467 = 60,137. Wait, that's different from what I had before. Hmm, maybe I messed up the alignment.\n\nWait, when adding 5467 and 54670, the second number is 54670, which is 5 digits, so when adding to 5467 (4 digits), we need to align the rightmost digits:\n\n\u202f\xa0\xa05467\n+\xa054670\n--------\nStarting from the right:\n7 + 0 = 7\n6 + 7 = 13 (carry 1)\n4 + 6 + 1 = 11 (carry 1)\n5 + 5 + 1 = 11 (carry 1)\n0 + 0 + 1 = 1\n\nSo, the sum is 60,137. Wait, so my initial calculation was wrong. I thought it was 59,137, but it's actually 60,137. That's a mistake. Let me correct that.\n\nSo, 5467 + 54670 = 60,137. Then, adding 1562 to 60,137:\n\n60,137 + 1,562. Let's do this step by step.\n\n60,137 + 1,000 = 61,137\n\n61,137 + 500 = 61,637\n\n61,637 + 60 = 61,697\n\n61,697 + 2 = 61,699\n\nSo, the total is 61,699. Let me check that again.\n\nAlternatively, 60,137 + 1,562:\n\n60,137 + 1,562. Let's add 60,137 + 1,000 = 61,137\n\n61,137 + 500 = 61,637\n\n61,637 + 60 = 61,697\n\n61,697 + 2 = 61,699. Yes, that's correct.\n\nSo, the final product is 61,699. Let me verify this using another method to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the distributive property. Let's see:\n\n781 × 277. Let's break down 277 into 200 + 70 + 7.\n\nSo, 781 × 200 = 156,200\n\n781 × 70 = 54,670\n\n781 × 7 = 5,467\n\nNow, add them all together:\n\n156,200 + 54,670 = 210,870\n\n210,870 + 5,467 = 216,337\n\nWait, that's different from 61,699. Hmm, that can't be right. There must be a mistake here. Wait, no, hold on. Wait, 781 × 200 is 156,200? Let me check that again.\n\n781 × 200. 781 × 2 = 1,562. Then × 100 is 156,200. That's correct.\n\n781 × 70. 781 × 7 = 5,467. Then × 10 is 54,670. Correct.\n\n781 × 7. 5,467. Correct.\n\nAdding 156,200 + 54,670: 156,200 + 50,000 = 206,200; 206,200 + 4,670 = 210,870. Then 210,870 + 5,467: 210,870 + 5,000 = 215,870; 215,870 + 467 = 216,337. So, according to this method, the product is 216,337. But that contradicts the previous result of 61,699. Clearly, something is wrong here. Wait, but 781 × 277. Let me check with another method.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake in the first method. Let me check the first multiplication step-by-step again.\n\nFirst partial product: 781 × 7. Let's compute 781 × 7:\n\n700 × 7 = 4,900\n\n80 × 7 = 560\n\n1 × 7 = 7\n\nAdding them: 4,900 + 560 = 5,460; 5,460 + 7 = 5,467. That's correct.\n\nSecond partial product: 781 × 70. 781 × 7 = 5,467; ×10 is 54,670. Correct.\n\nThird partial product: 781 × 2. 700 × 2 = 1,400; 80 × 2 = 160; 1 × 2 = 2. Adding: 1,400 + 160 = 1,560; 1,560 + 2 = 1,562. Correct.\n\nAdding the three partial products:\n\nFirst: 5,467\n\nSecond: 54,670\n\nThird: 1,562\n\nAdding 5,467 + 54,670:\n\n5,467 + 54,670. Let's do 54,670 + 5,000 = 59,670; 59,670 + 467 = 60,137. Then add 1,562: 60,137 + 1,562 = 61,699. So that's correct.\n\nBut when I broke down 277 into 200 + 70 + 7, I got 216,337. That's way off. Wait, why is that? Because 781 × 277 is not the same as 781 × 200 + 781 × 70 + 781 × 7. Wait, no, actually, that is the distributive property. Wait, 277 is 200 + 70 + 7, so 781 × 277 = 781 × (200 + 70 + 7) = 781×200 + 781×70 + 781×7. But according to this, it's 156,200 + 54,670 + 5,467 = 216,337. But that contradicts the first method. So, which one is correct?\n\nWait, let me check with a calculator. Let me compute 781 × 277. Let's do it step by step.\n\nFirst, 781 × 200 = 156,200\n\n781 × 70 = 54,670\n\n781 × 7 = 5,467\n\nAdding them: 156,200 + 54,670 = 210,870; 210,870 + 5,467 = 216,337.\n\nBut according to the other method, the product is 61,699. That's a discrepancy. So, clearly, one of the methods is wrong. Wait, but how?\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, 781 × 277. Let me compute it directly.\n\nLet me use another approach. Let's compute 781 × 277.\n\nFirst, multiply 781 by 7: 5,467\n\nThen, multiply 781 by 70: 54,670\n\nThen, multiply 781 by 200: 156,200\n\nWait, but 277 is 200 + 70 + 7, so adding those gives 156,200 + 54,670 + 5,467 = 216,337. But when I did the standard multiplication, I got 61,699. That's a problem. So, which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the standard multiplication. Let me check that again.\n\nStandard multiplication:\n\n\u202f\xa0\xa0781\n×\xa0\xa0277\n--------\n\nFirst, multiply 781 by 7:\n\n781\n×\xa0\xa07\n------\n5,467\n\nThen, multiply 781 by 70 (shift one position left):\n\n781\n×\xa0\xa070\n------\n54,670\n\nThen, multiply 781 by 200 (shift two positions left):\n\n781\n×\xa0\xa0200\n------\n156,200\n\nNow, add them up:\n\n5,467\n+54,670\n+156,200\n--------\nLet's add 5,467 + 54,670 first. 5,467 + 54,670. Let's compute:\n\n5,467 + 54,670. Let's add 5,467 + 50,000 = 55,467; then add 4,670: 55,467 + 4,670 = 60,137. Then add 156,200: 60,137 + 156,200 = 216,337. Wait, so now I get 216,337. But earlier, when I thought I had 61,699, that was a mistake. Wait, so where did I go wrong before?\n\nWait, in my first calculation, I thought that 5467 (first partial product) + 54670 (second partial product) + 1562 (third partial product) equals 61,699. But actually, when adding 5,467 + 54,670 + 156,200, it's 5,467 + 54,670 = 60,137; 60,137 + 156,200 = 216,337. So, my initial addition was wrong because I misaligned the numbers. So, actually, both methods give the same result: 216,337. Wait, but earlier I thought the first method gave 61,699, but that was a miscalculation. So, both methods agree that the product is 216,337. Therefore, the correct answer is 216,337.\n\nBut wait, let me confirm with another method. Let's use the standard algorithm again, but more carefully.\n\nMultiplying 781 by 277:\n\nFirst, write 781 × 277.\n\nStart with the units digit of 277, which is 7. Multiply 781 by 7:\n\n781\n×\xa0\xa07\n------\n5,467\n\nNext, multiply 781 by the tens digit, which is 7 (since we move to the next digit, which is 7 in the tens place). So, we add a zero at the end of 781, making it 7810. Then multiply by 7:\n\n7810\n×\xa0\xa07\n------\n54,670\n\nThen, multiply 781 by the hundreds digit, which is 2 (since the hundreds digit in 277 is 2). So, we add two zeros to 781, making it 78100. Multiply by 2:\n\n78100\n×\xa0\xa02\n------\n156,200\n\nNow, add the three partial products:\n\n5,467\n+54,670\n+156,200\n----------\nLet's add 5,467 + 54,670 first. 5,467 + 54,670. Let's compute:\n\n5,467 + 54,670. Let's break it down:\n\n5,467 + 50,000 = 55,467\n\n55,467 + 4,670 = 60,137\n\nThen add 156,200 to 60,137:\n\n60,137 + 156,200. Let's compute:\n\n60,137 + 156,200. Break it down:\n\n60,137 + 100,000 = 160,137\n\n160,137 + 56,200 = 216,337\n\nSo, the total is 216,337. Therefore, both methods confirm that the product is 216,337. So, my initial mistake was in the first addition where I incorrectly added 5467 + 54670 as 59,137 instead of 60,137. That was an error in aligning the digits. So, the correct product is 216,337.\n\nBut wait, let me check with another approach. Let's use the multiplication algorithm step by step.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the fact that 781 × 277 = 781 × (200 + 70 + 7) = 781×200 + 781×70 + 781×7 = 156,200 + 54,670 + 5,467 = 216,337. So, same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the standard multiplication:\n\n\u202f\xa0\xa0781\n×\xa0\xa0277\n----------\n\xa0\xa05,467\xa0\xa0(781 × 7)\n\xa0\xa054,670\xa0\xa0\xa0(781 × 70, shifted one position left)\n\xa0+156,200\xa0\xa0\xa0(781 × 200, shifted two positions left)\n----------\n\xa0\xa0216,337\n\nYes, that's correct. So, the product is 216,337. Therefore, the answer is 216,337.\n\nBut wait, let me check with a calculator to be absolutely sure. Let me compute 781 × 277.\n\nFirst, 781 × 200 = 156,200\n\n781 × 70 = 54,670\n\n781 × 7 = 5,467\n\nAdding them up: 156,200 + 54,670 = 210,870; 210,870 + 5,467 = 216,337. Yep, that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, using a calculator: 781 × 277. Let me compute 781 × 277.\n\nLet me compute 781 × 277:\n\nFirst, compute 781 × 200 = 156,200\n\n781 × 70 = 54,670\n\n781 × 7 = 5,467\n\nAdding all together: 156,200 + 54,670 = 210,870; 210,870 + 5,467 = 216,337. So, same result.\n\nTherefore, the correct product is 216,337. My initial mistake was in the first addition step where I miscalculated the sum of 5467 and 54670. After correcting that, both methods give the same result. So, the answer is 216,337.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 781 and 277 is \\boxed{216337}.\n"] 53%|█████▎    | 263/500 [4:14:31<3:42:28, 56.32s/it]
