n algorithm correctly. Let me try writing it out.\n\nMultiplying 171 by 271:\n\nFirst, multiply 171 by 1 (units digit of 271):\n\n171\n*   1\n------\n171\n\nThen multiply 171 by 7 (tens digit of 271):\n\n171\n*  7\n------\n1197\n\nThen multiply 171 by 2 (hundreds digit of 271):\n\n171\n*  2\n------\n342\n\nNow, add these three partial products:\n\n171\n1197\n------\n342\n------\n171 + 1197 = 1368\n\n1368 + 342 = 1710\n\nSo, the total is 1710. But when I shifted the second partial product left by one place, it's 11970, and adding 171 + 11970 = 1368, then adding 342 gives 1710. So that seems correct.\n\nBut earlier, when I did the digit multiplication method, I got 990, which is different. So there's a conflict here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with actual multiplication. Let's compute 171 * 271 using another method.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the distributive property.\n\n171 * 271 = 171 * (200 + 70 + 1) = 171*200 + 171*70 + 171*1\n\nCompute each term:\n\n171*200 = 34,200\n\n171*70 = 12,070\n\n171*1 = 171\n\nNow, add them together:\n\n34,200 + 12,070 = 46,270\n\n46,270 + 171 = 46,441\n\nWait, so that gives 46,441. But that's different from 1710. So clearly, there's a problem with my previous methods.\n\nWait, so now I'm really confused. Which one is correct? Let's check with a calculator.\n\nWait, I can check using a calculator. Let me compute 171 * 271.\n\n171 * 271. Let's compute 170*271 + 1*271.\n\n170*271: 170*200 = 34,000; 170*70 = 11,900; 170*1 = 170. So 34,000 + 11,900 = 45,900 + 170 = 46,070.\n\nThen 1*271 = 271. So total is 46,070 + 271 = 46,341.\n\nSo the correct product is 46,341. Therefore, my previous methods were wrong. So where did I go wrong?\n\nFirst, in the digit multiplication method. Let me check that again.\n\nBreaking down 171 into 100 + 70 + 1 and 271 into 200 + 70 + 1.\n\nThen, multiplying each part:\n\n100*200 = 20,000\n\n100*70 = 7,000\n\n100*1 = 100\n\n70*200 = 14,000\n\n70*70 = 4,900\n\n70*1 = 70\n\n1*200 = 200\n\n1*70 = 70