output:  ['\n\nTo find the product of 263 and 361, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm and verify the result using the distributive property.\n\n**Standard Multiplication Method:**\n\n1. Multiply 263 by the units digit (1) of 361:\n   \\[\n   263 \\times 1 = 263\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 263 by the tens digit (6) of 361, shifted one position to the left:\n   \\[\n   263 \\times 6 = 1578\n   \\]\n   Shifted one position left: \\(\\ 15780\\)\n\n3. Multiply 263 by the hundreds digit (3) of 361, shifted two positions to the left:\n   \\[\n   263 \\times 3 = 789\n   \\]\n   Shifted two positions left: \\(\\ 78900\\)\n\n4. Add the partial products:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     263 \\\\\n   + 15780 \\\\\n   + 78900 \\\\\n   \\hline\n   94943 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n**Verification using the Distributive Property:**\n\n1. Break down 361 into 300 + 60 + 1:\n   \\[\n   263 \\times 361 = 263 \\times (300 + 60 + 1)\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate each part separately:\n   \\[\n   263 \\times 300 = 78900\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   263 \\times 60 = 15780\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   263 \\times 1 = 263\n   \\]\n\n3. Add the results:\n   \\[\n   78900 + 15780 + 263 = 94943\n   \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 263 and 361 is \\(\\boxed{94943}\\).', "\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 263 and 361. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two three-digit numbers can be a bit tricky, but maybe there's a way to break it down step by step. Let me start by recalling the standard multiplication algorithm. \n\nFirst, I'll write 263 multiplied by 361. Let me set it up like this:\n\n\u202f\xa0\xa0263\n×\xa0\xa0361\n--------\n\nStarting with the rightmost digit of the second number, which is 1. I need to multiply 263 by 1. That should be straightforward. 263 × 1 = 263. So I write down 263 and carry over nothing.\n\nNext, I move to the middle digit of 361, which is 6. Now, I need to multiply 263 by 6 and add the result to 263. Let me calculate 263 × 6. Breaking it down