      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 100 and 226. Hmm, let's see. The product means multiplication, right? So I have to multiply 100 by 226. Alright, let me think about how to approach this.\n\nFirst, I know that multiplying by 100 is the same as multiplying by 10 and then by 10 again. Because 100 is 10 times 10. So maybe I can break it down. Let me try that.\n\nSo, 100 times 226. If I first multiply 226 by 10, that should be 2260. Then, multiplying that result by another 10 would be 2260 times 10. Wait, but 2260 times 10 is just moving the decimal point over one place, right? So 2260 becomes 22600. So that would be 100 times 226 equals 22600. Let me check if that makes sense.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can think of 100 as 10^2. So 226 times 10^2. Which is the same as 226 squared? Wait, no, 10 squared is 100, so 226 times 10 squared is 226 times 100. Yeah, that's the same as before. So 226 times 100 is indeed 22600. Let me verify with another method to be sure.\n\nAnother way to multiply 226 by 100 is to add 226 a hundred times. But that's tedious. Maybe I can use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's try that.\n\nMultiplying 226 by 100. Let's write it out:\n\n\u202f\u00a0\u00a0226\n\u00d7\u00a0\u00a0100\n-------\n\u00a0\u00a022600\n\nYes, that works. The way it works is that multiplying by 100 adds two zeros to the end of the number. So 226 becomes 22600. So that's consistent with the first method.\n\nWait, let me make sure I didn't make a mistake in the standard multiplication. Let's do it step by step. First, multiply 226 by 1 (the ones place of 100). That's 226. Then, multiply 226 by 0 (the tens place of 100). That's 0. Then, multiply 226 by 0 (the hundreds place of 100). That's also 0. Then add them up: 226 + 0 + 0 = 226. Wait, that doesn't seem right. Wait, no, hold on. Wait, in standard multiplication, when you multiply by 100, you actually need to shift the digits two places to the left, not add zeros. Maybe I confused the steps.\n\nWait, no. Let me correct that. When you multiply by 100, you take the original number and add two zeros at the end. So 226 becomes 22600. But in the standard multiplication method, you can think of it as multiplying by 10 and then by 10 again. So first, 226 \u00d7 10 = 2260. Then, 2260 \u00d7 10 = 22600. So that's correct. So the standard multiplication would be:\n\n\u202f\u00a0\u00a0226\n\u00d7\u00a0\u00a0100\n-------\n\u00a0\u00a022600\n\nYes, that's right. The first step is multiplying by 10 (which adds a zero at the end), then multiplying by another 10 (adding another zero). So the result is 22600.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the distributive property. 100 is 100/1, so 226 \u00d7 100 = 226 \u00d7 (100/1) = 226 \u00d7 100. Hmm, that's the same as before. Not sure if that helps.\n\nAlternatively, maybe breaking down 226 into 200 + 26. Then, 100 \u00d7 200 = 20,000 and 100 \u00d7 26 = 2,600. Then, adding those together: 20,000 + 2,600 = 22,600. So that's another way to get the same result. That checks out.\n\nSo all methods lead to 22,600. Let me just verify with a calculator to be absolutely sure. If I take 226 and multiply by 100, moving the decimal two places to the right gives 22600. Yep, that's correct.\n\nI think that's solid. All different methods confirm that the product is 22,600. So I can be confident that's the right answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 100 and 226 is \\boxed{22600}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 100 and 226, we can use the fact that multiplying by 100 is equivalent to multiplying by 10 and then by 10 again. \n\n1. First, multiply 226 by 10:\n   \\[\n   226 \\times 10 = 2260\n   \\]\n2. Then, multiply the result by 10 again:\n   \\[\n   2260 \\times 10 = 22600\n   \\]\n\nWe can also verify this by recognizing that multiplying by 100 adds two zeros to the end of the number 226, resulting in 22600. Additionally, breaking down 226 into 200 + 26 and then multiplying each part by 100 and adding the results also confirms the result:\n   \\[\n   200 \\times 100 + 26 \\times 100 = 20000 + 2600 = 22600\n   \\]\n\nThus, the product of 100 and 226 is \\(\\boxed{22600}\\).",
      "logprobs": null,
