output:  ['\n\nTo find the product of 684 and 663, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm and break down the problem step by step:\n\n1. Multiply 684 by the rightmost digit (3):\n   \\[\n   684 \\times 3 = 2052\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 684 by the next digit (6), shifted one position to the left:\n   \\[\n   684 \\times 6 = 4104 \\quad \\text{(shifted one position left becomes 41040)}\n   \\]\n\n3. Multiply 684 by the next digit (6), shifted two positions to the left:\n   \\[\n   684 \\times 6 = 4104 \\quad \\text{(shifted two positions left becomes 410400)}\n   \\]\n\n4. Add the results of the three multiplications together:\n   \\[\n   2052 + 41040 + 410400 = 453492\n   \\]\n\nWe can verify this result using an alternative method, such as expanding the product using algebraic decomposition:\n\\[\n684 \\times 663 = (700 - 16) \\times (600 + 63)\n\\]\n\\[\n= 700 \\times 600 + 700 \\times 63 - 16 \\times 600 - 16 \\times 63\n\\]\n\\[\n= 420000 + 44100 - 9600 - 1008\n\\]\n\\[\n= 464100 - 9600 - 1008\n\\]\n\\[\n= 454500 - 1008\n\\]\n\\[\n= 453492\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 684 and 663 is \\(\\boxed{453492}\\).', "\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 684 and 663. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two three-digit numbers can be a bit tricky, but maybe there's a way to break it down step by step. Let me start by recalling the standard multiplication algorithm. \n\nFirst, I'll write 684 multiplied by 663. Let me set it up like this:\n\n\u202f\xa0\xa0684\n×\xa0\xa0663\n--------\n\nStarting with the rightmost digit of the second number, which is 3. I need to multiply 684 by 3 and then multiply 684 by the next digit, which is 6, but since it's in the tens place, I have to remember to shift it one position to the left. Then, multiply 684 by 6 again, but this time two positions to the left (because it's in the hundreds place). Finally, add all those results together.\n\nLet me do each part step by step.\n\nFirst, multiplying 684 by 3:\n\n3 × 600 = 1800\n3