100 = 167200\n\n209 × 20. 209 × 2 = 418, so 418 × 10 = 4180\n\n209 × 6 = 1254 (as before)\n\nAdding them together: 167200 + 4180 + 1254\n\nFirst, 167200 + 4180:\n\n167200 + 4000 = 171200\n\n171200 + 180 = 171380\n\nThen add 1254: 171380 + 1254\n\n171380 + 1000 = 172380\n\n172380 + 254 = 172634\n\nWait, that's different. Wait, hold on. Wait, 171380 + 1254:\n\n171380 + 1000 = 172380\n\n172380 + 200 = 172580\n\n172580 + 54 = 172634\n\nBut earlier, when I added the three partial products, I got 7106. But here, using the distributive method, I get 172634. That's a discrepancy. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, that can't be. There must be a mistake here. Let me check.\n\nWait, 209 × 826. If I use the distributive method, breaking it into 800 + 20 + 6, then:\n\n209 × 800 = 167200\n\n209 × 20 = 4180\n\n209 × 6 = 1254\n\nAdding them: 167200 + 4180 = 171380; 171380 + 1254 = 172634\n\nBut when I did the standard multiplication, I got 7106. That's way off. So clearly, I made a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nWait, 7106 is way too small. 209 × 826 is definitely a larger number. Let me check my standard multiplication again.\n\nWait, 209 × 826. Let me do it step by step again.\n\nFirst, multiply 209 by 6 (ones place):\n\n209 × 6 = 1254\n\nThen, multiply 209 by 20 (tens place, shifted one position to the left):\n\n209 × 20 = 4180\n\nThen, multiply 209 by 800 (hundreds place, shifted two positions to the left):\n\n209 × 800 = 167200\n\nWait, but in the standard multiplication, when you multiply by the hundreds place (8), you have to shift the partial product two places to the left, so it's 167200, which is correct.\n\nThen, adding 1254 (ones) + 4180 (tens) + 167200 (hundreds). Wait, but 1254 + 4180 is 5434, then 5434 + 167200 is 172634. Wait, but that contradicts the previous result. Wait, so which is correct?\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, in the standard multiplication, when you multiply by the hundreds digit (8), you have to multiply 209 by 8 and then add two zeros, making it 167200. Then, when you add the partial products:\n\n1254 (ones)\n+4180 (tens)\n+167200 (hundreds)\n--------\nLet me add them properly:\n\nFirst, 1254 + 4180:\n\n1254 + 4180. Let's do 1254 + 4000 = 5254, then +180 = 5434. That's correct.\n\nThen, 5434 + 167200. Let's do 5434 + 167200:\n\n5434 + 167200. Let's break it down:\n\n5434 + 160000 = 165434\n\n165434 + 7200 = 172634\n\n172634 + 20 = 172654\n\nWait, no. Wait, 167200 is 167,200. So 5434 + 167200:\n\n5434 + 167200. Let's add 5434 + 167200:\n\nStart from 5434:\n\n5434 + 167200. Let's add 167200 to 5434.\n\n167200 + 5434:\n\n167200 + 5000 = 172200\n\n172200 + 434 = 172634\n\nYes, so 5434 + 167200 = 172634. So the total is 172634. But earlier, when I did the distributive method, I also got 172634. So why did I get 7106 before? Because I must have miscalculated when I first did the standard multiplication.\n\nWait, in my first standard multiplication attempt, I wrote:\n\nFirst partial product: 1254\n\nSecond: 4180\n\nThird: 1672\n\nThen added them as 1254 + 4180 + 1672. But 1254 + 4180 is 5434, then 5434 + 1672 is 7106. Wait, that's wrong. Wait, 5434 + 1672 is 7106? Let me check:\n\n5434 + 1672:\n\n5000 + 1000 = 6000\n\n400 + 600 = 1000\n\n30 + 70 = 100\n\n4 + 2 = 6\n\nWait, but 5434 + 1672:\n\n5434 + 1672. Let's do it digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n4 + 2 = 6\n\n3 + 7 = 10, carry over 1\n\n4 + 6 + 1 (carry) = 11, carry over 1\n\n5 + 1 + 1 (carry) = 7\n\nSo total is 7106. Wait, so 5434 + 1672 = 7106. But that contradicts the previous result where 1254 + 4180 + 1672 = 172634. Wait, no. Wait, no, that's not possible. Wait, 1254 + 4180 is 5434, then 5434 + 1672 is 7106. But how can that be? Because 1254 + 4180 + 1672 is 1254 + (4180 + 1672) = 1254 + 5852 = 7106. Wait, but 1254 + 4180 is 5434, then 5434 + 1672 is 7106. But that would mean that 1254 + 4180 + 1672 = 7106. But that's not possible because 1254 + 4180 is 5434, and 5434 + 1672 is 7106. Wait, but 5434 + 1672 is indeed 7106. So where is the mistake here?\n\nWait, no. Wait, 1254 + 4180 + 1672. Let's compute it step by step:\n\nFirst, 1254 + 4180:\n\n1254 + 4180. Let's do 1254 + 4000 = 5254, then +180 = 5434. Correct.\n\nThen, 5434 + 1672:\n\n5434 + 1672. Let's compute:\n\n5434 + 1672:\n\nStart from the right:\n\n4 + 2 = 6\n\n3 + 7 = 10, carry 1\n\n4 + 6 + 1 = 11, carry 1\n\n5 + 1 + 1 = 7\n\nSo total is 7106. So 1254 + 4180 + 1672 = 7106. But that contradicts the other method where I added 167200 + 4180 + 1254 = 172634. Wait, but that can't be. There's a mistake here. Wait, no. Wait, in the standard multiplication, the three partial products are 1254, 4180, and 167200. Wait, no. Wait, in the standard multiplication, when you multiply by the hundreds digit (8), you have to shift the partial product two places to the left, so it's 167200. Then, when adding, you add 1254 (ones place) + 4180 (tens place) + 167200 (hundreds place). But 1254 is 1 thousand, 4 hundreds, 5 tens, 4 ones. 4180 is 4 thousands, 1 hundred, 8 tens, 0 ones. 167200 is 167 thousands, 2 hundreds, 0 tens, 0 ones. So when adding, you have to align them properly:\n\n  1254\n+ 4180\n+167200\n--------\n 172634\n\nAh! So I made a mistake in the first addition. I thought 1254 + 4180 + 167200 is 7106, but actually, it's 172634. So my initial addition was wrong. I must have misaligned the digits. So the correct total is 172634. Therefore, both methods give the same result. So the product is 172634.\n\nBut why did I get 7106 before? Because I added 1254 + 4180 + 1672 as if they were all in the same place value, but actually, 167200 is a larger number. So the correct total is 172634.\n\nTherefore, the correct product is 172634.\n\nBut let me verify this with another method. Let's use the standard multiplication algorithm step by step.\n\nMultiplying 209 by 826:\n\nFirst, write 209 × 6 = 1254\n\nThen, write 209 × 20 = 4180 (shifted one position left)\n\nThen, write 209 × 800 = 167200 (shifted two positions left)\n\nNow, add them:\n\n1254\n\n+4180\n\n+167200\n\nLet'