output:  ['\n\nTo find the product of 174 and 374, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Multiply 174 by the units digit (4) of 374:\n   \\[\n   174 \\times 4 = 696\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 174 by the tens digit (7) of 374, shifting the result one position to the left:\n   \\[\n   174 \\times 7 = 1218 \\quad \\text{(shifted one position left becomes 12180)}\n   \\]\n\n3. Multiply 174 by the hundreds digit (3) of 374, shifting the result two positions to the left:\n   \\[\n   174 \\times 3 = 522 \\quad \\text{(shifted two positions left becomes 52200)}\n   \\]\n\n4. Add the three partial products together:\n   \\[\n   696 + 12180 + 52200 = 65076\n   \\]\n\nWe can also verify this using the distributive property:\n- Break down 374 into 300, 70, and 4.\n- Multiply 174 by each part and sum the results:\n  \\[\n  174 \\times 300 + 174 \\times 70 + 174 \\times 4 = 52200 + 12180 + 696 = 65076\n  \\]\n\nThus, the product of 174 and 374 is \\(\\boxed{65076}\\).', "\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 174 and 374. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers can be done by breaking them down into smaller parts or using some multiplication techniques. Let me think about the best way to approach this.\n\nFirst, maybe I can just use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's try that. So, 174 times 374. Let me write it out vertically to keep track:\n\n   174\nx 374\n-------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit of the second number, which is 4. Multiply 174 by 4. Let's do that:\n\n174 x 4: 100 x 4 = 400, 70 x 4 = 280, 4 x 4 = 16. Adding those together: 400 + 280 = 680, plus 16 is 696. So, the first partial product is 696.\n\nNext, multiply 174 by the next digit, which is 7. But wait, in the standard multiplication method, we start from the rightmost digit, so the next digit is 7 (tens place). So, we take the 7 and multiply it by 174, but we have to shift one position to the left because it's the tens place. So, 174 x 7. Let's calculate that:\n\n174 x 7: 100 x 7 = 700, 70 x 7 = 490, 4 x 7 = 28. Adding those: 700 + 490 = 1190, plus 28 is 1218. Then, since it's the tens place, we add a zero at the end, making it 12180.\n\nNow, the next digit is 3 (hundreds place). Multiply 174 by 3 and add it to the previous partial product, which is now shifted left by one more position (so total shift is two positions, making it 121800). Let's compute 174 x 3:\n\n174 x 3: 100 x 3 = 300, 70 x 3 = 210, 4 x 3 = 12. Adding those: 300 + 210 = 510, plus 12 is 522. Then add this to 121800: 121800 + 522 = 122322.\n\nWait, let me check that again. So, the first partial product was 696 (units place), then 12180 (tens place), then 122322 (hundreds place). Adding them together:\n\n696 + 12180 = 12876\n\nThen 12876 + 122322 = 135,198.\n\nWait, that seems a bit high. Let me verify with another method to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the distributive property. Let's break down 374 into 300 + 70 + 4. Then multiply each part by 174 and add them up.\n\nFirst, 174 x 300. That's 174 x 3 x 100 = 522 x 100 = 52,200.\n\nNext, 174 x 70. That's 174 x 7 x 10 = 1,218 x 10 = 12,180.\n\nThen, 174 x 4 = 696, as before.\n\nAdding those three results: 52,200 + 12,180 = 64,380. Then 64,380 + 696 = 65,076. Wait, that's different from the previous result of 135,198. Hmm, so which one is correct?\n\nWait, hold on. There's a mistake here. When I broke down 374 into 300 + 70 + 4, that's correct. But when I multiplied 174 x 300, that's 174*3*100. 174*3 is 522, so 522*100 is 52,200. Then 174*70 is 174*7*10. 174*7 is 1,218, so 1,218*10 is 12,180. Then 174*4 is 696. So adding 52,200 + 12,180 + 696. Let's compute that step by step.\n\n52,200 + 12,180: 52,200 + 12,000 = 64,200; 64,200 + 180 = 64,380.\n\nThen 64,380 + 696: 64,380 + 600 = 64,980; 64,980 + 96 = 65,076.\n\nBut earlier, using the standard multiplication, I got 135,198. These two results are different. That means I made a mistake somewhere. Let me check the standard multiplication again.\n\nOriginal problem: 174 x 374.\n\nFirst partial product: 174 x 4 = 696. Correct.\n\nSecond partial product: 174 x 7. Since it's the tens place, we need to shift left by one, so 174 x 7 = 1,218. Then add a zero: 12,180. Correct.\n\nThird partial product: 174 x 3. Hundreds place, so shift left by two, so 174 x 3 = 522. Then add two zeros: 52,200. Wait, but in the standard multiplication, when you shift left by two positions, you add two zeros. So 522 becomes 52,200. Then adding the three partial products:\n\n696 (units) + 12,180 (tens) + 52,200 (hundreds). Let's add them:\n\n696 + 12,180 = 12,876. Then 12,876 + 52,200 = 65,076. Wait, that's the same result as the other method! So earlier, when I thought the third partial product was 122,322, that was a mistake. I must have miscounted the shift.\n\nWait, in standard multiplication, when you multiply by the hundreds digit (3), you shift the partial product two places to the left, which is equivalent to multiplying by 100. So 174 x 3 = 522, then shift two places: 522 x 100 = 52,200. So that's correct. Then adding 52,200 + 12,180 + 696 = 65,076. So both methods give the same result. Therefore, the correct product is 65,076.\n\nBut wait, why did I get 135,198 earlier? Let me check that again. Maybe I added the partial products incorrectly. Let's see:\n\nFirst partial product: 696\n\nSecond partial product: 12,180\n\nThird partial product: 52,200\n\nAdding them:\n\n696 + 12,180 = 12,876\n\n12,876 + 52,200 = 65,076. Yes, that's correct. So my initial mistake was in the third partial product where I incorrectly shifted the second partial product (12,180) by two places instead of one. Wait, no. Wait, in the standard multiplication, when you multiply by the hundreds digit (third digit from the right), you shift the previous partial products by two more positions. Wait, let me clarify.\n\nWait, in the standard multiplication algorithm, when you multiply by the units digit (4), you get the first partial product. Then, when you multiply by the tens digit (7), you shift the first partial product one position to the left (making it two digits longer) and add the second partial product. Then, when you multiply by the hundreds digit (3), you shift the previous partial product two positions to the left (making it three digits longer) and add the third partial product. So, the third partial product is 52,200, which is 522 shifted two places left (522 -> 52,200). Therefore, adding 696 + 12,180 + 52,200 gives 65,076. So that's correct.\n\nBut when I first did the standard multiplication, I thought the third partial product was 122,322, which was wrong. So, the correct total is 65,076. Therefore, the product of 174 and 374 is 65,076.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using another method. Let's try using the difference of squares formula or some other algebraic identity. Wait, 174 and 374 don't seem to form a nice pair for difference of squares. Alternatively, maybe approximate the product.\n\nAlternatively, use calculator-like multiplication. Let's compute 174 x 374.\n\nAlternatively, break down 374 into 300 + 70 + 4, which we already did, and got 65,076. So that seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, use another multiplication method. Let's try multiplying 174 by 374 using the lattice method. Hmm, that might be more complicated, but let's try.\n\nBut maybe it's easier to just use the standard multiplication again, carefully.\n\nLet me write it out step by step:\n\nMultiply 174 by 374.\n\nStart with the units digit of 374, which is 4. Multiply 174 by 4:\n\n174\nx  4\n------\n696\n\nNext, multiply 174 by the tens digit of 374, which is 7. Since it's the tens place, we shift the result one position to the left:\n\n174\nx  7\n------\n1218\n------\n12180\n\nThen, multiply 174 by the hundreds digit of 374, which is 3. Shift the result two positions to the left:\n\n174\nx  3\n------\n522\n------\n52200\n\nNow, add the three partial products:\n\n696\n12180\n52200\n-------\n65076\n\nYes, that's the same result. So, the product is indeed 65,076. Therefore, the answer should be 65,076.\n\nBut just to be absolutely sure, let me verify using another approach. Let's compute 174 x 374 using the distributive property again, but more carefully.\n\nFirst, note that 374 = 300 + 70 + 4. So:\n\n174 x 374 = 174 x (300 + 70 + 4) = 174 x 300 + 174 x 70 + 174 x 4.\n\nCompute each term:\n\n174 x 300: 174 x 3 = 522, then 522 x 100 = 52,200.\n\n174 x 70: 174 x 7 = 1,218, then 1,218 x 10 = 12,180.\n\n174 x 4 = 696.\n\nAdding them together: 52,200 + 12,180 = 64,380; 64,380 + 696 = 65,076. Same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the standard multiplication with more attention to shifting:\n\n   174\nx 374\n-------\n   696  (174 x 4)\n 12180  (174 x 7, shifted one position left)\n 522000  (174 x 3, shifted two positions left)\n-------\n 65076\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, when you shift 174 x 3 two positions left, it's 522 x 100 = 52,200. But if I write it as 174 x 3 = 522, then shifted two positions becomes 52200. So, 52,200. Then adding 696 + 12,180 + 52,200 = 65,076. So that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, if I mistakenly shifted 174 x 3 by three positions, that would be 522000, but that's not the case here. The hundreds place is the third digit from the right, so shifting two positions. So, 522 becomes 52200. So, correct.\n\nTherefore, after multiple methods and checks, the product of 174 and 374 is 65,076. So, the answer should be 65076.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 174 and 374 is \\boxed{65076}.\n"] 65%|██████▍   | 323/500 [5:06:31<1:27:58, 29.82s/it]
