y this because sometimes when adding multiple numbers, it's easy to make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method to cross-check. Let's try adding the partial products another way. Let's add 6408 and 2136 first. \n\n6408 + 2136. Let's break it down:\n\n6000 + 2000 = 8000\n\n400 + 100 = 500\n\n0 + 30 = 30\n\n8 + 6 = 14\n\nAdding those: 8000 + 500 = 8500; 8500 + 30 = 8530; 8530 + 14 = 8544. So, 6408 + 2136 = 8544.\n\nNow, add 5696 to 8544. Let's do that:\n\n8544 + 5696. Breaking it down:\n\n8000 + 5000 = 13,000\n\n500 + 600 = 1,100\n\n40 + 90 = 130\n\n4 + 6 = 10\n\nAdding those: 13,000 + 1,100 = 14,100; 14,100 + 130 = 14,230; 14,230 + 10 = 14,240. Okay, same result. So, that seems correct.\n\nWait, but let me check with another method. Maybe using the distributive property. Let's see. 712 \u00d7 839 can be broken down into (700 + 12) \u00d7 (800 + 30 + 9). Let's expand this:\n\nFirst, 700 \u00d7 800 = 560,000\n\n700 \u00d7 30 = 21,000\n\n700 \u00d7 9 = 6,300\n\n12 \u00d7 800 = 9,600\n\n12 \u00d7 30 = 360\n\n12 \u00d7 9 = 108\n\nNow, add all these together:\n\n560,000 + 21,000 = 581,000\n\n581,000 + 6,300 = 587,300\n\n587,300 + 9,600 = 596,900\n\n596,900 + 360 = 597,260\n\n597,260 + 108 = 597,368\n\nWait, so according to this method, the product is 597,368. But earlier, the standard multiplication gave me 14,240. That's way off. Wait, that can't be right. There must be a mistake here. Let me check my breakdown again.\n\nWait, hold on. The problem is that when I broke down 712 into 700 + 12, and 839 into 800 + 30 + 9, then multiplied each part, but when adding them up, I might have made a mistake. Let me check each multiplication step again.\n\nFirst, 700 \u00d7 800: 7 \u00d7 8 = 56, so 56,000. Then 700 \u00d7 30: 7 \u00d7 3 = 21, so 2,100. 700 \u00d7 9: 7 \u00d7 9 = 63, so 6,300. Then 12 \u00d7 800: 12 \u00d7 8 = 96, so 9,600. 12 \u00d7 30: 12 \u00d7 3 = 36, so 360. 12 \u00d7 9: 12 \u00d7 9 = 108. \n\nAdding all these: 56,000 + 2,100 = 58,100; 58,100