dd two zeros: 160800)\n- 402 * 8 = 3216 (thousands place, add three zeros: 3216000)\n\nBut when adding these, the fourth partial product is 3216000, which is a 7-digit number. However, the other partial products are 1206 (3 digits), 16080 (5 digits), 160800 (6 digits). So when adding, we need to align them properly:\n\n1206\n\n16080\n\n160800\n\n3216000\n\nBut when adding, we need to consider the place values. Let's write each partial product with their place values:\n\n1206: 1,206\n\n16080: 16,080\n\n160800: 160,800\n\n3,216,000: 3,216,000\n\nBut when adding, we need to add each corresponding digit:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n6 (from 1206) + 0 (from 16080) + 0 (from 160800) + 0 (from 3,216,000) = 6\n\nTens place:\n\n0 (from 1206) + 8 (from 16080) + 0 (from 160800) + 0 (from 3,216,000) = 8\n\nHundreds place:\n\n2 (from 1206) + 0 (from 16080) + 8 (from 160800) + 0 (from 3,216,000) = 10\n\nThousands place:\n\n1 (from 1206) + 6 (from 16080) + 0 (from 160800) + 6 (from 3,216,000) = 13\n\nTen thousands place:\n\n0 (from 1206) + 1 (from 16080) + 0 (from 160800) + 0 (from 3,216,000) = 1\n\nHundred thousands place:\n\n0 (from 1206) + 0 (from 16080) + 1 (from 160800) + 0 (from 3,216,000) = 1\n\nMillions place:\n\n0 (from 1206) + 0 (from 16080) + 0 (from 160800) + 3 (from 3,216,000) = 3\n\nNow, adding these together:\n\nStart with the units: 6\n\nTens: 8\n\nHundreds: 10\n\nThousands: 13\n\nTen thousands: 1\n\nHundred thousands: 1\n\nMillions: 3\n\nAdding step by step:\n\n6 + 8 = 14\n\n14 + 10 = 24\n\n24 + 13 = 37\n\n37 + 1 = 38\n\n38 + 1 = 39\n\n39 + 3 = 42\n\nWait, this is giving 42, which is not possible. Clearly, this approach is flawed. The mistake here is that when adding the partial products, we need to consider their place values correctly. However, the confusion arises because the partial products are of different lengths. To resolve this, perhaps we should use the fact that each partial product corresponds to a different place value and add them