at seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using another method. Let's see. Maybe using the distributive property differently.\n\nAlternatively, 779 x 620 can be thought of as (700 + 70 + 9) x (600 + 20). But that might complicate things. Alternatively, maybe use the fact that 620 is 600 + 20, which is what I did before.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the standard multiplication algorithm step by step.\n\nLet me try that again.\n\nMultiplying 779 by 620:\n\nFirst, write 779 x 620.\n\nStart with the units digit of 620, which is 0. Multiply 779 by 0. That's 0. Write that down.\n\nNext, the tens digit of 620 is 2. Multiply 779 by 2. Let's compute that:\n\n779 x 2:\n\n700 x 2 = 1400\n\n70 x 2 = 140\n\n9 x 2 = 18\n\nAdding those: 1400 + 140 = 1540 + 18 = 1558. So write 1558.\n\nNow, the hundreds digit of 620 is 6. Multiply 779 by 6:\n\n700 x 6 = 4200\n\n70 x 6 = 420\n\n9 x 6 = 54\n\nAdding those: 4200 + 420 = 4620 + 54 = 4674. Write that down.\n\nNow, add the three partial products:\n\nFirst partial product: 0\n\nSecond partial product shifted left by one: 15580\n\nThird partial product shifted left by two: 467400\n\nAdding them:\n\n0\n\n+15580\n\n+467400\n\n= 482,980\n\nYes, same result. So that seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use a calculator to verify. But since I don't have a calculator here, let me check with another approach.\n\nAlternatively, break down 779 into 700 + 70 + 9 and 620 into 600 + 20, then use distributive property.\n\nSo, (700 + 70 + 9) x (600 + 20) = 700*600 + 700*20 + 70*600 + 70*20 + 9*600 + 9*20.\n\nCompute each term:\n\n700*600 = 420,000\n\n700*20 = 14,000\n\n70*600 = 42,000\n\n70*20 = 1,400\n\n9*600 = 5,400\n\n9*20 = 180\n\nNow, add all these together:\n\n420,000 + 14,000 = 434,000\n\n434,000 + 42,000 = 476,000\n\n476,000 + 1,400 = 477,400\n\n477,400 + 5,400 = 482,800\n\n482,800 + 180 = 482,980\n\nSame result! So that's a good confirmation. So regardless of the method, the product is 482,980.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be 48