=323,600\n\n809*6=4,854\n\nAdding them: 323,600 + 4,854 = 328,454. \n\nSame result. So that's correct. \n\nWait, but the problem says \"What is the Product of 809 and 406?\" So the answer is 328,454. But let me check once more with another method. \n\nAlternatively, use the standard multiplication step-by-step:\n\nMultiply 809 by 6:\n\n809\nx  6\n------\n4854\n\nMultiply 809 by 0 (tens place):\n\n809\nx  0\n------\n0000\n\nMultiply 809 by 4 (hundreds place):\n\n809\nx  4\n------\n3236\n\nNow, add them up:\n\n4854\n+0000\n+3236\n-------\n4854 + 3236 = 8090\n\nWait, 4854 + 3236: 4000 + 3000 = 7000, 800 + 200 = 1000, 54 + 36 = 90. So total is 7000 + 1000 = 8000 + 90 = 8090. Then add the 0000, which is still 8090. Wait, that can't be right. Wait, no. Wait, when you shift the second line (809*4) two positions to the left, it's 323600, not 3236. Wait, no, in the standard multiplication, when you multiply by the tens digit (0 in this case), you shift left once, so 809*4=3236, then shift left once: 32360. But in the problem above, I wrote 323600, which is shifting two positions. Wait, maybe I messed up the shifting. \n\nWait, let's clarify. When multiplying by the units digit (6), you don't shift. When multiplying by the tens digit (0), you shift one position. When multiplying by the hundreds digit (4), you shift two positions. So:\n\nFirst, multiply 809 by 6 (units digit): 4854, no shift.\n\nSecond, multiply 809 by 0 (tens digit): 0, shifted one position: 0000.\n\nThird, multiply 809 by 4 (hundreds digit): 3236, shifted two positions: 323600.\n\nThen add them up:\n\n4854 (units)\n+0000 (tens)\n+323600 (hundreds)\n= 4854 + 323600 = 328454\n\nYes, that's correct. So the product is 328,454. \n\nBut wait, the problem is presented as \"What is the Product of 809 and 406?\" So the answer is 328,454. But let me check with a calculator to be absolutely sure. \n\nAlternatively, maybe use another decomposition. Let's see. \n\n809 can be written as 800 + 9, and 406 as 400 + 6. Then, 