ace values. So:\n\n  306,000\n\n+  27,200\n\n+   2,720\n\nBut when adding, the rightmost digits are:\n\n306,000: 3 0 6 0 0 0\n\n27,200: 0 2 7 2 0 0\n\n2,720: 0 0 2 7 2 0\n\nWait, no. Wait, actually, when you multiply by the digits, the partial products are shifted left by one digit each time. So:\n\nMultiplying by 8 (units place) gives 2,720, which is four digits.\n\nMultiplying by 80 (tens place) gives 27,200, which is five digits.\n\nMultiplying by 900 (hundreds place) gives 306,000, which is six digits.\n\nTherefore, when adding, you need to align them as:\n\n  306,000\n\n+  27,200\n\n+   2,720\n\nBut when adding, the digits are:\n\n306,000: 3 0 6 0 0 0\n\n27,200: 0 2 7 2 0 0\n\n2,720: 0 0 2 7 2 0\n\nWait, but that's not correct. Wait, actually, when you multiply by the tens digit (80), the partial product is 27,200, which is 27,200. But when you shift it left by one digit (because it's the tens place), it becomes 272,000. Similarly, multiplying by the hundreds digit (900) gives 306,000, which is already shifted left by two digits. Wait, maybe I'm confusing the alignment.\n\nWait, perhaps a better way is to write each partial product with proper alignment:\n\n- The first partial product (units place): 2,720 (shifted left 0 digits)\n\n- The second partial product (tens place): 27,200 (shifted left 1 digit)\n\n- The third partial product (hundreds place): 306,000 (shifted left 2 digits)\n\nTherefore, when adding:\n\n  306,000\n\n+  27,200\n\n+   2,720\n\nBut when adding, the digits are:\n\n306,000: 3 0 6 0 0 0\n\n27,200: 0 2 7 2 0 0\n\n2,720: 0 0 2 7 2 0\n\nWait, but that's not correct. Wait, actually, when you shift left by one digit for the tens place, the partial product 27,200 becomes 272,000. Similarly, shifting left by two digits for the hundreds place, 306,000 is already shifted. So when adding:\n\n306,000\n\n+ 272,000\n\n+   2,720\n\nBut 306,000 + 272,000 = 578,000. Then 578,000 + 2,720 = 580,720. Wait, that's different. Hmm, now I'm confused. There must be