\u00d7 623 using another approach. Let's use the fact that 623 = 600 + 20 + 3, so:\n\n845 \u00d7 600 = 50700\n\n845 \u00d7 20 = 16900\n\n845 \u00d7 3 = 2535\n\nAdding them: 50700 + 16900 = 67600; 67600 + 2535 = 70135.\n\nWait, so now there's a conflict between the two methods. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, this is a problem. There must be a miscalculation in one of the steps. Let me check 845 \u00d7 600 again.\n\n845 \u00d7 600: 845 \u00d7 6 = 5070, so 5070 \u00d7 10 = 50700. Correct.\n\n845 \u00d7 20: 845 \u00d7 2 = 1690, so 1690 \u00d7 10 = 16900. Correct.\n\n845 \u00d7 3: 2535. Correct.\n\nSo 50700 + 16900 = 67600. Then 67600 + 2535 = 70135. But according to the partial products method, it's 520135. So which is correct?\n\nWait, perhaps the error is in the partial products method. Let me check the partial products again.\n\nFirst partial product: 845 \u00d7 3 = 2535. Correct.\n\nSecond partial product: 845 \u00d7 2 = 1690, shifted left by 1: 16900. Correct.\n\nThird partial product: 845 \u00d7 6 = 5070, shifted left by 2: 507000. Correct.\n\nAdding them: 2535 + 16900 = 19435. Then 19435 + 507000 = 520135. Hmm.\n\nBut when I compute 845 \u00d7 623 as 845 \u00d7 (600 + 20 + 3) = 50700 + 16900 + 2535 = 70135. So which is correct?\n\nWait, perhaps the error is in the partial products method. Let me check the addition again.\n\n2535 + 16900:\n\n2535 + 16900. Let's add 2535 + 16900.\n\n2535 + 16900. Let's add 2535 + 16900:\n\n2535 + 16900. Let's add 2535 + 16900:\n\n2535 + 16900. Let's break it down:\n\n2535 + 16000 = 18535\n\n18535 + 900 = 19435. Correct.\n\nThen 19435 + 507000:\n\n19435 + 507000. Let's add 19435 + 507000.\n\n19435 + 507000. Let's add 19435 + 507000:\n\n19435 + 507000. Let's add 19435 + 507000:\n\n19435 + 507000. Let's add 19435 + 507000:\n\n19435 + 507000. Let's add 19435 + 507000:\n\n19435 + 507000. Let's add 19435 + 507000:\n\n19435 + 507000. Let's add 19435 + 507000:\n\n19435 + 507000. Let's add 19435 + 507000:\n\n19435 + 507000. Let's add 19435 