 40×300 = 12,000\n\n40×70 = 2,800\n\n40×7 = 280\n\nNext, 6×300 = 1,800\n\n6×70 = 420\n\n6×7 = 42\n\nNow, let's add all these results together:\n\n30,000 + 7,000 = 37,000\n\n37,000 + 700 = 37,700\n\n37,700 + 12,000 = 49,700\n\n49,700 + 2,800 = 52,500\n\n52,500 + 280 = 52,780\n\n52,780 + 1,800 = 54,580\n\n54,580 + 420 = 55,000\n\n55,000 + 42 = 55,042\n\nWait, so adding all those up gives me 55,042? Let me check that again because that seems like a big number, and I might have made a mistake in the addition.\n\nStarting over:\n\nFirst, 30,000 + 7,000 = 37,000\n\n37,000 + 700 = 37,700\n\n37,700 + 12,000 = 49,700\n\n49,700 + 2,800 = 52,500\n\n52,500 + 280 = 52,780\n\n52,780 + 1,800 = 54,580\n\n54,580 + 420 = 55,000\n\n55,000 + 42 = 55,042\n\nHmm, that seems correct. But let me verify using another method to be sure. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm.\n\nLet's multiply 146 by 377 step by step.\n\nFirst, multiply 146 by 7:\n\n146 × 7 = 1,022\n\nThen, multiply 146 by 70 (which is 7 × 10):\n\n146 × 70 = 10,220\n\nThen, multiply 146 by 300 (which is 3 × 100):\n\n146 × 300 = 43,800\n\nNow, add those three results together:\n\n1,022 + 10,220 = 11,242\n\n11,242 + 43,800 = 55,042\n\nOkay, same result. So that's reassuring. So the product is 55,042.\n\nWait, but let me check once more with another approach. Maybe using the difference of squares or something? Hmm, not sure if that applies here. Alternatively, maybe approximate the numbers to check.\n\n146 is approximately 150, and 377 is approximately 380. So 150 × 380 = 57,000. But since both numbers are a bit less than 150 and 380, the actual product should be a bit less than 57,000. 55,042 is indeed less than 57,000, so that seems reasonable.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the formula (a + b)(c + d) = ac + ad + bc + bd. Wait, that's similar to what I did before. Let me try that.\n\nLet me write 146 as 100 + 46 and 377 as 300 + 77. Then:\n\n(100 + 46)(300 + 77) = 100×300 + 100×77 + 46×300 + 46×77\n\nCompute each term