pancy of 1,000. Therefore, one of the methods is wrong. But which one?\n\nWait, perhaps the expansion is incorrect. Let me check the original problem again. The original problem is 739 \u00d7 289. Let me compute 739 \u00d7 289 using another method, such as the difference of squares.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the fact that 289 is 17\u00b2, so 739 \u00d7 17\u00b2. But that might not help. Alternatively, compute 739 \u00d7 289 directly.\n\nAlternatively, use the method of multiplying 739 by 289:\n\nLet me write it out step by step:\n\nFirst, write 739\n\nMultiply by 289:\n\nStart with the rightmost digit: 9\n\n739 \u00d7 9 = 6,651\n\nShift left by one: 66,510\n\nNext digit: 8\n\n739 \u00d7 8 = 5,912\n\nShift left by one: 59,120\n\nNext digit: 2\n\n739 \u00d7 2 = 1,478\n\nShift left by two: 147,800\n\nAdd them up:\n\n66,510 + 59,120 = 125,630\n\n125,630 + 147,800 = 273,430\n\nWait, that's different. Wait, no, this is incorrect. Wait, no, when you shift left by one, the first partial product is 66,510, then 59,120 shifted left by one is 59,120 \u00d7 10 = 591,200? Wait, no, no. Wait, when you shift left by one, you multiply by 10. So 739 \u00d7 8 = 5,912, then shifted left by one is 5,912 \u00d7 10 = 59,120. Similarly, 739 \u00d7 2 = 1,478, shifted left by two is 1,478 \u00d7 100 = 147,800. Therefore, adding 6,651 + 59,120 + 147,800 = 213,571. So that's correct.\n\nBut when I tried to do the expansion, I got 212,571. Therefore, the expansion must be wrong. But how?\n\nWait, perhaps the expansion is incorrect. Let me check the expansion again. Let me compute (700 + 39) \u00d7 (200 + 80 + 9) again.\n\nFirst, 700 \u00d7 200 = 140,000\n\n700 \u00d7 80 = 56,000\n\n700 \u00d7 9 = 6,300\n\n39 \u00d7 200 = 7,800\n\n39 \u00d7 80 = 3,120\n\n39 \u00d7 9 = 351\n\nAdding them up:\n\n140,000 + 56,000 = 196,000\n\n196,000 + 6,300 = 202,300\n\n202,300 + 7,800 = 209,100\n\n209,100 + 3,120 = 212,220\n\n212,220 + 351 = 212,571\n\nBut according to standard multiplication, it's 213,571. Th