6,000 + 500 is 226,500, plus 65 is 226,565. That seems right. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method to verify. Let's try multiplying 401 and 565 directly using the standard algorithm. \n\nStarting with the units place: \n\nFirst, multiply 401 by 5. \n\n401 \u00d7 5. \n\n5 \u00d7 1 = 5. \n\n5 \u00d7 0 = 0. \n\n5 \u00d7 4 = 20. \n\nSo, writing that down: 2000 + 0 + 5 = 2005. \n\nBut since we're multiplying by 565, which is a three-digit number, we need to shift the result one place to the left for each digit beyond the units place. \n\nSo, 401 \u00d7 5 = 2005. \n\nNext, multiply 401 by 60 (the tens place of 565). \n\n401 \u00d7 60. \n\n401 \u00d7 6 = 2406. \n\nThen add a zero at the end: 24060. \n\nNow, shift this result one place to the left because we're dealing with the tens place. So, 24060 becomes 240600. \n\nThen, multiply 401 by 500 (the hundreds place of 565). \n\n401 \u00d7 500. \n\n401 \u00d7 5 = 2005. \n\nAdd two zeros: 200500. \n\nShift this two places to the left because we're dealing with the hundreds place. So, 200500 becomes 20050000. \n\nNow, add all three results together: \n\nFirst result: 2005 (units place) \n\nSecond result: 240600 (tens place) \n\nThird result: 20050000 (hundreds place) \n\nAdding them up step by step. \n\nStart with 2005 + 240600. \n\n2005 + 240600 = 242,605. \n\nThen add 2,005,000 to that. \n\n242,605 + 2,005,000. \n\nLet's break it down: 2,005,000 + 200,000 = 2,205,000. \n\nThen add the remaining 42,605: 2,205,000 + 42,605 = 2,247,605. \n\nWait, that's different from the previous result of 226,565. Hmm, that's a problem. \n\nWait, hold on. Maybe I messed up the shifting in the standard multiplication method. Let me check again. \n\nWhen multiplying by the tens place (60), the result should be shifted one place to the left, so 24060 becomes 240600. \n\nWhen multiplying by the hundreds place (500), shifted two places to the left, so 200500 becomes 20050000. \n\nBut when adding them, 2005 + 240600 + 20050000. \n\nWait