git is 5 in the tens place, so we multiply by 5 and shift left by one):\n\n110\n\nx 50\n\n= 5500\n\nShift left by one: 55000? Wait, no. Wait, when you multiply by the tens place (5), you have to multiply 110 by 5 and then shift the result one place to the left. So 110 x 5 = 550, then shift left by one: 5500. So that's correct.\n\nThen, multiply 110 by 400 (since the next digit is 4 in the hundreds place, so multiply by 4 and shift left by two):\n\n110\n\nx 400\n\n= 44000\n\nShift left by two: 440000? Wait, no. Wait, multiplying by the hundreds place (4), so 110 x 4 = 440, then shift left by two places: 44000. So that's correct.\n\nNow, adding the three partial products:\n\n770 (from 7)\n\n+ 5500 (from 50)\n\n+ 44000 (from 400)\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, in the standard multiplication, when you multiply by the hundreds digit, you shift left by two, so 44000. But in my previous calculation, I added 770 + 5500 + 440. But 440 is from 110 x 4, which is shifted left by two, so 44000. But in the breakdown, I wrote 440. So that's the mistake!\n\nAh! There's the error. In the standard multiplication, when you multiply by the hundreds digit (4), you have to shift the result two places to the left, so 44000, not 440. Therefore, the third partial product should be 44000, not 440. Therefore, adding them up:\n\n770 + 5500 + 44000.\n\nLet's compute that.\n\nFirst, 770 + 5500 = 6270.\n\nThen, 6270 + 44000 = 50270.\n\nWhich matches the breakdown result. So my initial mistake was in the standard multiplication step where I wrote 440 instead of 44000. That's why the two methods gave different results. So the correct product is 50270.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 50270.\n\nBut wait, let me confirm once again. Let's compute 110 x 457 using another method. Let's use the multiplication of 110 by 457.\n\nAlternatively, we can compute 110 x 457 as follows:\n\nFirst, compute 100 x 457 = 45700.\n\nThen, compute 10 x 457 = 4570.\n\nThen, add them together: 45700 + 4570 = 50270.\n\nYes, that's correct. So the correct product is 50270.\n\nTherefore, my initial mistake was in the standard multiplication step where I incorrectly shifted the 440 by only one place instead of two. That's why the two methods gave different results. Once I corrected that, both methods agree.\n\nSo the correct answer is 50270.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 110 and 457 is \\boxed{50270}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 110 and 457, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Multiply 110 by the units digit (7):\n   \\[\n   110 \\times 7 = 770\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 110 by the tens digit (5) and shift the result one place to the left:\n   \\[\n   110 \\times 5 = 550 \\quad \\text{Shifted left by one place:} \\quad 5500\n   \\]\n\n3. Multiply 110 by the hundreds digit (4) and shift the result two places to the left:\n   \\[\n   110 \\times 4 = 440 \\quad \\text{Shifted left by two places:} \\quad 44000\n   \\]\n\n4. Add the partial products together:\n   \\[\n   770 + 5500 + 44000 = 50270\n   \\]\n\nWe can also verify this by breaking down 110 into 100 + 10:\n- \\(100 \\times 457 = 45700\\)\n- \\(10 \\times 457 = 4570\\)\n\n5. Add the results of these multiplications:\n   \\[\n   45700 + 4570 = 50270\n   \\]\n\nThus, the product of 110 and 457 is \\(\\boxed{50270}\\).",
