ightmost digits: 3 + 9 = 12. Carry over 1. Next digit: 7 + 1 = 8, plus the carried 1 is 9. Next digit: 4 + 4 = 8. Next digit: 1 + 9 = 10, plus the carried 1 is 11. So writing that out: 1192. Wait, that can't be right. Wait, no. Wait, 1473 + 4419. Let me write it vertically:\n\n  1473\n+4419\n-------\n  5892\n\nYes, that's correct. Because 1473 + 4419: 1473 + 4000 = 5473, then 5473 + 419 = 5892. So the total product is 5892. \n\nBut let me verify this with another method to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Maybe using the distributive property. \n\n491 x 921 can be written as 491 x (900 + 20 + 1). Let's compute each part:\n\n491 x 900. 491 x 9 = 4419, so 4419 x 100 = 441900.\n\n491 x 20. 491 x 2 = 982, so 982 x 10 = 9820.\n\n491 x 1 = 491.\n\nNow add them all together: 441900 + 9820 + 491.\n\nFirst, add 441900 and 9820. 441900 + 9820. Let's break it down: 441900 + 9000 = 531900, then 531900 + 820 = 532720.\n\nThen add 491 to 532720. 532720 + 491. Let's do 532720 + 400 = 533120, then 533120 + 91 = 533211. Wait, that's different from the previous result of 5892. Hmm, that can't be. There must be a mistake here. \n\nWait, hold on. Wait, 491 x 900 is 441900. Then 491 x 20 is 9820. Then 491 x 1 is 491. So adding those three numbers: 441900 + 9820 + 491. \n\nWait, 441900 + 9820: Let's compute 441900 + 9820. \n\n441900 + 9820. Let's add 441900 + 9000 = 531900, then 531900 + 820 = 532720. Then 532720 + 491. \n\n532720 + 491. Let's break it down: 532720 + 400 = 533120, then 533120 + 91 = 533211. \n\nBut earlier, using the standard multiplication, I got 5892. That's a discrepancy. Which one is correct? \n\nWait, 5892 vs 533211. Clearly, 533211 is way too big. So I must have messed up the second method. Let me check again. \n\nWait, 491 x 900. 491 x 9 is 4419, so 4419 x 100 is 441900. That's correct. \n\n491 x 20. 491 x 2 is 982, so 982 x 10 is 9820. Correct. \n\n491 x 1 is 491. Correct. \n\nSo adding 441900 + 9820 + 491. \n\nWait, 441900 + 9820: Let's compute 441900 + 9820. \n\n441900 + 9820. Let's add 441900 + 9000 = 531900. Then 531900 + 820 = 532720. Then 532720 + 491. \n\n532720 + 491. Let's do 532720 + 400 = 533120. Then 533120 + 91 = 533211. \n\nBut when I multiplied using the standard algorithm, I got 5892. That's way off. So there's a mistake here. \n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the standard multiplication. Let me check that again. \n\nOriginal multiplication:\n\n      491\n    x 921\n    --------\n\nFirst step: 491 x 1 = 491. \n\nSecond step: 491 x 2 = 982. \n\nThird step: 491 x 9 = 4419. \n\nThen adding them: 491 + 982 + 4419. \n\nWait, 491 + 982 is 1473. Then 1473 + 4419. Let's compute that again. \n\n1473 + 4419. \n\nStarting from the right: 3 + 9 = 12, write down 2, carry over 1. \n\n7 + 1 = 8, plus 1 carried is 9. \n\n4 + 4 = 8. \n\n1 + 9 = 10, plus the carried 1 is 11. \n\nSo the total is 1192. Wait, that's not right. Wait, 1473 + 4419. \n\nWait, 1473 + 4419. Let's do 1473 + 4000 = 5473. Then 5473 + 419. \n\n5473 + 400 = 5873. \n\n5873 + 19 = 5892. \n\nAh, okay, so that's correct. So the standard multiplication gives 5892, but the distributive method gives 533211. That's a problem. \n\nWait, so which one is correct? Let me check with another method. Maybe using the difference of squares or something. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake in the distributive approach. Let me check 491 x 921. \n\nWait, 921 is 900 + 20 + 1. So 491 x 900 = 441900. 491 x 20 = 9820. 491 x 1 = 491. Then adding them: 441900 + 9820 = 451,720? Wait, wait, 441,900 + 9,820. Let's compute 441,900 + 9,820. \n\n441,900 + 9,820. \n\n441,900 + 9,000 = 531,900. \n\n531,900 + 820 = 532,720. \n\nThen 532,720 + 491 = 533,211. \n\nBut that's way too big. So clearly, there's a mistake here. \n\nWait, but 491 x 921. Let me compute it directly. \n\nLet me try multiplying 491 x 921 using the standard algorithm again, step by step. \n\nFirst, write 491 x 921:\n\n      491\n    x 921\n    --------\n\nStart with the rightmost digit of 921, which is 1. Multiply 491 by 1: 491. \n\nNext, the middle digit is 2. Multiply 491 by 2: 982. \n\nNext, the leftmost digit is 9. Multiply 491 by 9: 4419. \n\nNow, add these three results: 491 + 982 + 4419. \n\nLet's compute 491 + 982 first. \n\n491 + 982. \n\n400 + 900 = 1300. \n\n90 + 80 = 170. \n\n1 + 2 = 3. \n\n1300 + 170 = 1470. \n\n1470 + 3 = 1473. \n\nThen add 4419 to 1473. \n\n1473 + 4419. \n\n1000 + 4000 = 5000. \n\n400 + 400 = 800. \n\n70 + 10 = 80. \n\n3 + 9 = 12. \n\nWait, no. Wait, 1473 + 4419. Let's do it digit by digit:\n\nThousands place: 1 (from 1473) + 4 (from 4419) = 5.\n\nHundreds place: 4 (from 1473) + 4 (from 4419) = 8.\n\nTens place: 7 (from 1473) + 1 (from 4419) = 8.\n\nOnes place: 3 (from 1473) + 9 (from 4419) = 12.\n\nSo writing that out: 5 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 12 (ones). But since we can't have two digits in the ones place, we carry over 1 to the tens place. So the tens place becomes 8 + 1 = 9, and the ones place is 2. So the total is 5892. \n\nSo the standard multiplication gives 5892. \n\nBut when I did the distributive method, I got 533211. That's way off. So why is there a discrepancy? \n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the distributive approach. Let me check 491 x 921 again. \n\nWait, 921 is 900 + 20 + 1. So 491 x 900 = 441900. 491 x 20 = 9820. 491 x 1 = 491. Then adding them: 441900 + 9820 + 491. \n\nWait, 441900 + 9820 is 441900 + 9820. Let's compute 441900 + 9820. \n\n441900 + 9820. \n\n441900 + 9000 = 531900. \n\n531900 + 820 = 532720. \n\nThen 532720 + 491. \n\n532720 + 491. \n\n532720 + 400 = 533120. \n\n533120 + 91 = 533211. \n\nBut that's not possible. So clearly, there's a mistake here. \n\nWait, but 491 x 921. Let me check with another method. Let's use the fact that 921 = 900 + 21. So 491 x 900 = 441900. 491 x 21. Let's compute 491 x 21. \n\n491 x 20 = 9820. \n\n491 x 1 = 491. \n\nSo 9820 + 491 = 10311. \n\nThen total is 441900 + 10311 = 452211. \n\nWait, that's different again. Now 452211. \n\nWait, this is confusing. Let me