= 18, write 8, carry 1.\n\n1 \u00d7 3 = 3 + 1 = 4.\n\n9 \u00d7 3 = 27.\n\nSo 2748.\n\nNext, multiply 916 by 1 (tens place), shifted one position left:\n\n916 \u00d7 1 = 916, shifted left by one: 9160.\n\nThen, multiply 916 by 5 (hundreds place), shifted two positions left:\n\n916 \u00d7 5 = 4580, shifted left by two: 45800.\n\nNow, add them:\n\n2748\n\n+9160\n\n+45800\n\nLet's add 2748 + 9160:\n\n2748 + 9160. Let's compute:\n\n2748 + 9160. Let's add 2748 + 9000 = 11748, then add 160: 11748 + 160 = 11908.\n\nThen add 11908 + 45800:\n\n11908 + 45800. Let's compute:\n\n11908 + 45800. Let's add 11908 + 40000 = 51908, then add 5800: 51908 + 5800 = 57708.\n\nSo total is 57,708. But earlier, when I thought the answer was 16,488, I must have added the partial products incorrectly. So why the discrepancy?\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the initial partial products. Let me check 916 \u00d7 513 again using another method. Let's use the distributive property.\n\n513 can be written as 500 + 13.\n\nSo 916 \u00d7 513 = 916 \u00d7 (500 + 13) = 916 \u00d7 500 + 916 \u00d7 13.\n\nCompute 916 \u00d7 500:\n\n916 \u00d7 500 = 916 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 100 = 4580 \u00d7 100 = 458,000.\n\nCompute 916 \u00d7 13:\n\n916 \u00d7 10 = 9,160\n\n916 \u00d7 3 = 2,748\n\nSo 9,160 + 2,748 = 11,908.\n\nNow, add 458,000 + 11,908 = 469,908.\n\nWait, that's different again. Now I'm getting 469,908. But that's even more different. So clearly, there's a problem here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check with a calculator. Let me compute 916 \u00d7 513.\n\nUsing calculator: 916 \u00d7 513.\n\nLet me compute 916 \u00d7 500 = 458,000\n\n916 \u00d7 13 = 11,908\n\nTotal: 458,000 + 11,908 = 469,908.\n\nBut according to the previous step-by-step multiplication, we have 469,908. But when I added the partial products as 2748 + 9160 + 45800, I got 57,708. That's way off. So clearly, there's a mistake in how I was adding the partial products. Wait, but how?\n\nWait, perhaps I confused the partial products. Let me check again.\n\nWait, when multiplying 916 by 513, the partial products should be:\n\nFirst, 916 \u00d7 3 = 2748.\n\nSecond, 916 \u00d7 1 (tens place) = 916, shifted left by one: 9160.\n\nThird, 916 \u00d7 5 (hundreds place) = 4580, shifted left by two: 45800.\n\nSo adding 2748 + 9160 + 45800.\n\nBut 2748 + 9160 is 11,908, and 11,908 + 45,800 is 57,708. But according to the other method, it's 469,908. So clearly, there's a mistake here. Wait, but how?\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the shifting. Let me check the shifting again.\n\nWhen multiplying by the tens digit (1), which is in the second position (tens place), we shift the partial product one place to the left. So 916 \u00d7 1 = 916, shifted left by one: 9160.\n\nSimilarly, when multiplying by the hundreds digit (5), which is in the third position (hundreds place), we shift the partial product two places to the left. So 916 \u00d7 5 = 4580, shifted left by two: 45800.\n\nTherefore, adding 2748 + 9160 + 45800.\n\nBut 2748 + 9160 is 11,908, and 11,908 + 45,800 is 57,708. But according to the other method, it's 469,908. So there's a conflict here. Therefore, one of the methods is wrong.\n\nWait, but when I used the distributive property, breaking down 513 into 500 + 13, and then multiplying, I got 469,908. Let's check that again.\n\n916 \u00d7 500 = 458,000.\n\n916 \u00d7 13:\n\nFirst, 916 \u00d7 10 = 9,160.\n\n916 \u00d7 3 = 2,748.\n\nAdding them: 9,160 + 2,748 = 11,908.\n\nTotal: 458,000 + 11,908 = 469,908.\n\nSo that's correct. Therefore, the correct product is 469,908. But why is the partial product method giving me 57,708? That's way off. So clearly, I made a mistake in the partial product method.\n\nWait, perhaps I confused the digits. Let me check the original multiplication.\n\nWait, 916 \u00d7 513. Let's write it out properly:\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0916\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00d7\u00a0\u00a0513\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0----------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n1. 