e the lattice method or prime factorization.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the calculator method. Wait, but since I don't have a calculator here, let me try another approach.\n\nAlternatively, let's compute 117 * 563.\n\nLet me write it out vertically:\n\n    117\n\n  x 563\n\n------------\n\nFirst, multiply 117 by 3:\n\n117\n\n  3\n\n-----\n\n351\n\nThen, multiply 117 by 60 (shifted one position):\n\n  7020\n\nThen, multiply 117 by 500 (shifted two positions):\n\n 585000\n\nNow, add them:\n\n  585000\n\n  7020\n\n  351\n\n------------\n\nLet's add 585000 + 7020 first. 585000 + 7000 = 592000; 592000 + 20 = 592020. Then add 351: 592020 + 351 = 592,371. Wait, that's different again. Wait, no, that can't be. Wait, 585,000 + 7,020 = 592,020. Then 592,020 + 351 = 592,371. But that's not matching either.\n\nWait, this is getting confusing. Let me check with another method. Let's use the distributive property.\n\n117 * 563 = (100 + 17) * (500 + 60 + 3)\n\nBut expanding this would be tedious. Alternatively, maybe use the fact that 563 = 500 + 60 + 3, so:\n\n117 * 563 = 117*500 + 117*60 + 117*3 = 58,500 + 7,020 + 351 = 65,871. That's the first method. So that's correct.\n\nBut why does the second method give 38,961? Because when you shift the partial products, you have to consider the place values correctly. Wait, perhaps the second method is not the standard multiplication algorithm. Let me check.\n\nWait, when you multiply 117 by 563, you can also think of it as:\n\nFirst, multiply 117 by 500: 58,500.\n\nThen, multiply 117 by 60: 7,020.\n\nThen, multiply 117 by 3: 351.\n\nThen, add them all together: 58,500 + 7,020 + 351 = 65,871. That's correct.\n\nBut in the second method, when you shift the partial products, you have to add them in the correct place values. Wait, maybe the confusion is arising because when you shift the partial products, you have to add them in the respective place values. Let me try again.\n\nWait, the standard multiplication algorithm is:\n\nMulti