3400. That's a problem. Wait, no. Wait, in the standard multiplication, the partial products are 0, 2550, and 850. Then adding them: 0 + 2550 = 2550, then 2550 + 850 = 3400. But that's not the same as 110,500. So clearly, there's a mistake here.\n\nWait, no. Wait, hold on. Wait, in the standard multiplication, when you multiply by the tens place (1 in 130), you shift the partial product one place to the left. So 850*3 (which is the 3 in the tens place) is 2550, which is a three-digit number. Then multiplying by the hundreds place (1 in the hundreds place) gives 850*1 = 850, which is a three-digit number. Then adding them together, 2550 + 850, which is 3400. But that's only two digits. Wait, but 850*130 is a four-digit number. So clearly, something is wrong here.\n\nWait, no. Wait, 850*130. Let's compute 850*130 step by step.\n\nFirst, 850*100 = 85,000\n\nThen, 850*30 = 25,500\n\nAdding them together: 85,000 + 25,500 = 110,500\n\nBut when I do the standard multiplication, I get 3400. That's a problem. So which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the standard multiplication. Let me try again.\n\nMultiplying 850 by 130:\n\nStart with the units digit: 0. 850*0 = 0. Write that.\n\nNext digit: 3. 850*3 = 2550. Write that.\n\nNext digit: 1. 850*1 = 850. Write that.\n\nNow, add them up:\n\n0\n\n2550\n\n850\n\n------\n\n3400\n\nBut 3400 is way smaller than 110,500. So clearly, there's a mistake here. Wait, but how?\n\nWait, no. Wait, 850*130. Let me compute 850*130 using another method. Let's use the fact that 130 is 10 + 30. So 850*10 = 8500, 850*30 = 25,500. Then 8500 + 25,500 = 110,500. So that's correct. So why does the standard multiplication give 3400? That must mean I did the standard multiplication wrong.\n\nWait, no. Wait, in the standard multiplication, when you multiply by the tens place (1 in 130), you have to shift the partial product one place to the left. So 850*3 (tens place) is 2550, which is three digits. Then 850*1 (hundreds place) is 850, which is 